window.__WEBC_SEARCH__=[{"t":"What is WebC?","p":"docs/index.html","g":"Start","h":["The bet","What is in the box","How a request runs","Where to start"],"b":"WebC is a framework for web applications written in C23. Not a binding, not a wrapper around another runtime: the route table, the model layer, the template language and the browser half are all C, compiled by the same compiler, checked by the same type checker. It exists because one language all the way down turns out to be worth a lot: a field you add to a model is a column, a form input, a JSON key and a struct member at the same time, and none of those four can drift from the other three without the compiler saying so. The bet Three decisions shape everything else in the box. Memory is a scope, not a collector. The framework installs an arena per scope \u2014 the process, the request, the render \u2014 and resets it in O(1). An app says fmt() for a string it needs now and persist() for one that must outlive the request. There is no GC, no reference counting, and no hidden copy. When you read an allocation in the source, you are looking at every allocation the program makes. One set of views, two halves. src/view.c is compiled into the server binary and into a wasm module. The server renders it to HTML; the module hydrates that exact markup and takes over. There is no separate client template to keep in sync, no serialisation format to design, and no fa\u00e7ade to write: the boundary is a file listing in webc.json . Every surface is counted. webc api counts the public names and fails when the list grows without a note in the changelog. webc lint fails when a name that belongs to the framework leaks into an app, an example, a scaffold or these docs. make budget fails when the wasm or the binary grows past its number. The gates run against this repository first, so they are not advice. What is in the box Area What you get Language C23: no preprocessor tricks beyond macros, no GC, no hidden allocation Errors WErr / WErrRes with try / tryv / errdefer ; a panic becomes a 500 at the boundary Concurrency M:N green threads on kqueue, channels, select , timers, mutexes, wait groups HTTP typed router, typed request and response, middleware chain, gzip on by default, byte ranges, SSE, websockets, graceful drain; the page's head spliced on the way out, with authored chrome from partials/ and structured data as one expression Data model-first SQL over sqlite, postgres and MySQL/MariaDB \u2014 each a hand-written wire protocol, each tested against a real server; migrations with a twin per dialect, enums, documents, and a query cache keyed by generation The ORM typed filters ( WWhere ), aggregates, db_page , and relations declared once \u2014 prefetch / ref / refs read a whole page's relations in one statement per collection, db_save_all fills the foreign keys. A view never queries, and db_statement_count() proves it Redis RESP3 over parked sockets \u2014 strings, hashes, lists, sets, sorted sets, expiry, counters, pipelines, pub/sub Frontend signals, memo, effects, a DOM builder with node reuse, keyed lists, parts, component instances, live bindings, SSR and hydration, charts Browser the same views as wasm32-wasip1, js() for the seam, WebC.call / WebC.dispatch for the other direction Standard library 21 opt-in headers, from table to richtext , taken one #include at a time Tooling webc new/build/dev/serve/test/fmt/migrate/doctor/routes/size/deploy/check/lint/api/stdlib/add , and webc lsp \u2014 the language server, in the same binary, with a dependency-free VS Code client in editors/vscode/ Ops structured logs, Prometheus metrics, /debug , a CPU sampler, config from env and JSON with the model as its schema How a request runs The server accepts on a green thread. A request gets an arena; the router matches a table the compiler generated from your GET / POST macros. Middleware runs as a chain of plain functions. A handler returns a Response value \u2014 it does not write a socket. Rendering a view fills a second arena for the render. page(View, ctx) renders into public/index.html if it exists, writing data-wc anchors so the client can adopt the markup later without rebuilding it. The request's arena is reset in one instruction when the response is written. In the browser, the same view functions run against a build of the DOM tree, which the bridge diffs into operations. A render follows every action and every host callback; there is no tick to bump. Where to start Install \u2014 a compiler, the repository, and webc doctor . Quickstart \u2014 from nothing to a running app with a database in ten minutes. Mental model \u2014 the three decisions above, spelled out with code. The reference \u2014 every macro, every limit, in order. The ORM \u2014 filters, relations, pages and trees, and what each costs. The editor \u2014 webc lsp , the VS Code extension, and any other editor that speaks LSP. Agentic coding \u2014 the AGENTS.md every scaffold writes, the skill generated from this documentation, and webc mcp . Who it is for. People who like C, who want one language across the whole app, and who would rather be told by a compiler than by a code review. Who it is not for: anything that needs to run on a platform without a C toolchain, or a team that would rather not think about lifetimes at all. The framework hides the arenas; it does not pretend they are not there."},{"t":"Install","p":"docs/install.html","g":"Start","h":["What you need","Build the framework","Check the install","Optional services","The editor","Next"],"b":"Two doors, both one command. The first is the one a curious developer meets \u2014 a prebuilt CLI and framework tree, no compiler needed until the first webc dev : bash That lays out ~/.local/share/webc/<version>/ (the CLI, libwebc.a , the headers, the bridge, the skills, the docs) and symlinks ~/.local/bin/webc into it \u2014 and the CLI finds its own tree through the symlink, so nothing is baked in and the tree can move. WEBC_HOME points it at a checkout instead, any time. There is no Homebrew tap yet, and no half-filled formula pretending there is one: brew users get the same curl | sh line, and the tarball it unpacks is the one a formula would pour. The second door is the checkout \u2014 for developing on the framework itself, or when you prefer the build. WebC is a C23 project with one external library (sqlite3) and one optional toolchain (wasi-sdk, for the browser half). Nothing else is installed, vendored or fetched at build time. What you need Piece Why Test A C23 compiler nullptr , __VA_OPT__ , [[maybe_unused]] , #embed clang --version \u2014 21 or newer, which is where #embed lands and what the suite needs. gcc --version \u2014 15 or newer takes -std=c23 , but the suite is not run against it make builds the library, the CLI and the examples make --version sqlite3 the default database pkg-config --exists sqlite3 wasi-sdk only for an app with a browser half ls ~/.local/share/wasi-sdk-* node only for the bridge and check test suites node --version macOS (Apple Silicon and Intel) and Linux (x86_64 and aarch64) both build and pass make test in CI; the prebuilt artefacts are macOS today and Linux builds from the checkout. The scheduler uses kqueue on macOS and epoll on Linux, and the Linux leg is what exercises the epoll backend. Windows is not supported. Build the framework bash make install needs no sudo: it writes ~/.local/share/webc/<version>/ (binary, library, headers, assets, skills, docs, the lessons) and symlinks ~/.local/bin/webc into it \u2014 the shape the CLI resolves itself through. make install PREFIX=/usr/local for a system install, make uninstall to take it back out. make produces three things worth knowing about: build/libwebc.a \u2014 the framework, one static library; build/webc \u2014 the CLI, which is also how you build an app; build/include/ \u2014 the generated headers ( webc.json schemas, wasm glue). Put the CLI on your path: bash Check the install console doctor checks the compiler actually accepts the dialect, that the library is built, and where the optional pieces are. If wasi-sdk says not found , a server-only app still builds and runs \u2014 only --template=web and --template=fullstack need it. Optional services The defaults are deliberately boring: a file database in data/ , no cache, no queue. bash There is no separate server to install for sqlite \u2014 it is linked in. Postgres, MySQL and Redis speak their wire protocols directly, so libpq , libmysqlclient and hiredis are not dependencies either. The editor webc lsp is the language server, and it is the binary you just built \u2014 no Node, no second download. In VS Code (or Cursor, or Antigravity) run it out of the tree once: bash A whole guide covers installing it for good, Neovim and Emacs, the three settings, and living beside clangd. Next Quickstart \u2014 scaffold, run, change, test. The API boundary \u2014 the OpenAPI document and the typed client, generated. The ORM \u2014 the query API a model gives you. Project layout \u2014 what webc new wrote and why. webc learn \u2014 five lessons, one idea each; a lesson's own gate fails until the edit lands. Coming from React, Solid and Next \u2014 the dictionary, if those are the names you know. Agentic coding \u2014 if an agent will be doing the writing, start here."},{"t":"Quickstart","p":"docs/quickstart.html","g":"Start","h":["1. Scaffold","2. A model is the schema, the form and the JSON","3. The table","4. A view, and the routes that fill it","5. Watch it work","6. Prove it","7. Add a browser half","8. Keep it this way","Where to go next"],"b":"Ten minutes from nothing to a running app with a database, a page and a JSON API \u2014 and not one line of it about arenas, targets or HTTP plumbing. Every snippet below is a file you can compile: it carries its own #include , declares the model it uses, and names the file it belongs in. 1. Scaffold bash webc dev compiles what webc.json names, runs the app on :8080 , and watches the tree \u2014 without losing the page: a stylesheet edit re-fetches that one file, a browser-half edit swaps the wasm module under the page (DOM, signals, scroll and caret survive), and a build failure shows the translated error over the last good build. The scaffold already builds and passes its own tests, so the first thing you do is change something, not fix something. New here? webc learn is five lessons, one idea each \u2014 real apps whose own gates fail until the one edit lands. Three shapes, one command each: bash 2. A model is the schema, the form and the JSON This is src/app.h . It is the one header both halves include. c That declaration gives you Note_modelof() (a descriptor table), JSON in and out, form parsing, validation, and the SQL text for the notes table. Text is a const char * : a model field prints with %s , no .p / .n , no cast. .max and .min are string lengths ; numbers use .gte / .lte . The compiler says so if you mix them up. 3. The table migrations/0001_init.sql : sql bash The server applies migrations/ at boot when the files are there, so in development you rarely run this by hand. Postgres has its own spelling of each file ( 0001_init.postgres.sql ) and the tool picks the right one for the driver. 4. A view, and the routes that fill it src/view.c \u2014 markup and nothing else. The model is repeated here only so the snippet stands alone; in a real project this file starts with #include \"app.h\" . c src/server.c \u2014 routes, and the three words that answer a request: c page() renders into public/index.html when that file exists, and into a bare fragment when it does not. It writes data-wc anchors into the HTML, so the same markup can be adopted by the client later without being rebuilt. Nothing above mentions an arena. A request's strings live as long as the request; a render's strings live as long as the render. fmt() formats into the current scope and the result is safe for the rest of it. persist() is the one escape hatch, for a string that has to survive the request. 5. Watch it work bash A validation failure answers with the field that failed and why: console A database failure is not a mystery either: the driver's own words come back, with the SQLSTATE. 6. Prove it bash A test file is a list of TEST blocks, and it is a C file webc test compiles for you: c A handler is reachable without a port, so the suite runs in milliseconds. webc test prints one line per test, keeps going after a failure, and turns a panicking handler into a failed test. 7. Add a browser half An app with a client half is three file sets, and webc.json says which is which: json shared \u2014 the model and the view, compiled for both targets. It cannot reach a database or a request: <webc/shared.h> does not declare db_* or req , so a db_get in a view is a compile error rather than a code review. server \u2014 routes, database, boot. client \u2014 signals, actions, the mount. This is src/client.c as the fullstack scaffold writes it: one context, decoded from the seed the page already carries, then the same view mounted over the server's markup. c bash page() renders the view into the shell's #root , so the first paint needs no JavaScript; hydrate: true adopts those nodes and the page becomes interactive. The wasm target needs wasi-sdk \u2014 webc doctor tells you whether it is there. 8. Keep it this way bash webc lint walks src/ , tests/ and public/ and names the line, the retired call and what to write instead. webc check runs it with everything else, and in the framework repository the same command adds the examples, every scaffold it can generate, and these docs. Where to go next Routes, requests, responses \u2014 the handler surface in full. Models and migrations \u2014 relations, enums, documents, schema drift. Signals and the DOM \u2014 what re-renders, and what does not. The API boundary \u2014 the OpenAPI document and the typed fetch client, generated from what your routes and models already declare. Coming from React, Solid and Next \u2014 the dictionary, if those are the names you know. Agentic coding \u2014 the AGENTS.md in your new app, the skill, and webc mcp : what to give an agent so it writes WebC instead of C that looks like it. The reference \u2014 every macro and every limit, starting at \u00a71 House rules ."},{"t":"Project layout","p":"docs/layout.html","g":"Start","h":["The three lists are the boundary","The boot block","What .webc/ is","Files you may not need"],"b":"webc new writes the same shape every time, and the shape is the architecture: three files that compile for the server, for the browser, or for both, and a webc.json that says which is which. bash text webc.json for that project: json The three lists are the boundary List Compiles into Can see Cannot see shared both binaries models, views, signals, pure helpers db_* , req , res_* , sockets server the server binary everything, plus the database and the request \u2014 client the wasm module the shared surface, DOM, js() , fetch the database, the request A function that reaches across the line is a compile error , not a review comment: <webc/shared.h> does not declare db_* or req . webc lint runs the wrong-half pass on top of that, because a wrong-half call is the mistake a #include makes easy. The boot block boot is the page shell, and it is why a fullstack app has no HTML file: title , lang , css \u2014 what the generated <head> carries; el \u2014 the selector page() renders into and the client mounts over; data \u2014 the URL the module fetches for its first document ( wc_app_data receives it); hydrate \u2014 whether the client adopts the server's nodes or rebuilds them. c declare_view(Notes, VecNote) is the one line that makes the name Notes mean the same function in src/view.c (where view(Notes, \u2026) defines it) and in src/server.c (where page() calls it) \u2014 handlers and views live in different files, and this is the header that ties them. partials/ is where authored HTML lives \u2014 favicons, theme colours, a banner, a whole maintenance page \u2014 included by name ( partial(\"site/head\") ), read once, cached, and verified by webc check . A fragment that needs a value is a view, not a partial. page() renders the view into the shell (or into public/index.html if you wrote one), and marks the nodes it drew with data-wc anchors. hydrate: true makes the client adopt those exact nodes \u2014 zero DOM creation on the first render. What .webc/ is webc build writes into .webc/ , which is generated and ignored: text Nothing in .webc/ is ever edited. webc dev rebuilds it on save; webc serve builds a release into it once and serves that. Files you may not need public/index.html \u2014 write one when you want control of the shell; otherwise boot generates it. It is also where page() writes the render when it exists. src/app.h \u2014 a convention, not a requirement: it is where the model and the declare_view line live so both halves include one header instead of two. migrations/ \u2014 optional for a toy, mandatory the moment you have data you care about."},{"t":"Mental model","p":"docs/mental-model.html","g":"Start","h":["1. A string belongs to a scope","2. A response is a value","3. A render follows an action","4. State has a lifetime, and the lifetime has a name","5. The halves are separated by a file listing","What follows from these"],"b":"Five ideas explain almost every API in WebC. They are not abstract: each one is the reason a particular function has the shape it has, and each one is something the framework will not let you forget. 1. A string belongs to a scope The framework installs an arena per scope \u2014 the process, the request, the render \u2014 and resets it in one instruction when the scope ends. An app never allocates one and never resets one; it only decides which scope a value must live in. c fmt() is right for a value you are about to render, validate or send. persist() is right for a cached string, a model in a global, or anything a second request will read. Using persist() for everything is a leak; using fmt() for something that outlives the scope is a bug that will show up as mojibake under load \u2014 which is why the framework's own review rule is \"if it is stored, name the scope it belongs to\". The rule of thumb. If you can point at the line that reads the value and say which function it returns from, fmt() is enough. If it goes into a struct that outlives the function, persist() . 2. A response is a value A handler does not write to a socket; it returns a Response . That is what makes a handler testable without a port, composable in middleware, and cheap to call from a test. c Errors travel the same way. try and tryv propagate a WErr , errdefer cleans up on the way out, and a panic that reaches the boundary is a 500 with the message in the log \u2014 not a dead server. 3. A render follows an action In the browser half there is no tick signal and no redraw() call. The bridge re-renders each mounted view when control returns to it: after a click, after a typed field, after a webc_later timer, after a fetch settles. Writing a signal does not schedule anything by itself \u2014 the flush point is the bridge boundary. c The exception is a live binding : text_live , attr_live , class_live , show_live write one node in place, and an action marked action_live skips the render entirely. That is for a pointer moving or a keystroke \u2014 not for anything structural. 4. State has a lifetime, and the lifetime has a name Declaration One value per Use it for state(T, x, init) page a value the whole page reads state_in(T, x, init) instance you own per-record state you hold state_own(T, x, init) render position a row, a mounted view state_own + comp(Name, \u2026) call site one copy per component call Choosing wrong is a real bug, not a style question: a state_own read from a page-wide timer panics, and a page-wide state written by one row is a value every row then shows. The reference has the long version in \u00a711c . 5. The halves are separated by a file listing shared code compiles into both binaries and can reach neither the database nor the request. That is the whole trick: the compiler enforces the client/server boundary, so a view cannot accidentally do I/O, and a migration cannot accidentally depend on a render. json The same view function is the server's HTML and the browser's tree. There is no second template, no serialisation schema, and no client-side model to keep in sync \u2014 which is most of the code you would otherwise write. What follows from these Nothing is hidden. Every allocation is a fmt() , a persist() , or a value on the stack. A handler is a function. It can be called from a test with a fabricated request. A view is a function. It can be compiled for two very different targets, or rendered to a string in a test. A UI state is a declaration. webc check reads the declarations and the reads, and names the ones that disagree \u2014 a signal nobody reads, a declaration read outside its position, a part called twice that thought it had two states."},{"t":"Routes, requests, responses","p":"guides/http.html","g":"Guides","h":["The route macros","Reading the request","Answering it","The page's head","Bodies","Errors","Middleware","What you get for free","See also"],"b":"A handler is a function that returns a Response . It does not write to a socket, which is why it can be called from a test without opening a port, wrapped by middleware, or run twice to see what it does. Routes are declarations at file scope, so the router is a table the compiler builds. Every snippet on this page is a whole file: it includes the header it needs and declares the models it uses, so you can paste it into an empty src/server.c and compile. The route macros c GET , POST , PUT , PATCH , DELETE , HEAD , OPTIONS , ANY , each with an _MW variant that attaches per-route middleware. Matching order is static segment, then :param , then *wildcard ; HEAD falls back to GET ; a trailing slash is not a different route; a known path with the wrong method is 405, not 404. webc routes prints the table the compiler built \u2014 method, pattern, handler, and which middleware runs where. It is the fastest way to answer \"why is my 404 not a 405\". Reading the request Call Type Note path(\"name\") Text a path parameter; the string type an app names query_text(\"name\") Text absent and empty are different; both are \"\" here path_i64(\"id\") , query_i64(\"amount\", 0) i64 parsed, or the default req_query_bool(\"all\", false) bool query(\"name\") str the raw bytes, for a value that may be binary header(\"accept\") , cookie(\"session\") str req->body , req->peer , req->request_id , req->arena the rest of the request Answering it One spelling per kind of answer: Call Sends json(status, Model, ptr) a model or a Vec as JSON json(status, text) a body you already built text(status, s) / html(status, s) text/plain / text/html page(View, ctx) / page_with(status, View, ctx) a view inside the page shell fail(status, msg, \u2026) / res_error(status, code, msg) an error body in the client's shape redirect(url) 303 with a Location res_file , res_download , res_bytes , res_stream , res_sse , res_upgrade the rest res_cookie , res_header(_str) a header on the response A mutation route usually ends with the line that serves both callers \u2014 a form post gets its redirect back to the page, a JSON call gets the row it changed: c The page's head The shell owns the document; the route owns the page's facts about itself. head_title() , head_meta() , head_prop() and head_link() splice into <head> on the way out \u2014 escaped once, before anything compresses \u2014 and two doors cover what a string of calls cannot: c head_ld() is not sugar for a <script> tag: that body is JSON and markup, and a title containing </script> would end the element if the escaping were the caller's job \u2014 webc lint names the hand-built spelling. A field you leave out is omitted, never written \"\" ( \"datePublished\":\"\" is not a date), and ASSERT_LD(r, \"author.name\", \u2026) is how a test holds the block to the page. partial(\"site/head\") is the same rule for chrome: bytes that never change per page belong in partials/site/head.html , not in a C string of escaped quotes; webc check verifies every name has its file. Bodies body(Model) reads whichever body the request carries \u2014 JSON or a form \u2014 validates it with the model's own rules, and answers the request itself on a failure. When you want to decide what happens next, the two spellings are explicit and end the handler early: c Both produce the same validation messages the browser would, because they run the same code over the same model descriptor. Errors A handler may return Response or raise: fail(status, fmt, \u2026) is a router-level error, and try / tryv propagate a WErr with errdefer cleaning up on the way out. A panic that reaches the boundary is a 500 and a log line \u2014 the connection is not lost and the process is not dead. c Middleware A middleware is a plain function \u2014 bool (*)(Request *, void *, WNext *) \u2014 and false stops the chain. The framework's own are not special; yours sits in the same list: c Built in: mw_logging , mw_request_id , mw_headers(HeadersCfg) / mw_headers_default() , mw_cors(&(CorsCfg){\u2026}) , mw_body_limit(bytes) , mw_timeout_ms(ms) , mw_auth_bearer(token) , mw_recover , mw_static(prefix, dir) , mw_static_spa(prefix, dir) , mw_metrics , mw_dev(port) , mw_web_assets(wasm_path) , mw_compress() , mw_document() . Each is a function you call; webc_use takes the value it returns. You rarely register all of them. webc_main() calls webc_install_default_middleware() after webc_boot() , so gzip, the request id, the CSP, the logging, the panic recovery, the body limit, /webc.js + /app.wasm , /public and /assets , the document pass and the 404 page are already in the chain \u2014 and because the app's own middlewares are registered first , they wrap all of it. webc dev starts the app with the dev reload middleware in the same place. What you register replaces the framework's, it does not stack beside it. A default whose name is already in the chain is not installed, so the mw_headers() above is the policy \u2014 the one from webc.json 's \"csp\" block is the fallback for a project that registers none. The same holds for mw_logging() , mw_recover() and mw_document() : one of each, the app's. Order matters twice, and both are stated in the reference ( \u00a78b ): gzip goes before the middlewares that answer, so it wraps their bodies; and mw_document() goes last , because it splices the page's <head> into the body and a splice after compression searches gzip bytes for </head> and finds nothing \u2014 which is how a page kept its generic title in every browser while curl looked fine. mw_recover is what turns a panic into a 500 rather than a lost connection; mw_timeout_ms cancels the request's context, so a query in flight is cancelled at the server rather than abandoned. What you get for free gzip \u2014 on by default when the client accepts it, with a small-body threshold and a cache of compressed responses keyed by size and etag. A real 404 \u2014 \"not_found\": \"public/404.html\" in webc.json answers a browser with that page inside your shell (title, theme, nonce), and leaves a JSON client its JSON error. A 500 that names the request \u2014 internal error \u2014 GET /a/x (route /a/:slug, id \u2026) in the body, the log line and the crash report, so a report is a route away from being fixed. Byte ranges \u2014 res_file answers 206 for a media element, so a <video> seeks. SSE and websockets \u2014 res_sse and res_upgrade , in-process; see Real-time . Metrics and /metrics \u2014 request counts, latency histograms, and the pool and scheduler gauges, without an exporter. See also Reference \u00a77 HTTP and \u00a78 Middleware . Models for what body() validates against. Testing for calling a handler without a port."},{"t":"Models and migrations","p":"guides/models.html","g":"Guides","h":["Declaring a model","The qualifiers","A column that can be absent","A closed set is a type, not a string","Migrations","The routes every table gets","Queries","Transactions","Documents","A row you computed","See also"],"b":"A model is the only place a piece of data is declared. From one declaration the framework generates the struct, a descriptor table, the SQL text for the table, the JSON codec, the form parser, the validation rules and the labels a form renders. Those six cannot drift apart, because there is only one of them. Declaring a model c That gives you Todo_modelof() (the descriptor), the table name todos (snake_case plural \u2014 override with WEBC_MODEL_TABLE(Todo, \"todos2\") ), and every one of: c Vec(Todo) declares VecTodo carrying its element type , so json(200, todos) needs no type name and for_each(t, todos) knows its key. The qualifiers All designated initialisers, so the compiler checks them \u2014 and a bare flag ( .required with no = true ) is not valid C, so it is a compile error rather than a silently ignored option. Qualifier Meaning .pk = true primary key; an integer pk is assigned by the database .required = true must be present and non-empty .not_null = true the same, spelled for rows rather than bodies .unique = true declared in generated SQL; enforced by the database .max = N / .min = N string length bounds .gte = X / .lte = X numeric bounds .format = \"email\" email , uuid , url , date .trim = true strip surrounding whitespace before validation .sensitive = true redacted by the logger .no_json = true never serialised .json_array = true a vector field serialises as an array of scalars .sql = \"column\" column name override .def = \"...\" SQL default expression .doc = \"...\" surfaced by webc doctor .null = true an integer column the row may not have; 0 is the NULL this side reads .references = \"table(col)\" the FK target the generated DDL carries .max / .min are lengths and .gte / .lte are numbers; mixing them up is a compile error, not a runtime surprise. A column that can be absent A foreign key nobody has chosen yet is the ordinary case, and the failure it produces is not: a plain i64 saved as 0 reaches the database as a value, the foreign key refuses it, and the message is FOREIGN KEY constraint failed on a POST . Declared nullable, the same save is a NULL : c c One qualifier carries through all four directions: the DDL has no not null and carries the references clause, db_save binds SQL NULL for 0 , a read turns NULL back into 0 , the JSON codec writes json: null and accepts null for this field only, and an empty form input means \"nothing chosen\". .null is for integer kinds alone \u2014 a float's 0 is a value and an enum's 0 is its first member \u2014 and .null with .required or .pk is a contradiction the compiler refuses. A closed set is a type, not a string c The field is an int in the struct and its name everywhere bytes are exchanged \u2014 JSON, forms, SQL text. A request that sends a stranger is a 422 naming the field, rather than a row that renders nowhere. A legacy integer column still reads, as its index. Migrations Migrations are SQL files in migrations/ , applied in filename order and recorded in schema_migrations . The dialect belongs to the database, so postgres gets its own spelling beside the sqlite one: text The tool picks the file for the driver and records the same name, so one sequence serves both. bash The server applies pending migrations at boot. webc check compares the models against the migrated schema and names a column that has drifted, which is the failure that otherwise shows up as a 500 in production. webc migrate diff is that comparison as statements: an alter table \u2026 add column , a create table , the references a .null field declares, the default a not null needs. It is additive on purpose \u2014 it never drops or narrows, because sqlite cannot drop a constraint without copying the table and a tool that guesses at a data migration loses rows \u2014 and what it cannot infer is a -- TODO line a person answers before the file is applied. The routes every table gets webc scaffold Post writes src/admin_post.c : the list (a WPager window and a ww_like search over the first bounded text field), new, edit, save and delete, plus tests/admin_post_test.c with one test per route. It reads the model from the app's own descriptor, so the columns and bounds are the ones you declared, and it never edits the model: the file is yours to change, webc check passes on it, and nothing that exists is overwritten without --force . Queries The typed filters are the way to read: db_by for one value, WWhere for conditions, relations declared once and read with ref() , and db_page for a list \u2014 The ORM is the whole guide. What follows is the raw layer those sit on, which is what you want for the report-shaped statement that is not worth a verb. c webc_config 's default connection is the one-argument forms above use; every function also takes an explicit WDB * . Transactions A transaction belongs to a session, and a session belongs to a request: the first statement a request makes pins a connection for that task, and every later statement runs on it. Two requests can never sit inside each other's BEGIN / COMMIT . c A nested scope is a SAVEPOINT , so an inner failure can roll back alone. A task that ends inside a transaction has it rolled back and the connection returned, with a warning naming the task. Documents A model is a row. A document is the shape a live page sends: several collections and scalars in one JSON object, one declaration for both directions. c On the client a document arrives through a WStore (see Real-time ), and wc_doc_join() links its relations in memory. A row you computed A dashboard tile is a row the database does not have \u2014 a count, a breakdown, a KPI. WEBC_ROW declares it exactly like a model and gives the same four things (struct, descriptor, Vec(T) , JSON), minus the table: c Declared as a WEBC_MODEL instead, webc check reports schema drift on every run for a table that was never meant to exist; db_save on a row panics with a sentence saying so rather than sending an insert at a table nobody created. See also Reference \u00a74 Models and \u00a79 Database . The ORM \u2014 filters, relations, pages and trees. MySQL and MariaDB \u2014 the same migrations on the third server. Forms \u2014 the same declaration drawn as inputs."},{"t":"The ORM: filters, relations, writes","p":"guides/orm.html","g":"Guides","h":["One field, one value","A filter with more than one condition","One question over several columns","Aggregates","Relations: declared once, read with no query","Questions across a relation","A page of a list","A tree of rows","What it does not do","Count the queries","See also"],"b":"A model is a row; the ORM is how you ask for rows without writing SQL. There is no unit of work, no lazy proxy and no second query language: a filter names the model's fields , so the compiler checks it, a relation is declared once next to the fields it points at, and a page or a whole tree of rows is one call each. That is the whole design. Everything below is the same idea at four sizes: one value, a condition, a relation, a page. One field, one value c The field name is checked against the model's descriptor, and the value is always a bound parameter: the clause is built from the field, never from a string you typed. db_by(db, Todo, \"id\", 3) is the same call on a handle you own (a test, a second database). A string literal goes through str_lit() , because _Generic does not decay an array \u2014 the reference \u00a79a has the long version of why. A filter with more than one condition WWhere builds the clause and its bound values from the descriptor. Nothing is pasted, so a value containing a quote is a value containing a quote. c call clause it writes ww_eq_i64 / ww_ne_i64 = ? / <> ? ww_gt_i64 / ww_gte_i64 / ww_lt_i64 / ww_lte_i64 > ? / >= ? / < ? / <= ? ww_eq_bool(&w, Todo, done, false) a bool, spelled the driver's way ww_eq_str(&w, Todo, title, s) string equality ww_like(&w, Todo, title, pattern) like \u2014 the pattern is yours, wildcards included ww_contains(&w, Todo, title, q) lower(col) like lower(?) escape '!' , escaped for you ww_order(&w, Todo, created, true) order by \u2026 desc ( true ) or asc ( false ) ww_limit(&w, 20, 40) limit 20 offset 40 Two of those are worth a sentence more: ww_limit belongs to the query, not to your loop. Reading forty rows to keep two is the shape this removes, and an offset with no limit (illegal in sqlite and MySQL) is repaired from the driver's declared capability instead of in every app. ww_contains owns the escaping. % , _ and ! in the user's words are escaped and the clause says escape '!' \u2014 not a backslash, which is an escape character inside a string literal on MySQL and makes escape '\\' a syntax error there. The field name is a compile-time check: ww_eq_i64(&w, Todo, onwer, 1) does not compile. A field that is not a column (a .sql() override aside) is the same error. One question over several columns A search box is one question about three columns, and answering it by filtering a whole table in memory is a page that works on your laptop: c Aggregates The same filter answers a number instead of rows \u2014 db_sum , db_min , db_max , db_avg : c order by and limit are stripped before the aggregate: a sum of the first page is a different question, and it is the one whose wrong answer looks plausible. No rows, or every row NULL , is 0 \u2014 the sum of nothing is nothing, not an error. One column, plus one, in one statement. db_increment(Todo, t, views) is update \u2026 set views = views + 1 , which is not the same thing as t->views += 1; db_save(Todo, t); \u2014 that rewrites the whole row (a str body and all) to change a counter, and loses a concurrent increment. Relations: declared once, read with no query A relation is a declaration , not a lazy proxy. Two lines go next to the models, in the header both halves include: c WEBC_MODEL_REF is pure data: it registers \"this field points at that key\" and nothing else. It costs one constructor call at startup and no memory per row, and it is what prefetch reads \u2014 which is why the column name appears exactly once in the app. Then the handler runs one query for the whole page and the view reads the links: c c One statement per collection, not per row. 2 000 sections are 2 000 owner keys, chunked into groups of 512, so the parameter count never meets a server's ceiling. ref() never fetches. A relation nobody prefetched reads as null ; a collection nobody prefetched reads as empty and logs relation read without a prefetch once, naming the model and the field \u2014 because an empty list that was never fetched is a page that lost its rows in silence. Hops chain left to right. prefetch(all, Section, stories, author) is two statements: the stories of every section on the page, then the authors those stories named. Three hops is the limit; deeper is a loop in the app, because a path string is a second query language nobody asked for. The links belong to the request. The server clears the link table at every request boundary, and per test \u2014 so a view can never read last request's rows, including the case the pool makes easy to get wrong, where the request arena comes back at the same address. prefetch_on(db, all, Section, stories) is the same on a handle you own. In the browser half there is no database, so a document's rows are joined with wc_doc_join() after they decode. Questions across a relation A filter can reach through a relation without changing the shape of the result \u2014 which matters, because the ORM reads rows back into models and a join changes the row shape: c ww_not_in_ref is the same clause negated. ww_order_ref is a correlated subquery, so it costs one index lookup per row on the referenced key \u2014 fine for a page of rows, and the honest alternative for a whole table is an indexed column on the row itself. A page of a list c Two statements on purpose: the count is what tells the pager how many pages there are, and a window cannot answer it. page is clamped into 1..pages , so an out-of-range page number is the same list rather than an empty render or a 404. Between the two statements a concurrent insert can shift every row by one; the framework does not open a transaction to hide it, because doubling the cost of every list render to hide a race nobody has hit is the wrong trade. A tree of rows Rows that belong to a row are written as one thing, and the foreign key comes from the MANY declaration rather than from your fingers: c db_save_all takes the collection , not a field name: matching the vector's own descriptor against the declaration is what makes a typo impossible, and a collection that is not declared on the model is a panic naming the declaration to add \u2014 not a write into the wrong column. db_delete_tree is the same in the other direction, children first, because an app's migration may not have on delete cascade . What it does not do No group by . \"Per kind, how many and how much\" is a filtered read plus a sum in C, or an aggregate per group in the app. Adding the verb is the fix; a hand-written join in one handler is not. No lazy loading. Nothing fetches on read. A view that needs rows gets them from a handler that prefetched them, which is the property that keeps a page's query count fixed. No unit of work. A save is a statement; a tree of saves is db_save_all inside tx_scope ( Models and migrations ). No dialect in your code. Identifiers, offset , returning and multi-statement files are declared per driver ( MySQL and MariaDB ), so the same filter runs on sqlite, postgres and MySQL. Count the queries The ORM's promise is a constant number of statements per page, and a stopwatch cannot check that. db_statement_count() can, and WEBC_TRACE_DB=1 prints the same number as one line per statement: c That test is where an N+1 gets caught: prefetch is one statement for the list and one per hop, so a page that grows a query per row fails a test instead of becoming a slow page nobody can reproduce. A typical seven-page app holds /table at three statements and /books at five, measured at three rows and again at fifteen. See also Reference \u00a79 Database \u2014 \u00a79a to \u00a79a4 are this guide in reference voice. Models and migrations \u2014 the declaration this rests on. MySQL and MariaDB \u2014 the third driver, and the four capabilities it declares. Reference \u00a79 Database \u2014 relations as declarations, the driver capability table, and how a driver classifies its own errors."},{"t":"MySQL and MariaDB","p":"guides/mysql.html","g":"Guides","h":["One URL, three drivers","Users and auth plugins","TLS","What the connection sets up","How a statement travels","What a dialect declares","Errors are classified, not searched for","Migrations","Running the tests","Timeouts, and a query that has already lost","What is not covered","See also"],"b":"MySQL and MariaDB are the third database WebC speaks, and the reason the SQL above the driver never branches on dialect: everything that actually differs between servers is declared by the driver and read by the framework \u2014 a third driver is a capability table, not an abstraction over SQL. The driver is src/db_mysql.c : the wire protocol, by hand. No libmysqlclient, no .so to install, no version to match \u2014 the same reason there is no libpq and no hiredis. One URL, three drivers bash mysql:// and mariadb:// are one driver (the protocol is the same; the two servers differ in a few error codes and in the name of one timeout variable, and the driver asks which server answered at handshake time). Anything without :// is still a sqlite path, and a scheme nobody implements is refused at open , naming the URLs this build speaks. Opened as a sqlite file named after the URL, postgre://x shows its first sign as \"my tables are empty\" three screens later. c db_driver_name(db) answers \"mysql\" (or \"mariadb\" , \"postgres\" , \"sqlite\" ). The framework keeps those names as data , not as two string comparisons, and that list is what the migration runner reads when it looks for a <name>.mysql.sql twin \u2014 so a fourth dialect would be a line there and a file next to the other two. Users and auth plugins Which plugin your user has decides what the connection can do, and this is the part that surprises people: plugin this driver needs mysql_native_password SHA-1 scramble, always available nothing caching_sha2_password the SHA-256 fast path after the first login the full path needs TLS sha256_password supported, TLS only TLS ed25519 , GSSAPI refused by name , with the two alternatives in the message \u2014 caching_sha2_password is the default for a fresh MySQL 8 user, and its fast path only works once the server has cached the login \u2014 on a cold cache it asks for the password over a secure channel. With TLS, the driver answers; without TLS it refuses and says so, rather than sending a password in the clear or failing as \"access denied\" when the password was right. There is no RSA public-key exchange, on purpose. A client that will hand its password encrypted with whatever key the server offers is a client that gives the password to whoever is in the middle; ?ssl-mode=require is the answer, and it is one word in the URL. The cost is honest: sha256_password and a cold caching_sha2_password need TLS, and the error says exactly that. That is why make mysql-test starts its container with --mysql-native-password=ON and a non-root webc user: a build without TLS can only speak the first row of that table, while MySQL 8.4 loads that plugin only when asked. With TLS ( make WEBC_TLS=openssl build/webc ) the stock root account works as it is. TLS bash A build without TLS refuses require , verify-ca and verify-full at open , with a message that names the build flag \u2014 an app that has quietly been running in plaintext is worse than one that failed to start. prefer is the one that bends: a server that offers no TLS logs one warning and continues in plaintext, and a failed handshake reconnects in plaintext with the reason in the log, because a half-written handshake cannot be continued in the clear and the alternative is a confusing \"malformed packet\" three statements later. What the connection sets up Every connection, before your first statement: sql Strict mode is not decoration. Without it MySQL silently truncates an over-long string, which would make the WDBE_TOO_LONG class (a 422) unreachable and let a 250-character title into a varchar(200) column. With it, the server refuses and the request says which field, which is the behaviour sqlite and postgres already had. time_zone='+00:00' is what makes a datetime mean the same instant everywhere: one server in the app's local zone would otherwise shift every row it wrote. How a statement travels Two paths, chosen by whether the statement has parameters: No parameters \u2192 COM_QUERY . This is what lets a whole migrations/0001_init.mysql.sql run in one call, and if it carries several result sets the last one is what you get back (the rule all three drivers follow). Parameters \u2192 COM_STMT_PREPARE / COM_STMT_EXECUTE , binary rows, cached per connection: 64 statements, LRU, keyed on the SQL text (statements longer than a struct-sized key are prepared each time rather than kept). A prepared statement is a small win, but it is also the reason a :name placeholder can never be spliced into SQL \u2014 the value goes on a different wire than the text. Rows are decoded from the column definition in both paths: tinyint(1) is a bool (that length is how MySQL spells one, and the model already knows the field is a bool), DECIMAL is read as an i64 or an f64 by its digits, and a FLOAT / DOUBLE column is always a real even when its value looks integral \u2014 sum(1.5 + 2.5 + 4.0) is 8.0 , not 8 . FOUND_ROWS is deliberately not set: affected means \"changed\", which is what a job queue and a db_increment want to know. A count is a db_count , not an affected-rows reading of a select . What a dialect declares Four flags, three drivers. This table is the whole of the dialect branching in the framework: sqlite postgres mysql returning \u2014 a new key comes back with the insert no ( last_insert_rowid() ) yes ( insert \u2026 returning ) no (the OK packet's last_insert_id ) offset_needs_limit \u2014 offset 5 alone is legal no yes no ident_quote \u2014 what quotes a column \" \" ` multi_statement \u2014 one call may carry several yes yes yes Read as behaviour: db_insert reports the new key on all three without the app looking at which one it is; a ww_limit(&w, 0, 40) (offset, no limit) becomes limit 9223372036854775807 offset 40 , which is the largest signed 64-bit value because MySQL documents the unsigned one and sqlite answers datatype mismatch to it; and a model field called rank , groups or order is quoted on the way out, so a reserved word is a column name rather than a syntax error. A name that is not a plain identifier ( public.users , a .sql() override) is passed through untouched, because that is the caller's own SQL. Two more shapes are worth knowing because they are the same value arriving differently: An aggregate is DECIMAL here. sum(int) arrives as DECIMAL(32,0) and avg(int) as DECIMAL(x,4) . The driver's text decoder reads both, so db_sum returns an i64 and db_avg an f64 , exactly as on the other two servers. A like pattern is escaped with ! . A backslash is an escape character *inside a string literal* on MySQL, so escape '\\' is a syntax error there \u2014 every search filter in every app would have been a 500 on the third driver. ww_contains writes escape '!' , and no dialect gives ! a second meaning. Errors are classified, not searched for Each driver maps its own codes into one class, and the class picks the status. Nothing looks for the word \"unique\" in an error message \u2014 that holds on sqlite and postgres and fails on MySQL. class MySQL errno what the request answers WDBE_CONFLICT 1062, 1586, 1451 409 WDBE_BAD_REFERENCE 1452, 1216, 1217 400 WDBE_NOT_NULL 1048, 1364 422 WDBE_TOO_LONG 1406, 1264, 1292 422 WDBE_CHECK 3819, 4025 422 WDBE_DEADLOCK 1213 503 WDBE_TIMEOUT 1205, 3024 503 WDBE_AUTH 1045, 1698 500 WDBE_LOST 2006, 2013, 1053 500, and the connection is not reused An error the driver cannot classify stays WDBE_NONE and answers 500 with the server's own sentence \u2014 deliberately, because a guess at 409 is worse than a 500 that says what happened. Migrations The migration runner puts the driver's name into the file name: <name>.mysql.sql is used instead of <name>.sql when it exists, and the same logical name is recorded in schema_migrations , so one sequence of migrations serves the three servers. bash Type the columns the way the field is stored , which is the mistake that costs an afternoon: field column on MySQL why (id, i64, .pk = true) bigint auto_increment primary key the identity column is spelled by the server an enum ( RKind , Lane ) varchar(40) \u2014 never int the framework stores an enum by name bool tinyint(1) which is also how the driver reads it back str indexed or unique varchar(190) text cannot be indexed without a prefix length str , unindexed text money integer (cents) never a float SQLite accepts a name in an integer column and will not tell you, so a migration can be wrong for months and only fail the day someone runs it against postgres or MySQL ( invalid input syntax for type integer: \"balanced\" ). webc check compares the models against the migrated schema and names a column that has drifted. Running the tests An app's own suite runs on MySQL by pointing it at the server: bash make mysql-test is the framework's version of that, for this repository: it starts MySQL 8 in docker (port 33406, --mysql-native-password=ON ), creates a non-root webc user, runs the cross-driver parity test, the driver's own suite, the ORM suites and both example apps, and removes the container. One target, one line at the end: mysql: all green , or the first suite that was not. Use WEBC_MY"},{"t":"Forms, with and without JS","p":"guides/forms.html","g":"Guides","h":["The form that works","The form drawn from the model","Validation, three ways","Enhancing it","Uploads","Accessibility is part of the form","See also"],"b":"A form in WebC is a form first: it submits, it validates on the server, it redirects or re-renders. The browser half then makes the same form faster without changing what it does \u2014 which is why an app that never loads its module is still a working app. Each snippet here is a file you can compile on its own. The form that works c c body(Note) reads application/x-www-form-urlencoded or JSON, decides by the content type, and validates with the rules the model declared. A failure answers 422 with the field that failed: console The form drawn from the model Writing the inputs by hand is fine for two fields. For a model you already declared, form.h draws them \u2014 the kind, the bounds, the label and the error all come from one declaration: c f->prefix = \"edit\" gives a second form on the same page its own id space \u2014 the thing a hand-written field set gets wrong at the second form. form_field_long(f, \"bio\", 6) is the one place a textarea is chosen, because rows is a decision about the page, not about the model. Adding a field to the struct adds a field to the form, with its label and its rules. The handler that renders it builds the form around the draft it is validating: c Validation, three ways The same rules run in three places, from model_validate() : Where How the user learns the form post a 422 and a re-render with form_errors() the JSON API a 422 body naming field and message the browser half the same fields, checked before the request goes out None of them is the only check. A server never trusts the client's copy of the rules, and a client never sends a request it already knows will fail. Enhancing it A form needs nothing to become interactive. The client half mounts the same view and the same post_to \u2014 the submit becomes a fetch and a re-render, and the no-JS path stays as a fallback for the first paint and for anything that breaks. This is a whole client-only app in one file: the view, the signal it binds, the action that reads it, and the mount. c bind_str is the whole wiring: the input writes the signal on every keystroke, and a live binding reading it writes the node back. There is no second line in client_main to forget. Uploads upload.h is the file case, and it does the four things a hand-rolled upload forgets: it checks the magic bytes rather than the extension, it makes up the name on the server side (so a client cannot choose a path), it enforces a size before reading the whole body, and it answers with a URL that is inside the app's own root. c Three arguments and one struct: up.why is a sentence for the person who uploaded it, not a code, because it is going next to the field they just filled in. A body that is not a multipart/form-data upload says so \u2014 \"a <form> with enctype set sends one\" \u2014 which is the mistake that actually happens. Accessibility is part of the form form_fields() emits a <label> bound by for , an aria-describedby pointing at the error, and the inputmode and autocomplete the field's kind implies. form_errors() puts each message in a live region, so a screen reader hears what changed. overlay.h and keys.h follow the same rule: the ARIA is in the component, not in each app. See also Reference \u00a75 JSON and forms , and the form module in \u00a712 The shelf . Models \u2014 the declaration these inputs come from."},{"t":"Auth: passwords and sessions","p":"guides/auth.html","g":"Guides","h":["The pieces","A password is a hash, and the hash is a string","Signing up, and signing in","The guard is one call","The cookie, exactly","The secret","Testing it","What it is not","Related"],"b":"Accounts need three things and this framework has exactly three: a password hash, a signed cookie, and a way to read the cookie back. There is no session table, no user model, no library to install \u2014 <webc/auth.h> is one header with nine functions, and examples/accounts is a working app built on it. Every snippet here is a whole file: it declares the model it uses and includes the header it needs. The pieces Call Signature What it is for auth_hash(password) const char * \u2192 Text hash a password on signup; allocates in the current scope, like fmt() auth_verify(stored, password) const char * , const char * \u2192 bool check a password on login; constant-time compare auth_hash_password(a, password) Arena* , str \u2192 str the same hash when the value is already a str auth_hash_password_iters(a, password, n) \u2026, u32 \u2192 str the same, with your iteration count instead of 210 000 auth_set_session(&res, secret, uid) Response* , str , i64 sign a session cookie onto a response auth_clear_session(&res) Response* expire it on logout auth_get_session(req, secret, &uid) Request* , str , i64* \u2192 bool read and verify the cookie auth_require_session(req, secret) Request* , str \u2192 i64 the same, 0 when absent, invalid or expired auth_secret() , auth_secret_set(s) \u2192 str the signing secret, resolved once per process Two constants: WEBC_SESSION_COOKIE is \"session\" , WEBC_SESSION_TTL_S is one week. The two passwords flavours are not redundancy. A model field is a Text ( const char * ), and so is every form value, so the Text pair is what an app calls; the str pair is for code that already holds a length and a pointer. A password is a hash, and the hash is a string c auth_hash returns pbkdf2-sha256$210000$<salt-b64>$<hash-b64> \u2014 the iteration count, the salt and the hash in one string, so the stored value is self-describing and the cost can be raised later without a migration: auth_verify reads the iteration count out of the stored value and re-derives with it. Per-password salt, PBKDF2-SHA256, and a comparison that does not return early on the first differing byte. What matters for an app is that the hash is the string in the row, and the password never is. auth_hash allocates in the current scope, which in a handler is the request's arena \u2014 the value is valid until the response is written, which is exactly as long as it is needed, and db_save has copied it by then. Signing up, and signing in c Both POSTs redirect, so the page after a signup is a GET and a refresh does not post twice. The guard is one call c The second check is not paranoia: the cookie is signed, so it cannot be forged, but it also cannot know that the row was deleted. A row that is gone is a redirect, not a 500. Where a handler must answer itself \u2014 an API route that returns 401 JSON rather than a redirect \u2014 auth_get_session(&uid) gives the boolean and the id separately. The cookie, exactly auth_set_session writes <uid>.<expiry>.<hmac> into a cookie named session , with HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax , valid for WEBC_SESSION_TTL_S (604800 seconds). Signed, never encrypted: the browser and the user can read the id and the expiry, and cannot change either without invalidating the HMAC. That is why there is no session table \u2014 the server trusts the signature, and a revoked session is a deleted account rather than a row that has to be found. SameSite=Lax is the cross-site request story for a form-driven app: a POST from another origin does not carry the cookie. If you need a session cookie on a cross-site POST, use a token, and a token is application code. The secret auth_secret() resolves once per process, in this order: auth_secret_set(value) \u2014 what a test calls, so the cookies a test mints are the cookies it verifies; WEBC_SECRET , the environment variable, which is what a deployment sets; data/.webc-secret \u2014 a file the dev loop writes on first boot, so a restart does not log everybody out (getting this wrong costs an afternoon: every reload looks like broken auth); a random secret for this process, **with a log line that says sessions will not survive a restart**. In production, set WEBC_SECRET and treat it like the database password: changing it invalidates every session, and losing it is not recoverable. Testing it A test mints a cookie and sends it back, with no socket in between: c TEST_SETUP(boot) pins the secret ( auth_secret_set(str_lit(\"test-secret\")) ) and points the app at sqlite::memory: , so every test starts from an empty database; WEBC_TEST_DB=postgres://\u2026 webc test runs the same suite against a live Postgres, which is the only way to know a login flow is portable. examples/accounts/tests/accounts_test.c is the whole version, including the cookie jar. What it is not Not a user model. Account is the app's, with the app's fields and the app's migrations; the header only knows how to hash and how to sign. Not roles or permissions. A role is a column and a check in the handler; there is no ACL engine to learn, and no @requires_role decorator that hides the check. Not password reset, email verification, or OAuth. Each is a route, a token you store, and a mail you send ( webc/mail.h ); making them a module would make them a decision the app did not take. Not a CSRF token. SameSite=Lax covers the form case; a cross-site flow needs a token the app owns. Not a rate limit. That is webc/limits.h , and it is one line in the login route: if (!rate_ok(\"login\", 5, 15 * 60 * 1000)) return res_error(429, \"slow_down\", \"Too many attempts.\") \u2014 five attempts per quarter hour per key, in a fixed window, from the header that documents it. Related Models and migrations \u2014 the Account model, .unique , and the migration that makes it a constraint. Forms \u2014 body_try , page_with(422, \u2026) , and the error under a field. The standard library \u2014 mail for a reset link, limits for the throttle. Reference \u00a710 Auth and \u00a79 Database ."},{"t":"Signals and the DOM","p":"guides/signals.html","g":"Guides","h":["What re-renders","Declaring state","A list's state","The store: frames in, generations out","Where the DOM lives","Gotchas worth knowing before they bite","See also"],"b":"A view in WebC is a C function that draws. State is a declaration, and the two questions that decide whether an app behaves are what is the state one of? and what re-renders? \u2014 this page answers both, in the order they bite. The snippets here are client-half files: each starts with #include <webc/web.h> and compiles on its own. A view that is compiled for both halves wires its controls with named() + bind() instead of on_click() \u2014 the quickstart shows that file end to end. What re-renders A render follows every action and every host callback. The bridge re-renders each mounted view \u2014 and diffs it \u2014 when control returns to it: after a click, after a typed field, after a webc_later timer, after a fetch or an SSE frame settles, after a push_url nav. Writing a signal does not schedule a render by itself; the flush point is the bridge boundary. So an action never has to \"touch the view\": c There is no tick signal to bump, and set(tick, get(tick) + 1) is a write nobody reads. The exception is a live binding \u2014 text_live , attr_live , class_live , show_live \u2014 which writes one node in place when its signal changes, and action_live , which declares that its handler only writes signals so the bridge sends the ops and skips the render: c Everything structural \u2014 an if , a for_each , a part , a new node \u2014 still needs the render. Declaring state Three declarations, one rule between them: what is this state one of? Declaration One value per Reached with Fits state(T, n, init) page (translation unit) get(n) / set(n, v) the app's own state state_in(T, n, init) a WInst you create get_in(&inst, n) , or get(n) inside with_inst a view mounted N times by your code state_own(T, n, init) render position get(n) / set(n, v) a row of a list, a mounted view's own copy c Writing the same value does not re-run effects (byte comparison). Effects run in topological order, once per batch. state , computed and effect declare file-scope statics, so they do not work inside a function body \u2014 and they are one per translation unit , which is right for app-wide state and wrong for a view that appears twice. That last point is the one that produces real bugs, and it has three answers: a row of a list takes state_own (below), a component called twice takes comp(Stepper, \u2026) ( Parts and components ), and a view you mount twice yourself takes state_in plus the WInst you pass to mount() . state_own outside the position that owns it panics , with a message that names the signal and the two ways out. That is deliberate: a per-row value read from a page-wide timer has no answer, and guessing one is worse than stopping. A list's state c webc check --wiring reads these declarations and names the mistakes: a signal a write feeds and nothing reads, a live binding on a page-wide signal inside a for_each (every row then shows the same value), and a stateful part called twice that thought it had two states. The store: frames in, generations out A live document arrives as frames \u2014 a seed, then a stream \u2014 and each frame's strings must die when the frame two later replaces it. WStore owns that rotation: c The server twin is WFrame ; together they are the ~100 lines every streaming client otherwise writes first. Where the DOM lives el(\"div\") { \u2026 } is a builder over a tree the framework owns. It is not a template language: it is C, so a loop is a for , a conditional is an if , and a helper that returns markup is a function. c Keyed lists ( for_each ) reuse nodes by key rather than by position, which is what makes a reorder cheap and keeps focus in the right input. See Parts and components for the function-shaped pieces. Gotchas worth knowing before they bite class_() replaces the attribute. A second class_() on the same element is not additive: compose the whole class string in one class_fmt() call. str is not a C string. It is a pointer and a length: print it with %.*s and its length, and copy it with str_cdup(a, s) when something downstream needs a NUL. Framework macro names are plain words. <webc/*.h> defines 104 lowercase words as macros \u2014 show , head , html , state , set , text , attr , page , first , last , name , key . A local helper called show() will not compile, and the error will point somewhere confusing. See also Reference \u00a711 Signals \u2014 including \u00a711c on state_own and \u00a711d on component instances. Parts and components \u2014 part , comp , lifecycle, children."},{"t":"Parts and components","p":"guides/components.html","g":"Guides","h":["The part","The problem a part does not solve","The component","Lifecycle","Children","What a component costs","When not to use one","Migrating a codebase","See also"],"b":"A view is a page. A part is a function that draws a piece of one. A component is a part that gets its own state per call site \u2014 and that difference is the whole feature, because a plain C function call pushes no render position and two calls of one part share one slot. The part c PART(Name, (arg, Type), \u2026) declares the prototype; the definition follows. That is not ceremony \u2014 the framework's own headers need the prototype to call the part from a header-declared view, and the macro keeps the two in step. The problem a part does not solve c After one click both steppers show a=1 / b=1 : state_own is per render position , and a function call does not push one. webc check --wiring names this file: Stepper is called 2 times in Order() and declares state_own \u2014 every call draws into the same position, so they share the state / fix: comp(Stepper, \u2026) at the calls that each need their own, or state(\u2026) The component c comp(Name, args\u2026) enters the call site as a render position, calls the part, and leaves \u2014 on every exit path, including an early return and a panic. What that buys, in one list because every item is a promise the framework keeps: state_own and computed_own inside the part resolve to this call ; identity is `(the position that drew it, the call site)`, so a re-render finds the same instance and the state survives; effect_own and mount_own start per instance and die with it; on_cleanup runs when the call goes away \u2014 a branch stops drawing it, a row leaves, the mount is destroyed; text_live , attr_live , class_live , show_live , bind_str and every action already resolve through the node's owner, so they inherit the instance with no extra wiring; a row that moves in a keyed list keeps its components. Calling a part without comp() keeps the older, coarser rule \u2014 it draws in the position that called it. That is what makes the feature opt-in: existing code is unchanged, and the check tells you which call sites want the other thing. Lifecycle Declaration What it does state_own / computed_own a slot per render position, created on first read, disposed with the position effect_own(name) { \u2026 } declares an effect that belongs to a render position; the body is at file scope, like every declaration mount_own(name) the one line in a view that starts that effect for the instance being drawn on_mount(fn, ctx) once the instance's nodes are in the document on_cleanup(fn, ctx) inside an effect: before each re-run, and when the position dies. Outside: when the position dies c Why the extra line: a C function body is not something a view can splice in \u2014 there are no closures \u2014 so effect_own 's body lives at file scope and the one thing the framework cannot infer is which effects a given view wants. It is the same pair effect_in + mount_in already has, except the framework owns the lifetime instead of the app. on_mount is the other piece a view has no room for: measure a box, focus an input, scroll to a row \u2014 once the nodes exist, without a webc_later(0, \u2026) and a hope. on_cleanup has two scopes and picks by where it is written: inside an effect it belongs to that run, so a re-run cleans up the previous one; outside an effect it belongs to the position. Children A function cannot take a block, so a component that wraps other markup takes a callback and a context \u2014 the same (fn, ctx) pair for_each and prose_with already use. The builder is ambient, so the children land exactly where the part calls kids() : c kids_of remembers the position that wrote the call, and kids() re-enters it while the children run: markup the caller wrote reads the caller's state. That is React's rule too \u2014 children belong to the parent \u2014 and it is the one that surprises people when it is the other way round. What a component costs 56 bytes per instance drawn : a 24-byte WInst and a 32-byte table slot. A list of 49 cells that each hold a chip is 2.7 KB. A pure-markup part costs nothing, because you call it plainly. When not to use one A component is the right answer when the state belongs to the thing being drawn . It is the wrong answer when the state is cross-cutting \u2014 written by a keyboard shortcut, a timer, or a document-level listener, none of which has a render position: c In that case the state belongs to the page ( state ). The framework panics rather than guessing: focus is render-owned state read outside the position that owns it: it is drawn by a mounted view or a for_each row, and read from that view or an action on it. Declare it state(...) for one value per page, or state_in(...) if the app owns the lifetime. There is a second, subtler reason to keep state at the page level: a component's state cannot be enumerated . If a spin has to post {req, bets:[\u2026]} built by walking every row's draft, per-row component state is write-only \u2014 nothing can walk it. The list stays one page-level collection. Migrating a codebase The change is mechanical, and the tool does the finding: webc check --wiring lists every stateful part called more than once in one view. For each call site that needs its own state, wrap the call: Stepper(\"a\") \u2192 comp(Stepper, \"a\") . Leave the rest alone. A part with no state_own cannot be affected. See also Reference \u00a711d for the identity rule and the cost table. Signals and the DOM for state_own on its own. tests/comp_test.c \u2014 eleven tests, one per promise on this page."},{"t":"The standard library","p":"guides/stdlib.html","g":"Guides","h":["The shelf","What a module looks like","The module that is a security boundary","The module that is a contract with a crawler","The honest entry","CSS belongs to the module","See also"],"b":"WebC's floor is small on purpose. Everything else is an opt-in header : #include <webc/table.h> is the whole install, the whole manifest and the whole cost. Nothing is in the bundle unless a file you compile names it. bash The shelf Module Tier What it is ui markup switch, segmented control, tab, meter, kbd \u00b7 badge, stat, empty state, skeleton, icon button icons markup stroke icons as data: the ones you draw are the ones that link table markup sortable, paginated table: order without owning the rows overlay markup dialog, drawer, menu, toast, tip \u2014 with the ARIA and the focus form markup a form drawn from the model that validates it theme markup design tokens and a dark edition that does not flash keys interaction one table of shortcuts, bound and documented from the same rows motion interaction enter animation and stagger, no script, reduced-motion honoured dnd interaction pick a row up and put it down: the drop index, not the maths live interaction rooms over SSE: whole documents, coalesced, a dead tab costs nothing csv data in and out, quoted correctly, by header name search data a search box that ANDs its words, escapes a typed % , and ranks the title i18n data one app, two languages: catalogs, plurals, direction upload data a file arrives and is checked: magic bytes, a name this side makes up time data dates, ages and two-unit durations, UTC only and proud of it richtext data HTML a user wrote, rendered through a policy: parsed, allow-listed, escaped ld data structured data: the article, the site, the trail, the FAQ \u2014 one expression mail platform a real email from one view: SMTP, or a file in the outbox jobs platform work that outlives the request: a table, a lease, a retry limits platform a rate limit that is one line, and a table that cannot grow pwa platform installable and offline: manifest, worker, cache keyed by the build Taking all twenty costs 11.3 KB gzip on a floor of 33.9 KB \u2014 measured, not estimated, by webc stdlib --weigh , which builds a probe per module. A typical app takes two or three. richtext is 5.3 KB of that on its own: a parser and an allow-list in the browser half, paid only by the page that renders a stored body in wasm. What a module looks like They are not wrappers around a dependency. overlay is the five details that make a box on top usable \u2014 role , aria-modal , a name, focus that moves into it after the batch, and a way out that does not need a mouse: c The backdrop and the panel are siblings , not parent and child, because a scrim that wraps the panel cannot tell a click outside from a click inside. That defect was found by an app ( examples/board 's new-card dialog submitted nothing and closed instead), and the fix belongs in the module. keys is one table that both binds and documents: c keys_help(cmds, box, open) draws the sheet \u2014 a dialog of <kbd> rows from the same array, so a shortcut cannot be bound and left undocumented. The module that is a security boundary richtext is the one module that is not about drawing something: it is the third case for authored bytes, beside text() (copy) and markup() (bytes you wrote) \u2014 HTML a user wrote, which is what every CMS, wiki and comment field stores. It parses once and re-emits through el() / attr() / text() , so escaping is not a second implementation: c A tag outside the policy is dropped and its words stay; script , style , iframe , svg , math and form go with their contents ; every URL is entity-decoded before it is judged, so java&#115;cript: is refused; srcset is refused whole if one candidate fails; an external link gets rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" . rich_with(s, .allow = RICH_TIGHT, .no_images = true) is the policy for comments. It sanitises a fragment , on the way out \u2014 what the browser posted is still the app's call \u2014 and it is a shelf module rather than a floor feature so an app that never stores HTML pays nothing for the parser it would not use. The module that is a contract with a crawler ld writes the JSON-LD a page states about itself, and its whole point is what it refuses to write. A block built with fmt() cannot omit a field, so a draft ends up claiming \"datePublished\":\"\" , and its nesting is retyped per page: c author becomes a Person , publisher an Organization , image an ImageObject , and a field left null is absent rather than empty. head_ld() is the door, and it is not sugar for head_html() : a <script> body is JSON and HTML, so a title containing </script> would close the element \u2014 the door escapes < , > and & as \\uXXXX , which is the same string to a JSON reader and cannot end a tag. webc lint names the hand-built version. ld_breadcrumbs , ld_faq , ld_graph , ld_website , ld_organization and ld_person cover the rest of what a publishing site emits; ASSERT_LD(res, \"author.name\", \u2026) is how a test holds it to its word. The honest entry table renders a header, a pager, sorting and empty states without owning your rows \u2014 and it is the one module with a gap: it composes the chrome but not the body, so a list of rows is still drawn by a for_each in your view. It is on the list. form is the one to read for how a module is supposed to feel: the input's kind, bounds, label, error and aria-describedby all come from the model declaration, so adding a field to a struct adds a field to the form. CSS belongs to the module Every module ships the stylesheet it expects: webc stdlib <module> --css prints it, and you either copy it into public/ or @import it. The markup a module emits carries class names, not inline styles, so a design system can restyle it without forking. theme is the exception that proves the rule: it exists to make the cascade right \u2014 the light block, then :root[data-theme=dark] , then @media (prefers-color-scheme:dark) \u2014 so the first paint is correct and nothing flashes. See also Reference \u00a712 The shelf \u2014 each module in detail. examples/stdlib \u2014 every module on one page, runnable."},{"t":"Redis and the query cache","p":"guides/data.html","g":"Guides","h":["The query cache","Redis","Which one is which","See also"],"b":"Two things go between your app and its database: a cache that is invisible when it works, and Redis for the state that is not a row. Neither is a dependency you have to install twice \u2014 the sqlite driver is linked in, and the postgres, MySQL ( the third driver ) and Redis clients speak the wire protocols directly. The query cache Every statement in the framework goes through one function, WRes db_exec() , so the cache is one function too. It stores the result set \u2014 the same WRes a driver returns \u2014 and replays it, which means db_all , db_one , a view and a test all get a cache without knowing it. bash Nothing to call. With those two variables a select is served from Redis, a write invalidates it, and the app keeps its shape: c Invalidation is a generation counter per table , not a tag set and not SCAN . A write does INCR webc:{<app>}:g:<table> \u2014 one hop, O(1), atomic across processes \u2014 and a cached read's key carries the generations it was read under. When a counter moves the key changes, so the old entry is never read again and dies of old age. There is no invalidation walk, and no thundering herd on expiry. What is never cached , because each one is a wrong answer waiting to happen: Skipped Why any write they invalidate; caching a write is not a thing a read inside a transaction it must see its own writes, and it may be rolled back a statement with a volatile function ( now() , random() , \u2026) the answer is not a function of the inputs for update / for share it takes locks; replaying it takes none /* no-cache */ select \u2026 , or anything inside nocache { \u2026 } the author said no a statement with no tables ( select 1 ) nothing to invalidate it with c Variable Default Decides WEBC_QUERY_CACHE off the whole feature WEBC_CACHE_TTL_MS 30000 how long an entry lives WEBC_CACHE_TTL_JITTER 10 percent, so a thousand entries do not expire together WEBC_CACHE_MAX_BYTES 262144 a bigger result is served but not cached WEBC_CACHE_MS 5 the cache's budget per statement; over it, the database answers WEBC_CACHE_GEN_MS 0 0 is exact; a window saves a hop and stops seeing other processes' writes for that long Redis c redis() is the process-wide handle from REDIS_URL ; redis_open(url) is one you own and redis_close() frees. The string and counter verbs have a short form that uses the default handle; the list, set and sorted-set verbs only have the explicit-handle form ( redis_lpush_(redis(), \u2026) , which is also the form every verb has when the handle is yours). Every call is fail-open, and asking is one line: c The Redis connection parks like every other fd in the runtime, so a slow reply costs a task, not a thread. Twenty commands in a row are one round trip with a pipeline; a subscription is a task: c Which one is which Need Use a result set read a thousand times per second the query cache (nothing to call) a counter, a session, a queue, a presence set Redis work that must outlive the request jobs.h \u2014 a table, a lease, a retry a fan-out to every tab in a room SSE rooms ( live.h ), with Redis pub/sub behind them when there is more than one process See also Reference \u00a79 Database \u2014 the whole of it, including the pool and the transaction rules. Real-time \u2014 the layer above this one."},{"t":"Real-time: SSE and live rooms","p":"guides/live.html","g":"Guides","h":["The server side","The client side","Frames and generations","When you write the stream yourself","More than one process","See also"],"b":"Real-time in WebC is one decision made early: the server sends whole documents, not deltas. A frame is the board as it is; the client replaces what it had and re-renders. That removes the entire class of bugs where a delta is applied twice, out of order, or to a client that missed one. The server side c live_join(room, key, seed) opens an SSE stream, sends seed as the first frame, and then sends the room's document whenever it changes. live_join_as(...) picks the event name. A mutation broadcasts: c live_json(collection) encodes with the model that owns the rows, so a subscriber and a tab that just loaded the page cannot disagree about what the board is. A hand-written JsonWriter in the app is that function with the app's arena in it \u2014 which is how the board was written before it existed. The room table is fixed at 64 rooms . A room that is evicted costs its subscribers a rejoin, which is what a reconnect does anyway; a table that grows under load is what an outage is made of. Publishing to nobody is not an error and not a queue. The state of record is the database, and the next subscriber reads it there. live_doc(room, key) reads the room's document; live_presence_count() is how many are in it. The client side c One link per page is the rule: a second stream is a second reconnect loop and a second generation pair. A page that needs two rooms has one screen too many. Frames and generations A stream's memory discipline is a verb, because every streaming client otherwise writes the same hundred lines by hand. WFrame on the server and WStore on the client each own two generations : the frame being built and the one still in use, so a frame's strings die when the frame after next replaces it. c c When you write the stream yourself res_sse(fn, ctx) hands the body to a function that owns a socket for as long as it runs. The three facts that shape a correct stream: a subscriber wakes on a channel rather than spinning, a heartbeat is what notices a dead tab, and a failed write is how the loop ends. c The framework's live.h is that loop, with the coalescing: a hundred writes inside one frame produce one send, and a client that was slow gets the newest document rather than a queue of them. More than one process A room lives in the process. Behind a load balancer, put Redis pub/sub underneath: c sse_send_text and redis_publish are the two lines that make the fan-out work; the room table stays per process, which is the right granularity for presence. See also Reference \u00a77d \u2014 the stream pattern in full, and res_sse . Redis and the query cache \u2014 the layer under the fan-out. examples/board \u2014 a live kanban with drag and drop, end to end."},{"t":"Testing","p":"guides/testing.html","g":"Guides","h":["A test file","The assertions","Requests with more than a path","A browser, without a socket","Setup and teardown","Subtests","Testing the browser half","Wiring, not just behaviour","In the framework repository"],"b":"A test in WebC is a C function with a name, run by a binary the framework owns. A handler is reachable without a socket, which makes the usual unit/integration split unnecessary: the suite runs in milliseconds and it exercises the same code the server runs. bash --filter picks suites (and runs them whole); --test narrows by name across the suites you filtered. A name that matches nothing is an answer, not a failure \u2014 one flag for both would report \"no tests\" for a file you had just named. A test file c webc test prints one line per test ( --- PASS: list_notes (0.00s) ), keeps going after a failure, and turns a panicking handler into a failed test rather than a dead binary. The assertions Macro Checks ASSERT_EQ(a, b) / ASSERT_NE / ASSERT_TRUE / ASSERT_FALSE values ASSERT_STR_EQ(a, b) / ASSERT_STR_CONTAINS(hay, needle) strings ASSERT_NULL(p) / ASSERT_NOT_NULL(p) pointers ASSERT_HTML_COUNT(r, sel, n) a CSS selector, counted in the response's document ASSERT_HTML_TEXT(r, sel, text) the text of the first match ASSERT_HTML_ATTR(r, sel, name, value) an attribute ASSERT_JSON(r, \"0.body\", \"hello\") a dotted path into the response body ASSERT_JSON_DOC(doc, \"views\", \"42\") the same question about a document you built ASSERT_LD(r, \"author.name\", \"Ada\") a dotted path into the page's JSON-LD block; ASSERT_LD_ABSENT proves a draft's datePublished is not there \u2014 and not \"\" ASSERT_STATUS(r, 303) the status and nothing else ASSERT_HEADER(r, \"Location\", \"/notes\") a header by name, case-insensitively ASSERT_REDIRECT(r, \"/notes\") a 302/303/307 and the Location ASSERT_DB_ONE(Todo, title, \"hello\") / ASSERT_DB_ONE_I64(Todo, done, 1) the row a mutation should have written ASSERT_DB_COUNT(Todo, 1) how many rows the table has The HTML assertions parse the response with a real selector engine, so a test can pin structure ( main > ul > li.todo ) rather than a substring of the markup \u2014 and it keeps passing when the whitespace changes. Requests with more than a path c That is also how a test logs in by hand \u2014 take the Set-Cookie out of the signup response and send it back. A test that does it more than once wants the client below. A browser, without a socket TestClient keeps the cookies, encodes a model into the form the app parses, and knows what a successful mutation looks like: c tc_get , tc_send(method, path) , tc_post_form , tc_post_form_at , tc_post_json and tc_post_body are the six ways in. The form encoder matches model_from_form exactly \u2014 one input per field in declaration order, the pk left out, a bool as on / off , an enum as its name, a VecStr as one comma-separated value \u2014 so a test and the browser it stands in for post the same thing. What it does not do is run JavaScript (that is webc test --client ) or follow a redirect: a test that follows one hides the 303 it came for. Setup and teardown c TEST_SETUP(fn) and TEST_TEARDOWN(fn) run once per file, around every test in it. The framework owns webc_boot() and main() , so an app puts its setup in a webc_boot of its own only when the default is not enough \u2014 and then the suite calls the same function against a scratch database. Subtests c The row's name labels the failure, so a broken case reads as parses_numbers/negative: -8 != -7 . Testing the browser half <webc/client_test.h> runs a view without a browser: it renders to a string, dispatches a named control as if it had been clicked, and re-renders \u2014 which is how the framework tests its own components and how an app can too. c view_Rows is the function view(Rows) defines \u2014 a view is a C function, so a test can render it into a string with no browser, no container and no mount. The same file has client_type(\"body\", \"typed\") for a field and client_press(\"meta+k\") for a shortcut \u2014 the bridge's own tables, so a test cannot drift from the browser. client_dispatch_at(hook, ev, x, y, w, h) says where the pointer was, and ev_point() inside the action then answers what the bridge would have sent, which is how a chart crosshair or a tooltip becomes ordinary testable code. A queued on_mount that nobody ran is a failure rather than a silence. Wiring, not just behaviour webc check --wiring is the static half of the suite. It reads the declarations and the reads and names the disagreements: a signal written and never read (a leftover tick ), a live binding on a page-wide signal inside a for_each , a stateful part called twice that thought it had two states, a bind(...) naming an action no view renders. In the framework repository bash webc check also generates every scaffold and builds it, so the templates cannot rot."},{"t":"The editor: LSP and VS Code","p":"guides/editor.html","g":"Guides","h":["What it knows","In VS Code, Cursor, Antigravity, \u2026","Any other editor","Living beside clangd","When it looks broken","The gate","See also"],"b":"webc lsp is the language server, and it ships in the binary you already have: no Node, no second download, no build step. The VS Code extension in editors/vscode/ is a thin client for it \u2014 no dependencies, nothing fetched from a registry \u2014 and every other editor talks to the same command over stdio. What it is for is the seam between WebC and C. GET(\u2026) , view(\u2026) , el(\u2026) and the model tuples are macros, so the compiler's answer to a typo is use of undeclared identifier 'view_Nope' behind five frames of WC_CAT . The server answers \"no view named Nope in this project \u2014 view(Nope) draws it, or declare_view(Nope) in the shared header announces it\", and offers the line to add. What it knows LSP capability what you get publishDiagnostics WebC's own errors (a route in the wrong half, an unknown view or model field, a stdlib module in the wrong file set) and the compiler's, with the DSL's bytes left to the DSL engine completion inside a WEBC_MODEL tuple: field kinds, models, enums and every .qualifier ; inside el(\u2026) : HTML tags; inside page(\u2026) / mount(\u2026) : the project's views; inside #include <webc/\u2026> : the shelf, filtered by the half this file compiles for hover a macro's doc line, its shape and which half it belongs to; a model's fields and the file it is declared in; a view's signature and where it is drawn go-to-definition the model, the view, the action or the route handler the name refers to references every place that name is used document and workspace symbols the outline: models, views, actions, routes code actions ( quickfix ) two fixes, the ones a person actually makes: close an unclosed block, and add declare_view(Name) to the shared header The diagnostics have codes, so a rule in webc lint and an underline in the editor are the same sentence: code what it says no-view page(Nope) names a view nothing draws \u2014 with the fix that adds declare_view(Nope) to the header both halves include unclosed , unclosed-block el(\"li\") { never closed: one error, not thirty, and the fix closes it no-vec db_all(Todo) without a Vec(Todo) \u2014 the diagnostic names the line to add not-a-model a db_*(\u2026) call whose first argument is not a WEBC_MODEL type no-action on_click(name) with no action(name) to bind half-module a stdlib header that may not appear in this half ( webc/mail.h in a shared file) It knows all of that from the sources \u2014 including models declared in shared headers, which no webc.json lists \u2014 and never reads .webc/ , so a fresh clone is a working workspace. Two things it deliberately does not do: Format a document. clang-format rewrites el(\"h1\") { text(\"Todos\"); } into four lines, which is not this language's style. Whole-document formatting is refused with that reason; range formatting outside a DSL block is what webc lsp offers instead. webc fmt exists, but it is clang-format over the whole tree \u2014 point it at a project whose DSL blocks you have already formatted by hand, or expect it to disagree with you. Second-guess the compiler. A diagnostic inside a DSL macro's own parentheses is dropped: those bytes are the WebC engine's to explain. A block that never closed stops the compiler half from running at all, so an unbalanced brace is one error instead of thirty. Positions are UTF-16 (as the protocol requires) and every save sends the whole buffer: a WebC file is small, and a full text sync cannot desynchronise from the editor's copy. In VS Code, Cursor, Antigravity, \u2026 bash code is any VS Code\u2013compatible CLI. To install it for good, copy the folder into the editor's extension directory as webc.webc-lsp-0.1.0/ ( ~/.vscode/extensions/ for VS Code, ~/.antigravity-ide/extensions/ for Antigravity), or vsce package it into a .vsix . A symlink rather than a copy tracks the folder: bash Three settings, and two commands: setting meaning webc.lsp.path the webc binary. ~ , ${workspaceFolder} , ${userHome} and ${env:NAME} are expanded and a relative path is taken from the workspace folder. Empty: build/webc in the workspace or a parent, else webc on the PATH webc.lsp.args extra arguments, e.g. [\"--trace\", \"/tmp/lsp.jsonl\"] webc.lsp.enabled start the server at all (default true ) command what it does WebC: Show the language server log the channel, including every diagnostic the server sent WebC: Restart the language server respawn without reloading the window Changing a setting restarts the client in place \u2014 a setting that is read once at activation and never again is the reason \"I set the correct path and it still fails\" exists. When the binary cannot start, there is one message that says which of the three it is (not found, not executable, exited) with the resolved path and the last lines of the server's stderr, because \"set webc.lsp.path \" shown to someone who already did is the fastest way to teach them their setting is ignored. Any other editor The command is webc lsp --root <project> ; everything else is the editor's LSP client. lua elisp Anything that can spawn a process and speak LSP works the same way: Helix, Zed, Sublime, Emacs with lsp-mode . The server announces its capabilities at initialize , so an editor that offers fewer features simply uses fewer. Living beside clangd C editors have their own language server, and it is clangd. webc lsp writes compile_commands.json at the project root \u2014 the flags webc build uses, one entry per source \u2014 which clangd looks for without being told anything. That file is why clangd agrees with webc build about include paths instead of inventing errors: measured in this repository, twenty-one invented errors became none. WEBC_LSP_NO_COMPILE_COMMANDS=1 turns it off for a project that manages its own. Run both if you like. clangd is right about C and has no idea what el(\"li\") { \u2026 } means; the WebC server is right about the DSL and asks the compiler about the C. When it looks broken symptom the cause \"no diagnostics at all\" webc.lsp.enabled is false, or the workspace has no webc.json \u2014 the server needs a project, not a folder the extension's message names a path you did not type webc.lsp.path was wrong and ~ / ${\u2026} were expanded; the message shows the resolved path completions for models are empty the models are declared in a file the project does not list \u2014 put them in the shared header, where both halves can see them clangd floods the file with errors compile_commands.json is missing or stale; open a .c file and save it, and the server rewrites it a diagnostic stays after the fix save the file: the compiler half runs on save when a check costs more than 120 ms --trace FILE writes every message the server saw and sent as JSONL, which is the fastest way to report a problem with the editor integration rather than with your code. The gate make lsp is what keeps the two halves honest: bash --selftest asserts what the server says about a real project (diagnostics, a hover, a definition, a quick fix) and --replay diffs the whole protocol against a recorded session, so a change to the wire is a diff in review rather than a bug report. --bench exists because a language server that stutters is a language server people turn off. The client's own test is editors/vscode/resolve.test.js (no dependencies, run by make lsp when node is present): it checks the resolver against every way a path can be wrong \u2014 missing, a directory, not executable, relative, on no PATH \u2014 because a client that silently ignores the setting is a broken window. See also Reference \u00a714d The language server \u2014 the design, in reference voice. Agentic coding \u2014 the same diagnostics as data, through webc mcp . Reference \u00a712 \u2014 why there are two engines with one line between them. Testing \u2014 webc test , which is the other half of not guessing."},{"t":"Agentic coding","p":"guides/agents.html","g":"Guides","h":["Three things, cheapest first","1. AGENTS.md, which the scaffold writes","The documentation goes into the app","2. The skill","Why the leaves are generated","Installing it","What is in the leaves","3. The MCP server","The loop, end to end","Make the gate automatic","What agents get wrong here, and what stops it","Machine output is a contract","If you are writing a tool on top of this","What this is not","Related"],"b":"WebC is unusually good for a coding agent to work in, and most of that is not on purpose \u2014 it falls out of decisions taken for other reasons. A model is one declaration, so a change has one place to start and four places it lands. The halves are a file list, not a preprocessor test. webc check is one command with one exit code. And the framework has already retired 44 spellings because they were the second way to do something, which means the wrong shape is refused rather than merely not recommended. What was missing was the part between the agent and the compiler: a vocabulary, a gate it can call, and a way to ask the project a question without grepping. Three things now supply that, and this page is about giving them to an agent and what it gets. Three things, cheapest first Give it What it carries Who reads it Cost AGENTS.md + .webc/docs/ webc check as the gate, the half rules, fmt() / persist() , migrations, \"believe the lint\" \u2014 and the documentation itself, in the app any tool that reads AGENTS.md \u2014 which is most of them, with no setup a file webc new already wrote skills/webc/ the five ideas, the loop, recipes, the traps, and fourteen reference leaves generated from this documentation harnesses that read a skills folder \u2014 Claude Code, pi, and the ones that follow the same layout one symlink webc mcp the project index, the surface, the gate, the tests, and a dev server it can read clients that speak the Model Context Protocol one block of JSON in a config file They are layers, not alternatives. The AGENTS.md is what an agent reads before it writes anything; the skill is what it thinks in; the MCP server is what it asks when the first two are not enough. An agent with a shell and only the skill can do the whole job \u2014 the MCP server removes the greps, not the need to think. 1. AGENTS.md , which the scaffold writes Every webc new writes one into the app, next to webc.json . Eight lines, and each one is a mistake an agent makes by default: markdown This is the highest value per line of anything on this page. A skill has to be installed and a server has to be configured; AGENTS.md is read because it is there, in the one place every harness looks, and it answers the questions an agent would otherwise answer by guessing. The documentation goes into the app webc new also writes the framework's documentation into .webc/docs/ \u2014 SKILL.md plus one reference per topic \u2014 and the last lines of AGENTS.md say so. That is deliberate: an agent working in an app should not have to find the framework's sources to answer \"what does this name do\", and the one session that taught us this was spent grepping a checkout for auth_hash_password after deciding the auth API was undocumented. bash .webc/ is build output, so the docs are gitignored: the toolchain owns them, webc docs rewrites them, and a fresh clone gets them back with the one command AGENTS.md names. Nothing is vendored and nothing goes stale \u2014 the leaves are generated from the same reference the toolchain was built from. When a name is still not in a leaf, the answer is one command, not a search: bash The repository has its own AGENTS.md at the root, for a different reader: the gates ( make , make test , make lsp , make mcp , make dx , make skill-check ), the house rules (one scanner, one index, one set of diagnostics; the api ratchet; a budget is a gate; machine output is a contract), and the layout. If you are having an agent change the framework rather than an app, that is the file that matters. 2. The skill skills/webc/ is a skill in the shape harnesses expect: SKILL.md (a router, written by hand) and references/ (fourteen leaves, generated; one per reference section). SKILL.md carries the part that cannot be generated \u2014 what to do. The five ideas that make the rest of the framework a consequence rather than a list of rules: a string belongs to a scope \u2014 the framework installs the arena, an app writes fmt() or persist() ; a response is a value \u2014 a handler returns one, which is why tests need no socket; a render follows an action \u2014 one view, both targets, one context type; the halves are enforced by the compiler \u2014 server / shared / client are file sets, and a name that is not declared in your half is not available; a model is the schema \u2014 struct, descriptor, validation, JSON, form and SQL column types from one tuple. Then the loop, the shape of a project, five recipes (add a field, add a page, add an endpoint, read a request, make a button act), the rules that bite, and a routing table: which leaf to read for which task. Why the leaves are generated A hand-written skill teaches whatever was true the day it was written. This framework retires spellings \u2014 webc lint --rules is the table \u2014 and a skill still teaching a retired call is a language nobody can compile, delivered with confidence. So tools/gen_skill.py builds the fourteen leaves from pages that are already maintained: website/src/content/guides/*.md , anchored sections of docs/reference.md , and webc lint --rules for the traps table. Each leaf opens with a line-number index, so a reader loads a span rather than a file. make skill regenerates; make skill-check is the gate, and it fails on three things: a leaf that would change \u2014 the documentation moved and the skill did not; a retired spelling anywhere in the skill's own prose \u2014 the gate the framework applies to apps, applied to the thing that teaches them; a C block in SKILL.md that does not compile, or that has an ellipsis in it pretending to be a file. That third gate is website/tools/snippets.py , the same one that checks the site: bash It is worth saying that the gate works, because it is not a formality: it failed twice while this page was being written \u2014 once on a documentation edit that had not been propagated into the leaves, and once because SKILL.md named a retired target macro in prose in order to say don't . Both are exactly the failure the gate exists to make impossible to ship. Installing it The repository is the source of truth; install it by pointing at it. bash A symlink, not a copy, so make skill updates the installed skill in the same breath. Harnesses that keep skills elsewhere want the same directory \u2014 skills/webc/ is plain Markdown with YAML front matter ( name , description ) and needs no build step to be read. If your tool does not read a skills folder at all, it probably reads AGENTS.md . Add one line to the app you are working in \u2014 the scaffold's file already ends with the pointer: markdown What is in the leaves Leaf Read it when the task is core-and-strings.md a string, fmt() , a scope, an error, a Vec or Map models-and-orm.md a field, a table, a filter, a relation, a page of rows http-and-routes.md a route, a response, a cookie, SSE, middleware, a file views-and-signals.md markup, a signal, an effect, a part, a component client-and-wasm.md a button that acts, a live value, calling JavaScript data-and-sql.md Postgres, MySQL, a migration per dialect, Redis, the query cache forms.md a form, validation, the error under a field auth.md a password, a login, a session cookie, a guarded page concurrency.md a background task, a channel, select , a timer, a mutex stdlib.md choosing a module, and what it costs in bytes testing.md a test, an assertion, a fixture ops-and-deploy.md logs, metrics, limits, a deploy cli.md a webc command, the language server, the MCP server traps.md \"why does the compiler say that\" \u2014 the retired spellings, as a generated table Fourteen leaves for seventeen sections, and that is checked rather than intended: every top-level section of docs/reference.md must be quoted by some leaf, and make skill-check fails naming the section when one is not. The gate exists because auth was missing from the eleven-leaf version \u2014 the coverage was an opinion until it became a build error. 3. The MCP server webc mcp speaks the Model Context Protocol over stdio from the same binary as everything else: no Node, no second download, no version skew between the server that answers and the compiler it answers about. The design of it \u2014 and the seven tools in detail \u2014 is reference \u00a714f ; what matters here is when it earns its place. A shell already gives an agent webc check and grep . Four things it does not: What the agent needs What the shell costs What the tool returns the gate, as data prose to parse, per run {ok, steps:[{name, ok, output}]} the project's own index a grep that cannot tell a half from a comment models, fields, views, actions, routes, and each file's half, with positions the whole public surface grep api.txt , guessing at spellings a match's declaration, header and doc line the page it just built two processes, curl , a port to remember a dev server held across calls, and a fetch from it Point a client at it: json --root defaults to the working directory, so a client that starts in the project can leave it out. webc mcp --selftest runs a scripted session against examples/todo and asserts what the tools answer, which is how you tell a protocol "},{"t":"The API boundary","p":"guides/api.html","g":"Guides","h":["What the sources already say","The typed client","The ratchet","Why this is the beachhead","See also"],"b":"A WebC app's routes and models are typed end to end, and the moment an outside consumer asks what the API looks like, that typing has an answer: the boundary itself, generated. This page is the beachhead \u2014 the story of pointing a React app (or a mobile client, or a partner) at a WebC backend without anyone reading C. The API boundary: webc openapi and webc client --ts What the sources already say Every route is a macro with a typed path, every body comes from the body family, every JSON response is a json / json_list call with its status literal and the shape it names, and every model carries its own constraints. webc openapi writes that down as OpenAPI 3.1 : bash paths from the route table \u2014 :id becomes {id} , and the parameter is an integer where the handler's own path_i64 says so; request bodies from body / body_try (JSON and form content \u2014 both parse), from body_json / body_form (one each); responses from the json calls \u2014 json_list(200, db_all(Note)) is an array of Note , and so is json(200, db_all(Note)) ; schemas from the model descriptors: .max is maxLength , .gte / .lte are minimum / maximum , .format is format , .pk is readOnly , .no_json is absent, and the flat {error, message, status} every failure answers with is the WebcError schema. What it refuses to guess matters as much: a response that is not a model is an {} with a description, no query parameter is invented, and pages are not the API. The typed client A document is for tools; a developer wants functions. webc client --ts writes the typed fetch client from the same facts: bash ts The Input twin is what a create sends \u2014 no pk (the database assigns it), no .no_json fields. Names are mechanical on purpose: todos becomes Todo only because a model backs the rewrite, addresses stays addresses , a GET that answers a list reads as list\u2026 , a POST to a path that names its own act ( /delete ) reads as that act. Field names keep their wire spelling \u2014 the client describes what the server sends, not what a style guide wishes it sent. The file is the app's: edit the names, commit it, and --check fails when the sources drifted underneath it. The ratchet Both files are generated once and owned from then on \u2014 commit them, diff them in review. CI is one line, and webc ci writes it for you: the workflow runs the --check of each file the app ships. yaml Why this is the beachhead \"Ditch React\" is a hard sell; \"ditch your Node BFF first\" is an easy one. A React team can point the generated client at a WebC backend tomorrow \u2014 typed, no C to read \u2014 and once the backend is WebC, moving the view half is the natural next step: it is the same models. If React is the vocabulary you already speak, the Rosetta guide is the dictionary for that conversation. See also Reference \u00a714 for every flag ( --out , --check ). Deploying \u2014 webc ci and the dist artifact on a tag. The ORM \u2014 where the constraints being exported come from."},{"t":"Coming from React, Solid and Next","p":"guides/rosetta.html","g":"Guides","h":["The one mapping that explains most of it","Solid, specifically","A component, both spellings","What has no equivalent (and why that is the pitch)","Where to go next"],"b":"You already know most of WebC. The model is the same one every component framework uses \u2014 declared state, a render that reads it, events that write it \u2014 and the differences that matter are in which spelling does which job. This page is the dictionary: the name you know, the name WebC uses, and the sentence of difference underneath. Coming from React, Solid and Next The one mapping that explains most of it You know WebC The difference under the name useState (per component instance) state_own per render position \u2014 a row of a list, a mounted view, a comp() call site useState (app-wide) state one per translation unit; right for the app's own state useReducer state + an action the action is a named function, not a switch over string types useMemo computed / computed_own topological, runs once per batch useEffect effect_own + mount_own no closure: the effect body is file-scope C, started where the view draws it useEffect cleanup on_cleanup registered per run, and when the position dies props PART(Name, (title, Text), \u2026) typed arguments the compiler checks at every call site children WKids + kids_of(body, ctx) children are created by the parent \u2014 same rule as React's context the view's context struct render(Name, .section = sec, \u2026) fills it by field name, checked by the compiler JSX el(\"div\") { \u2026 } markup in C: attributes are typed macros, a wrong type is a compile error className class_() / class_fmt() class_fmt(\"row row-%d\", i) formats; class_(\"50% off\") is a value, not a format key={id} lists for_each(item, rows) keyed by the model's .pk ; the action receives the item, never a stale pointer conditional render if in the view a branch that appears or disappears is a supported render; show() is the no-node toggle useRef + focus named() + focus_hook() hooks by name; reveal_hook() scrolls into view controlled input el(\"input\") { bind_str(sig); } one line, no matching line in client_main Suspense / loading states WRes states res_loading / res_failed / res_ensure \u2014 four states, no wrappers error boundary mw_recover() + try a panic is a 500 with the request named; a fail() is a value react-router route(\"/todos/:id\", \u2026) the same path syntax the server router uses; push_url / on_nav fetch / SWR / TanStack Query webc_get / res_get_json a verb, not a shim: the bridge is the only code that touches fetch Server Components the shared file set one view compiles for both halves; there is no client/server boundary to mark \u2014 webc.json 's file sets are it \"use client\" (inverted) server-only names ( db_* , req , body ) are simply not declared in a shared file \u2014 the half is a compile error, not a directive Server Actions action() + post_to() the route is the contract: it works with JavaScript off and gets sharper with it on Next middleware the middleware chain webc_use(mw_*) in webc_boot() ; the framework's chain is installed unless you say otherwise Next route handlers GET() / POST() typed extractors, a Response value; file-scope because C23 removed nested functions loaders ( useLoaderData ) the route fills the context return render(Page, .site = site, .list = rows); \u2014 a typo is a compiler error naming the field <Link> href(url_to(\"story\", \u2026)) named routes build their URLs; push_url navigates without a reload Zod / schema validation WEBC_MODEL qualifiers .max = 500 validates the JSON body, the form post and the client half \u2014 one declaration tRPC / typed API webc openapi the boundary as OpenAPI 3.1, generated from the same facts Testing Library webc test ASSERT_HTML(r, \"ul > li\") \u2014 the document, not substrings; TestClient is the browser with a cookie jar Fast Refresh the wasm swap webc dev swaps the module under the page: DOM, signals, scroll and caret survive DevTools WebC.diagnostics() / WebC.why(x, y) the ops stream and the click inspector, in the console Solid, specifically Fine-grained reactivity is where Solid and WebC agree most \u2014 and the one place WebC is more explicit about the price: createSignal \u2192 state / state_own / state_in ; the table in the [mental model] decides which. createEffect \u2192 effect / effect_own . For keyed \u2192 for_each \u2014 but the whole view runs again after an action and diffs; the opt-out is a live binding : text_live writes one node, and an action_live skips the render entirely. That is Solid's per-node update, declared at the node instead of bought everywhere. createResource \u2192 WRes + res_get_json , with res_compute for CPU-shaped work, chunked. A component, both spellings The React you would write, then the WebC that is the same idea: jsx c One file where React has one component, and the difference is the contract: the view names its elements ( named(\"inc\") ), the client names the behaviour ( bind ), and webc check --wiring refuses to let the two disagree. Two comp(Stepper, \u2026) calls keep separate quantities because the call site is the owner \u2014 no key needed, no re-mount dance. In a fullstack app the same view lives in the shared half and the actions in the client half; the [components] guide is the long version. What has no equivalent (and why that is the pitch) No virtual DOM. The browser half patches the real DOM through a line protocol you can read ( WebC.diagnostics().ops ); the server half renders the same view for the first paint and the client hydrates it \u2014 measured at zero created nodes. No bundler, no npm. webc.json names the files; webc build compiles each half; the standard library is opt-in headers whose byte cost is measured per module ( webc size --modules ). Markup is C. That is not an apology \u2014 a class_() takes a str , a Text or a number, and the wrong one is a compile error, not a hydration warning at 2am. No suspense streaming. The live answer is SSE: whole documents, coalesced, behind live rooms \u2014 the [real-time guide] is that story. Strings belong to a scope. fmt() lives in the request or the render; persist() is the one escape hatch. The [mental model] page makes this one idea do most of the work. Where to go next [The mental model] \u2014 five ideas, and everything else follows from them. [Quickstart] \u2014 ten minutes to a running app with a database. The [signals] and [components] guides for the state table in full. [Testing] \u2014 what a suite looks like when a handler is a function you can call."},{"t":"Deploying","p":"guides/deploy.html","g":"Guides","h":["What is in the build","The three ways to run it","The ops files: webc deploy --pack","CI: webc ci","Configuration","Health, metrics, and looking inside","Behind a proxy","Databases in production","The size gate","A checklist","See also"],"b":"A WebC app deploys as one binary and one directory . There is no runtime to install, no node_modules, no PATH to arrange for a second interpreter: the server is a static-ish C binary, the browser half is a .wasm file it serves, and the assets are the files you wrote. bash What is in the build Artifact What it is .webc/app the server binary: routes, database, views, the SSE links .webc/app.wasm the browser half \u2014 only when webc.json has a client list public/ served as-is by mw_static , with cache headers partials/ authored HTML included by name ( partial(\"site/head\") ), read once at first use migrations/ applied at boot, in filename order webc size reports what each one costs and refuses to guess; make budget in the framework repo is the same number as a gate. The three ways to run it bash webc serve is the useful one in front of people: gzip on, byte ranges answered, graceful drain on SIGTERM (in-flight requests finish, streams are closed with a frame the client understands), and the dev reloader off. The ops files: webc deploy --pack The binary is the whole deploy; the files around it are the same in every app except for the name and the port. The lines that are not the same are the ones a copy-paste from a blog gets wrong: bash writes, into dist/ , the ritual stamped from the manifest it just built: File What it is Dockerfile the image around the binary. The base is an ARG , not a choice \u2014 a base image is a promise about someone else's machine fly.toml fly.io: the port, HTTPS, and a note on why a page with streams wants min_machines_running = 1 <name>.service a hardened systemd unit: DynamicUser , ProtectSystem=strict , and the one ReadWritePaths= a sqlite file needs Caddyfile.webc the proxy in front \u2014 flush_interval -1 is the line a live page cannot live without nginx-webc.conf the same for nginx: proxy_buffering off , a read timeout in hours, the websocket headers DEPLOY.md what each file is and the commands that use it The files are the app's the moment they land: edit them, commit them, diff them on the next deploy. No pack writes an active healthcheck \u2014 the health route is a convention the app implements (below), not something the framework serves, and a probe that works in the template and fails on the platform is worse than none. CI: webc ci The gate the docs promise, as a workflow: bash It runs webc check and webc test , attaches a dist/ artifact on tags, adds the wasi-sdk step by itself when the app has a client half \u2014 and pins the framework with one WEBC_REF at the top, the way any dependency is pinned. Bumping it is an edit the app makes deliberately. Configuration Environment first, then a JSON file, then the default \u2014 and the model is the schema for the file, so a typo in config.json is a startup error naming the field rather than a value that silently did not apply. bash A secret that is missing in production is a refusal to start, not a random key per boot \u2014 the failure mode of the latter is sessions that log everyone out on every deploy. Health, metrics, and looking inside bash /health is app code every scaffold carries ( GET(\"/health\") { return res_text(200, \"ok\"); } is the whole probe), and /readyz is the same idea for the database and Redis, by hand where an app wants it. /metrics and /debug are the framework's. Point a platform's probe at a route the app actually serves: the deploy packs ship no active healthcheck for exactly this reason, and a probe that works in a template and fails on the platform is worse than none. The metrics are the ones that matter under load, not a generic set: request counts by route and status, latency histograms, the database pool (open, waiting, refused), the scheduler (tasks runnable, parked), the query cache (hit, miss, bypassed) and the room table. /debug (behind WEBC_DEBUG=1 ) adds the request log with timings, the route table, the migrated schema, and a CPU sampler that names the function a slow request was in. Behind a proxy The framework reads X-Forwarded-For and X-Forwarded-Proto only when WEBC_TRUST_PROXY=1 ; that is the setting that makes rate limits and peer mean the client rather than the proxy. Cookies are Secure and SameSite=Lax when the request arrived as HTTPS. c Order matters in both directions: compression wraps whatever answers after it, and the timeout has to be inside the headers so the 504 it writes carries them. What is not in that function is already there: webc_main() calls webc_install_default_middleware() after webc_boot() , so the request id, the logging, the panic recovery, the body limit, the static files, the document pass and the 404 page are the framework's, and the app's middleware \u2014 registered first \u2014 wraps all of it. Two of the calls above are the framework's own defaults, named here because this deployment puts them in a particular place: a middleware whose name is already in the chain is not installed twice, so mw_compress() and mw_recover() are this app's, at this position, and there is still exactly one of each. That is also how mw_headers() above becomes the policy instead of a second header beside webc.json 's. Two keys in webc.json cover what would otherwise need C: \"csp\" for the policy a project without its own mw_headers() gets, and \"not_found\" for the page a browser gets for a URL nobody routed. Databases in production sqlite is a real choice for a single-node app: PRAGMA journal_mode=WAL is set for you, and a write transaction is a file lock rather than a network hop. postgres speaks the wire protocol directly \u2014 no libpq , no connection-string quirks \u2014 and its socket parks like every other fd, so a slow query holds a connection rather than a processor. Migrations run at boot. In a multi-instance deploy, run them once ( webc migrate up ) and let the instances boot against the migrated schema; webc check compares models to schema and names drift. The size gate Byte budgets are part of the build, not a review topic: console webc size --modules builds a probe per standard-library module, so \"what does table.h cost me\" has an answer instead of an opinion. make budget fails the build when a number grows past its limit, which is why the framework's own floor has stayed where it is. A checklist webc check clean \u2014 build, tests, doctor, boot, schema, routes. partials/ shipped beside public/ \u2014 a partial is read from disk at runtime, and a missing one renders as nothing (one warning in the log, nothing on the page). webc check catches a name with no file before you deploy, not after. WEBC_SECRET set, and different from the dev one. WEBC_TRUST_PROXY=1 if anything sits in front. webc migrate up run (or boot migrations enabled) before traffic. /readyz wired into the platform's health check, /metrics into its scraper. SIGTERM handled \u2014 webc serve already drains; make sure your platform sends it. See also Reference \u00a715 Ops and \u00a714 The CLI . Testing \u2014 what webc check runs before any of this."},{"t":"Reference: how to read it","p":"reference/index.html","g":"Reference","h":["The sections"],"b":"The reference is the framework's own manual: sixteen sections, in the order the framework is built, with every macro, every limit and every deliberate deviation written down. It is also the same text that ships in the repository ( docs/reference.md ) and that webc lint checks for retired names. Read it in three ways. As a book. Start at \u00a71 House rules and keep pressing next. The sections build: strings and arenas, collections, models, HTTP, the database, then the frontend in three parts (signals, DOM, the bridge), then the CLI, testing, ops and the limits. As a lookup. Everything is one page per section, so the browser's find is usually enough; or press <kbd>/</kbd> and search \u2014 the index covers every heading and every paragraph. As the contract. The reference states what is implemented and covered by tests , and says so when something is not. Where a choice is surprising, it says what the choice costs, in the same paragraph that makes it. The sections \u00a7 Section What is in it 1 House rules the six rules every API obeys 2 Core strings, arenas, errors, fmt , persist 3 Collections Vec , Map , iteration 4 Models field options, documents, JSON, forms 5 JSON and forms the wire shapes both directions 6 Concurrency green threads, channels, select , timers 7 HTTP router, request, response, middleware, gzip, ranges, SSE 8 Middleware the chain, and the ones in the box 9 Database sqlite, postgres and MySQL, the ORM (filters, relations, pages), migrations, the query cache, Redis 10 Auth passwords, sessions, the middleware 11 Frontend: signals state , memo , effect , live bindings 12 Frontend: DOM and SSR el , attributes, keyed lists, parts, components, charts, the shelf 13 The browser bridge wasm, hydration, js() , the host, the boot block 14 The CLI every command and every flag, the language server (\u00a714d) and its VS Code client (\u00a714e) 15 Ops logs, metrics, /debug , limits, config 16 Limits and deviations what is not implemented, and what is not going to be Reading a section that says \"not implemented\". Those sentences are part of the contract. The framework would rather name a gap than leave you to find it in production."},{"t":"1. House rules","p":"reference/house-rules.html","g":"Reference","h":[],"b":"Reference \u00b7 docs/reference.md One way to do it. If two APIs do the same thing, one of them is a bug. No hidden allocation. Every allocation happens through wm_alloc or an explicit Arena* . There is no GC and no hidden copy. One noun, one macro. text(...) is markup, text(status, \u2026) is a response: a name means one thing, and a call site never has two of them to choose between. Compile-time over run-time. Model descriptors, route tables and validation are all built by macros the compiler checks; nothing is parsed from a config file at run time except webc.json (the CLI). The reason is next to the code. A non-obvious choice is explained where it is made, in the comment above it, and in the page of this reference that covers it."},{"t":"2. Core: strings, memory, errors","p":"reference/core-strings-memory-errors.html","g":"Reference","h":[],"b":"Reference \u00b7 docs/reference.md c Types: i8..i64 , u8..u64 , f32 , f64 , bool , str , Bytes . Memory. An arena is a chunked bump allocator; arena_reset is O(1) and keeps the memory. The framework installs one per scope \u2014 the process, the request, the render \u2014 so application code never creates or resets one. wm_alloc / wm_free is the raw heap with a 16-byte header, byte accounting ( wm_stats() ), and an optional hard limit ( wm_set_limit ); that is what the framework itself uses. Allocating arena memory with wm_free is a panic, on purpose. What to use in an app, instead of an arena: c There are two string types and they are both ordinary: Text is a char * that fmt() returns and that markup helpers accept, and str is a {ptr, len} slice that model fields, routes and get() on a state(Text, \u2026) use. Crossing between them is one macro each way \u2014 WC_AS_STR to go to a str , text_of or fmt_s to go to a Text \u2014 and every markup helper in \u00a712 already takes either, so class_(fmt(...)) needs nothing at all. The four spellings, and what happens when you pick the wrong one. Four types is the thing people trip over, and the trip is always the same: a value that is one type, a place that wants another, and a compiler message about a struct. This is the whole table, and the third column is the message you get \u2014 which is the part a cheat sheet usually leaves out: where what it accepts if you get it wrong text(...) , attr(...) , class_(...) , href(...) str , Text , const char * , a number, a bool (one value, no format) text(\"%s\", x) is a format: %s is replaced. text(\"%s\") with one argument prints %s \u2014 it is a value. text(\"%s \u2014 %s\", a, b) two or more arguments: the first is a printf format, the rest are values a % you mean literally is written %% here. With one argument there is no format at all, so text(\"50% off\") is exactly those bytes a model field declared (title, Text) a Text (a char * ) a str is passing 'str' to parameter of incompatible type 'char *' \u2014 write text_of(s) a model field declared (blurb, str) a str a Text is passing 'char *' to parameter of incompatible type 'str' \u2014 write WC_AS_STR(t) or str_lit(...) query(\"page\") , path(\"slug\") , req_header(...) , get(...) all return str assigning one to a Text field is the error above; query_text(\"page\") is the Text door fmt(\"\u2026\", \u2026) a str , a Text or a literal; every one of them prints correctly the result is a Text , and keeping it past its scope is the bug WEBC_STRICT_LIFETIME finds res_text(200, \u2026) , res_html(200, \u2026) , res_json* str (a literal is one) a Text needs WC_AS_STR(t) a Buf * ( &b ) the place that built it handing one where a str goes is passing 'Buf *' to parameter of incompatible type 'str' \u2014 buf_str(&b) json(200, str) bytes already encoded anything else in that position is a collection: use json_list(200, v) for a list The rule behind the table: anything that writes text takes any string ( str , Text or a literal), and a wc_*_c name means exactly const char * . The conversion is always one macro \u2014 WC_AS_STR in, text_of / fmt_s out \u2014 so the fix is mechanical once the error is read as \"wrong spelling\", which is what the third column is for. WEBC_STRICT_LIFETIME=1 mprotects a scope arena when it dies, so a Text used past its scope crashes in development instead of showing yesterday's bytes. Errors. c The message is stored in the error ( WErr.buf , up to WC_ERR_MSG_CAP bytes; longer messages are truncated and truncated is set). A WErr you keep past the arena that raised it still reads correctly, and copying the error copies the message. Read it with werr_msg(&e) \u2014 a str into the error, so the error must outlive it \u2014 or printf(\"%.*s\", WERR_FMT(e)) . detail is the exception: it is request context and is borrowed. errdefer 's \"did this block fail\" counter lives in the task , like the panic frame and the current arena, so a try() that fails before an await and the errdefer that runs after it agree even when the task resumed on another worker. panic(...) prints, then unwinds to the nearest wc_try_begin frame if one is installed \u2014 which is how the recover middleware turns a panic into a 500 instead of killing a worker. A 500 says where it happened. The body is still a flat {error, message} , and the message carries the request: internal error \u2014 GET /a/x (route /a/:slug, id 6f1c\u2026) . A fail(500, \u2026) keeps its own sentence in that place; a genuine panic stays internal error so a broken handler cannot leak its internals to a stranger. The route, the method and the id are not internals \u2014 they are what makes the report usable \u2014 and the same four fields open the log line and the crash report's first line, so a crash-*.txt names the request it crashed in."},{"t":"3. Collections","p":"reference/collections.html","g":"Reference","h":[],"b":"Reference \u00b7 docs/reference.md c The descriptor lives inside the vector, so a collection names its element type at run time: json(200, &todos) needs no model argument, and for_each needs no key expression. db_all(Todo) returns a VecTodo * built the same way. The five primitive collections are prelude types, and vec_new() speaks their spelling. VecI64 , VecI32 , VecF64 , VecStr and VecBytes are declared by the framework, so an empty collection of numbers is one call rather than a hand-declared type: c vec_new() builds its type name by pasting Vec onto its argument, which spells str as Vecstr \u2014 a type nobody declared, so the one call every empty list needs did not compile and apps wrote the three lines by hand. The paste now lands on the prelude's own type. The other direction is a redeclaration ( Vec(str) would declare Vecstr a second time), so a primitive collection is named , never re-declared: Vec(i64) is what webc lint reports. The argument is the collection, not its address. A value goes as &todos , a VecTodo * from db_all() goes as it is \u2014 one & too many is a VecTodo ** , and that reads the pointer's own bytes as a descriptor and kills the process inside the writer. json_list(200, v) is the spelling that cannot be got wrong: it reads v->desc and v->at , so a pointer-to-pointer is a compile error naming the type ( member reference base type 'VecTodo **' is not a structure ), and json() grew a magic word in the descriptor so a wrong argument is a 500 with a sentence instead of a crash. Querying is the first answer to \"which rows?\", and db_all_where() is how you ask. For the collection you already have \u2014 grouped by desk, most-read first \u2014 two macros, both keyed on a field so the compiler and the model descriptor check the name: c vec_where_str / _i64 / _bool return a new collection of the same type; the comparison follows the field's WKind , so a Text field compares as text and an i64 as a number. The pointer spellings \u2014 vlen(rows) , vat(rows, i) , and the typed walk each(f, rows) { \u2026 f->number \u2026 } \u2014 take the Vec * the caller actually holds, so the value forms' at((*rows), i) / len(*rows) dance disappears from list views."},{"t":"4. Models","p":"reference/models.html","g":"Reference","h":["4a. Documents: several collections under one wire shape"],"b":"Reference \u00b7 docs/reference.md c The macro generates the struct, a descriptor table ( Todo_modelof() ), the table name ( todos \u2014 snake_case plural, override with WEBC_MODEL_TABLE(Todo, \"todos2\") ), and works for all of: c Qualifiers (all designated initialisers, so the compiler checks them): qualifier meaning .pk = true primary key; an integer pk is assigned by the database .required = true must be present and non-empty .not_null = true same, spelled for rows rather than bodies .unique = true declared in generated SQL; enforced by the database .max = N / .min = N string length bounds .gte = X / .lte = X numeric bounds .format = \"email\" email , uuid , url , date .trim = true strip surrounding whitespace before validation .sensitive = true redacted by the logger .no_json = true never serialised .json_array = true a vector field serialises as an array of scalars .sql = \"column\" column name override .def = \"...\" SQL default expression .doc = \"...\" surfaced by webc doctor .null = true an integer column that is NULL-able; 0 is how this side spells NULL .references = \"media_assets(id)\" the FK target, written into the generated DDL Bare flags ( .required with no = true ) are not valid C; write .required = true . A column that can be absent says so once . .null is for an integer kind \u2014 the one kind whose zero is not a value of its own \u2014 and it means the three things a nullable foreign key needs at once: c the DDL carries references media_assets(id) and no not null ; db_save binds SQL NULL when the field is 0 , and reads NULL back as 0 ; the JSON encoder writes null (and accepts null on the way in only for this field); an empty form input is the browser's way of saying \"nothing chosen\", so it becomes 0 . .null on a f64 , an enum or a Text is a compile error, and so is .null together with .required or .pk : a float's 0 is a value, an enum's 0 is its first member, and a primary key that could be NULL is not one. The workaround this replaces was a hand-written table rebuild, because sqlite cannot drop a constraint \u2014 the app in the review had exactly that migration. A model holds up to 64 fields , which is the number of slots the preprocessor machinery ( WC_MAP_S_* , generated by tools/gen_pp.py ) has. It was 32, and the 33rd field reported \"pasting formed WC_MAP_S_( \" \u2014 a message about a macro, explained (confidently, wrongly) as a fault in the field it named. A row this wide is usually two tables: long prose belongs in its own row and its own screen. A closed set is a field, not a Text you validate by hand. WEBC_ENUM declares the set; a field of that type is an int in the struct and its name everywhere bytes are exchanged \u2014 JSON, forms, SQL text \u2014 and a request that sends a stranger is a 422 naming the field, not a row that silently renders nowhere: c The generated T_N , T_index() , T_ok() and T_name() are all compile-checked against the declaration; an integer column still reads (as its index). A row you computed is still a row. WEBC_ROW declares a shape for JSON, a vector and a view, with no table behind it \u2014 the KPI, the dashboard line, the \"count by referrer\" that every admin page builds out of a select : c It is WEBC_MODEL + WEBC_MODEL_INTERNAL , which is what the schema check already skips, so the only difference is the name: db_save on a WEBC_ROW panics with a sentence instead of sending an insert at a table that does not exist. Without it, a computed row is either a WEBC_MODEL that makes webc check report drift forever, or a hand-written Vec of a struct the JSON encoder cannot see. Two models and a key between them is a relation, and it is declared next to the fields it names \u2014 WEBC_MODEL_REF(Story, section, Section, slug) and the reverse WEBC_MODEL_MANY(Section, stories, Story, section) . That declaration is the only place the foreign-key column is written; prefetch reads it, ref() / refs() read the links, and db_save_all fills the key. \u00a79a2 and \u00a79a3 are the whole story; the guide The ORM is the walk through. 4a. Documents: several collections under one wire shape A model is a row; a document is the shape a live page sends \u2014 several collections and scalars in one JSON object, one declaration for both directions: c Field types may be models, vectors of models, vectors of scalars, enums (by name) or the scalar kinds \u2014 everything the model codec checks applies. On the client, a document arrives through a store (\u00a711b): WStore owns the two generations a streaming page needs, and wc_doc_join() links relations (below) after a read. Relations inside a document. WEBC_MODEL_REF(Card, assignee, User, id) declares the link as always; on the server prefetch() fills it in one query; on a client that decoded a document, the join is in-memory: c Where a model stores an ISO timestamp as text (the common case; asking the app to parse it into milliseconds first is what makes every app carry its own calendar), the shared half has the patterns: c \"2026-10-11\" and \"2026-10-11T09:40:00Z\" both parse; a bad value gives \"\" and never panics. The pattern language is %Y %m %d %e %b %B %a %A %H %M %S %% \u2014 %e is the day without a leading zero, %B / %A the long names. fmt_date() is the same for a time_t . c All of them take a str , a Text or a C string and return a str (except read_minutes , which returns an i32 ). They live in <webc/url.h> , which shared.h includes, so the server and the browser half spell a date the same way \u2014 the alternative was a month table in each, which is what The Meridian had. The codec links in the browser half too \u2014 it always did; the comment that claimed otherwise cost real apps a hand-mirrored decoder whose field names no compiler checked. The browser-half spellings zero out first, so a partial document cannot leave a stale field behind: c"},{"t":"5. JSON and forms","p":"reference/json-and-forms.html","g":"Reference","h":[],"b":"Reference \u00b7 docs/reference.md JsonWriter (streaming, depth-bounded) for output, JVal (tree, depth-bounded) for input. The parser refuses input deeper than WC_JSON_MAX_DEPTH (256) instead of growing the stack. Unknown keys on input are ignored; a field that fails validation comes back as a WErrs list of {field, message} . Writing a response: you have write why one row json(200, Todo, &t) a Todo * carries nothing to recover the model from, so name it a collection json_list(200, rows) the descriptor is in the struct, and ->desc is checked by the compiler bytes that are already JSON json(200, some_str) a tree rendered elsewhere, or another service's payload an error res_error(422, \"bad_email\", \"\u2026\") a flat {error, message} an app can act on json(200, rows) also works for a collection \u2014 it is the same bytes \u2014 but the argument goes through void * , so a slip is a runtime answer ( 500 json_not_a_vector , one log line) where json_list makes it a compile error. Writing a document by hand \u2014 a payload in a shape no model has, or a fragment for someone else's API \u2014 is the writer, and its two costs are brackets and empty strings: c jw_obj_in / jw_arr_in are the el(...) pattern applied to the writer: the block closes itself, break and return included. jw_kv_* skips the key and the value when the value is absent, because \"datePublished\":\"\" is not a date \u2014 it is a claim a reader has to guess about. jw_str escapes JSON; it does not escape HTML, and the next section is why that distinction has a name."},{"t":"6. Concurrency","p":"reference/concurrency.html","g":"Reference","h":[],"b":"Reference \u00b7 docs/reference.md c wm_* primitives: WMutex , WRwLock , WOnce , WWaitGroup , and wfd_* for non-blocking I/O that parks the task rather than the thread. webc_serve runs an accept task per listener and one task per connection; blocking I/O inside a handler parks only that task. Cancellation is a value, not a goroutine: c A request carries one ( webc_req_ctx() ), cancelled when the client disconnects or the server drains. App-wide values live in a named-slot table: c Keep-alive channels, select and the Mutex / RwLock / Once / WaitGroup names from the spec are all spelled out in task.h ( mutex_lock , rwlock_rlock , once_do , waitgroup_wait , Chan(T) / chan_new / chan_send / chan_recv ). Stack size is 256 KiB per task and not configurable at run time. There is no guard page (see limits ); a runaway recursion faults instead of being diagnosed."},{"t":"7. HTTP","p":"reference/http.html","g":"Reference","h":["7b. The page head","7b1. A page is a value: head() and WPage","7b2. &lt;html&gt; and &lt;body&gt;","7c. Cookies","7d. A stream that stays correct: the SSE pattern","7e. A file, and the part of it the client asked for"],"b":"Reference \u00b7 docs/reference.md c Routes are declared at file scope (a C function cannot be defined inside a function), GET/POST/PUT/PATCH/DELETE/HEAD/OPTIONS/ANY , with _MW variants that attach per-route middleware. Matching order is static segment > :param > *wildcard ; HEAD falls back to GET ; a trailing slash is not a different route; a known path with the wrong method is 405, not 404. Request helpers: path(name) and query_text(name) are Text \u2014 the one string type an app names; path_i64(name) , query_i64(name, d) , req_query_bool(name, d) are the numbers they stand for. Then query(name) (the raw str , for a value that may be binary), header(name) , cookie(name) , req->body , req->peer , req->request_id , req->arena . Responses \u2014 one spelling each, and text / html take a C string or a Text alike: call what it sends json(status, Model, ptr) one row as JSON json(status, collection) / json_list(status, collection) a collection as JSON; json_list is the spelling the compiler checks json(status, text) a body you already built text(status, s) / html(status, s) text/plain / text/html page(View, ctx) , page_with(status, View, ctx) a view inside the shell res_error(status, code, msg) an error body in the shape the client expects redirect(url) 303 with a Location res_file , res_download , res_bytes , res_stream , res_sse , res_upgrade the rest res_cookie , res_header(_str) a header on the response The last line of a mutation route, either way a browser asks it \u2014 a form post gets its redirect back to the page, a JSON call gets the row it changed: c Bodies: TRY_BODY(Model, var) parses JSON, TRY_FORM(Model, var) parses application/x-www-form-urlencoded ; both validate with the same code the browser would run, and return 422 with {error, message, status} on a failure. Typed extractors take the model's name and fail the request on bad input (400 invalid_body for malformed JSON, 422 invalid_form for a form that parses but does not validate \u2014 the message names the field): c A route can be named with a second argument, and then a URL is built from the pattern instead of spelled again \u2014 url_to() fills :params from (name, value) pairs, link_to(Model, row) from the model's own route. A name that is not registered, or a parameter the pattern wants and the call did not pass, panics with the route table rather than producing a 404 a reader finds: c A page's context is filled in by field name when the view names its type with view(Name, CtxType *c) , or declare_view(Name, CtxType); in a header \u2014 the same view and its context type in one line, which is how a route in another file names it too. Every name is then checked by the compiler \u2014 a typo is field designator 'sectoin' does not refer to any field in type 'SectionPage_ctx'; did you mean 'section'? \u2014 and the route reads as prose: c must(ptr, msg\u2026) answers 404 with that sentence when the pointer is null (the whole route, not a paragraph); must_with(status, ptr, msg\u2026) for another status. render_with(status, Name, .field = \u2026) is the same page with a status. page(View, ctx) stays for the one-value case ( page(Notes, db_all(Note)) ). Route groups are a runtime scope (routes self-register before main , so a lexical block cannot wrap them): c An outbound client (dogfooded by webc get ): c Framing is Connection: close plus chunked decoding; redirects are returned unless you ask for them; TLS needs -DWEBC_TLS=openssl (\u00a716). Testing without a socket: Response r = webc_test_request(\"GET\", \"/users/1\"); 7b. The page head page() splices a view into the <div id=\"root\"> of an HTML file, so the <head> is the file's \u2014 but the title, the canonical URL and the social cards are per page. These fill the head of the response being built; the splice happens on the way out and before anything compresses the body (\u00a78b), so it works for page() , for html() , and for a document you assemble by hand \u2014 and it reaches a browser that sent Accept-Encoding: gzip , which is all of them: c A <title> the shell already has is replaced where it stands (the file keeps its shape); everything else is appended at the end of <head> . Text is escaped once, on the way in. A response with no </head> \u2014 a fragment, JSON \u2014 is left alone. head_ld() is the one exception to \"text is escaped on the way in\", and it exists because a <script> body is two languages at once. JSON's own escaping does not escape < , so a value containing </script> closes the element and the rest of the document is read as markup \u2014 with whatever nonce the framework splices into every <script> on the way out. head_ld() escapes the document for the context ( < , > , & , U+2028/U+2029 as \\uXXXX ) and writes nothing at all for an empty document, because an empty block is invalid rather than neutral. Never build one with head_html(fmt(\"\u2026\")) : webc lint names that line. 7b1. A page is a value: head() and WPage Five head_* calls in a row are five chances to forget one, and the reader's theme is a sixth thing to remember per route. A page context that begins with WPage gets all of it from render() : c A project may keep its headers in folders \u2014 one per concern \u2014 and it may keep them apart from the sources too ( src/models/people.h , src/views/admin/editor.h ). Every .h under src/ is the project's for the engine, whatever folder it is in: WEBC_MODEL , Vec(T) and declare_view are found in all of them, and a #include \"app.h\" in a nested file resolves because -I src is on every compile line. head(...) is a compound literal, so every name in it is checked by the compiler. render() , page() and page_with() all call webc_page_begin(&ctx) before the view runs: it fills page.env from the request and records the head, which is spliced into the document before anything compresses it (\u00a78b) \u2014 the one-value page(View, ctx) form included, which is what makes the two spellings the same page: page.head becomes title <title> , and og:title unless og_title is given ( og_title = \"\" suppresses it) desc <meta name=\"description\"> \u2014 skipped when empty og_type , og_image , og_site og:type , og:image , og:site_name section_name <meta name=\"article:section\"> canonical , alternate <link rel=\"canonical\"> , <link rel=\"alternate\"> page.env (filled, read in the view) theme the theme cookie when it is light or dark , otherwise \"\" dark theme == \"dark\" path this request's path \u2014 where a form should send the reader back to query ?q= as text, \"\" when absent A theme in env.theme is also written as data-theme on <html> , so the first byte is already the right colour. The types live in <webc/page.h> (shared), because the context is compiled for both targets and a view may read c->page.env.dark ; only filling it needs a request. A page with no title logs one line per route, because a document that is wrong in the tab is otherwise silent. The head_* functions still exist for a response you assemble by hand \u2014 webc_page_begin(&page) with a local WPage is the same thing without render() . 7b2. <html> and <body> A page's document element is per request too: a site that server-renders the reader's theme needs the attribute in the markup before the first byte , or the page flashes the wrong edition. html_attr() and body_attr() splice into <html \u2026> and <body \u2026> on the way out, the same way the head does, so an app never has to assemble a whole document by hand: c 7c. Cookies res_cookie(res, name, value, attrs) adds Path=/ , HttpOnly and SameSite=Lax for you, and Secure on a TLS request. attrs is extra attributes ( Max-Age=60 , SameSite=Strict , \u2026); an attribute you name yourself replaces the default instead of following it, so nothing appears twice on the wire. c 7d. A stream that stays correct: the SSE pattern res_sse(fn, ctx) hands the response body to fn , which owns a socket for as long as it runs. Three facts shape every correct stream, and none of them is obvious: c The client half is webc_subscribe(\"/api/stream\", \"sync\", on_frame) ; a frame the bridge does not know by name (the ping above) is ignored, which is what heartbeats want. Decode frames into a private generation arena and persist() anything that must outlive the frame \u2014 the pattern is \u00a713d's WLive , spelled out by hand. Presence is the subscriber list itself; a stale entry leaves when its next write fails, which the heartbeat bounds to one interval. A stream parks its task on the channel and on writes; the stall monitor does not count it (it is a streamed response, not a stuck handler). 7e. A file, and the part of it the client asked for res_file(status, path) is a complete HTTP file response, not a path and a length. It answers the four questions a file has: is this the copy you have (304), all of it or part (200/206), where does it end (416), and may I resume ( Accept-Ranges: bytes ). c text What it decides, and why each one is not optional: request answer Range: bytes=0-99 206, Content-Range: bytes 0-99/907 bytes=800- / bytes=-100 206, to the end / the last 100 bytes bytes=0-99999 206, clamped to the last byte \u2014 not an error bytes=907- (a 907-byte file) 416 with Co"},{"t":"8. Middleware","p":"reference/middleware.html","g":"Reference","h":["8a. The policy is a value: mw_headers()","8b. Order: the document is finished before anything compresses it","8c. A 404 that is a page","gzip"],"b":"Reference \u00b7 docs/reference.md c Built in: mw_logging , mw_request_id , mw_headers(HeadersCfg) (the CSP, nosniff, \u2026; mw_headers_default() is mw_headers((HeadersCfg){0}) and its policy is default-src 'self'; img-src 'self' data:; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; script-src 'self' 'nonce-<per request>' 'wasm-unsafe-eval' \u2014 the nonce is put on every <script> / <style> of an HTML response as it is written, and wasm-unsafe-eval is there because without it the browser refuses to instantiate the module mw_web_assets serves), mw_cors(&(CorsCfg){...}) , mw_body_limit(bytes) , mw_timeout_ms(ms) , mw_auth_bearer(token) , mw_recover , mw_static(prefix, dir) , mw_static_spa(prefix, dir) , mw_metrics , mw_dev(port) , mw_compress() , mw_document() and mw_not_found(file) (both below). 8a. The policy is a value: mw_headers() The default CSP is one string with three parts, and a project that needs a CDN in img-src would otherwise splice the header by hand. It is a struct, and webc.json reaches the same struct: c json One policy, not two. webc_main() installs mw_headers() from the csp block after webc_boot() , so the framework's copy is inside the app's chain and \u2014 on the way out \u2014 writes first. A project that registers its own mw_headers() therefore replaces the framework's default rather than stacking beside it: webc_install_default_middleware() skips any default whose .name the app already registered (\u00a78b). Without that rule the HeadersCfg above would be built, applied, and then overwritten by the policy from webc.json \u2014 a CDN list in C that never reached a browser. img_src and script_src replace the default lists ( script_src without a nonce entry means the nonce is not offered, which is what an app with no inline script wants), extra is appended verbatim, and no_default says the app writes the header itself \u2014 the framework then emits none. A handler may still choose the policy for its own response with res_header() ; mw_headers() defers to a policy that is already there. The devtools panel. WebC.devtools() (or ?__webc_devtools on the URL) opens a small panel in the corner of the page: the bridge's own numbers \u2014 ops, bad ops, hydrated, anchors, created elements \u2014 refreshed live, plus WebC.why() as a mode you arm and click. It is dev.js, not a bridge verb: nothing an app ships knows it exists. webc dev widens the policy that is in force rather than building one of its own, so whatever the app declared \u2014 in C or in webc.json \u2014 is what a browser sees in development too, with the reload additions ( 'unsafe-inline' , ws://localhost:<port> , dev.connect ). That distinction is worth a sentence, because a dev server that builds its own policy from scratch makes an img-src with a CDN in it hold in production and silently stopped holding under webc dev . The dev server widens whatever policy is in force with what live reload needs ( 'unsafe-inline' , ws://localhost:<port> , the origins in dev.connect ), so a strict policy costs nothing in development. res_header_get(res, \"Content-Security-Policy\") reads one back, which is what an app that rewrites its own needs; wc_csp_build() is the builder on its own, for a project that emits the header outside a response. 8b. Order: the document is finished before anything compresses it mw_document() is the framework's own last-installed (innermost) middleware, and it exists because of a bug worth understanding before changing the chain. A page's <head> \u2014 the title, the description, the OG tags \u2014 is collected in the request while the view runs and spliced into the body on the way out. When that splice ran at the socket, mw_compress() had already turned the body into gzip bytes; searching compressed bytes for </head> finds nothing, so the splice silently did nothing for every client that sends Accept-Encoding: gzip \u2014 which is every browser \u2014 while curl without that header got the head and every smoke test passed. So the splice happens while the body is still HTML: mw_document() runs inside the router's response, before mw_dev appends the reload script and before gzip. Two rules follow: Register it last , so it wraps the route: `webc_use(mw_compress()); webc_use(mw_document()); ...routes...`. Anything that appends to an HTML body goes outside it. mw_dev does: the reload script is added after the head is applied, and it carries the page's nonce itself because the policy names the nonce and the dev script is the framework's own inline script. A project rarely writes the chain at all: webc_main() calls webc_install_default_middleware() after webc_boot() (compress, dev, request id, headers, logging, recover, body limit, web assets, static, document, 404), so webc_boot() registers only what it adds. A default is skipped when the app registered one with the same .name \u2014 the singleton rule of \u00a78a \u2014 so \"my own logging\" or \"my own policy\" is one webc_use() in webc_boot() and never a second log line or a second header. static and the rest are additive: /public and /uploads are different files, so both are installed. 8c. A 404 that is a page The default answer to a URL nobody routed is a JSON error. A project that wants a real page for a browser names the file once: json mw_not_found() then answers a 404 that accepts HTML with the file's markup inside the app's own shell \u2014 title, theme and nonce included \u2014 and leaves everything else alone: a route that answered 404 with HTML keeps its own page, and a client that sent Accept: application/json still gets the JSON error. A file that is a whole document ( <html \u2026> ) is served as it is. gzip On by default. mw_compress() is the first middleware the framework installs ( install_default_middleware ), so every response the client said it accepts goes out as Content-Encoding: gzip \u2014 static files and rendered bodies: a JSON API shrinks by 70%, a server-rendered page by 60-80%, a wasm module by 58%. WEBC_NO_GZIP=1 turns it off, for an app that ships its own compressed bytes or sits behind something that compresses. It is a normal middleware, so an app may take it out of the default chain and put it where it wants \u2014 in front of the middlewares that answer, so it wraps their response: c What it will not do, and why: Compress what gzip does not help. Only text/* , JSON, JavaScript, XML, wasm and SVG are eligible \u2014 not text/event-stream , not PNG, not woff2. A file under WC_GZIP_MIN (1 KB) is left alone: the 18-byte wrapper costs more than it saves. Compress a 206. A byte range is answered from the disk as the identity bytes (\u00a77e): Content-Range counts bytes of the representation the client asked about, so compressing it would both lose the offsets and read a whole file into memory to serve its first kilobyte. 206 and 416 are skipped exactly like 204 / 304 . Decide by itself whether the client wants it. No Accept-Encoding , gzip;q=0 or identity gets the bytes as they are. Vary: Accept-Encoding goes on every response that could have been compressed, so a shared cache never crosses the two. Reuse a validator across the two encodings. The gzipped representation carries <etag>-gz , so If-None-Match from a gzip response is answered with a 304, and one from an identity response is not answered with a gzip 304. Compress a file twice. Files are cached by (path, size, mtime) \u2014 32 of them by default \u2014 so a 155 KB module is compressed once and served from memory after that. mw_compress_opts((CompressOpts){.level = 6, .slots = 8}) tunes it; level 9 (default) is the smallest bytes, and it is zlib doing it, so it matches gzip -9 . Because it is first, it is the last word on every body: whatever a middleware or a route wrote is what gets compressed. That is also why the page's <head> is spliced inside it (\u00a78b) \u2014 a splice after this one searches gzip bytes and finds nothing. webc serve and webc dev both serve through it (\u00a713b), so the bytes you measure in a browser are the bytes that ship. The -lz on every link line is this feature and only this feature: webc build adds it to the app's link, because the framework's own default chain references the middleware. zlib is on macOS and on every Linux worth deploying to; a platform without it is a platform where WEBC_NO_GZIP=1 does not help, and the fix is a -lz -shaped one. Global middleware runs for unmatched paths too \u2014 that is how static files and the dev reload script exist without being routes. A chain carries up to WC_MW_MAX (32) middlewares, globally, per group and per route, and the dispatcher runs every one of them \u2014 the same number in both places, because a registered middleware that never runs is the worst kind of silent failure. Going over the limit prints one line naming WC_MW_MAX instead of dropping the extra one. The framework's own defaults use ten of the thirty-two ( compress , dev , request_id , headers , logging , recover , web-assets , static twice, document , and not_found when webc.json names a file), so an app registers into an empty argument, not a race."},{"t":"9. Database","p":"reference/database.html","g":"Reference","h":["9a. A filter you can read, and one row by one field","9a2. Relations: one declaration, one query, no query in the view","9a3. The other direction: a section's stories, still one query","9a4. Questions about a relation, a page, and a whole tree","9a5. The third dialect: MySQL and MariaDB","9b. Fixtures and schema drift","9b2. The migration the models imply: webc migrate diff","9b3. The CRUD every table needs: webc scaffold","9c. Redis: queues, counters, pub/sub, a lock","9d. The query cache: results in Redis, invalidated by generation"],"b":"Reference \u00b7 docs/reference.md Three drivers open from one URL \u2014 sqlite (a path), postgres, and MySQL/MariaDB (\u00a79a5). Handles below are WDB * ; the statements are the raw layer. The ORM is \u00a79a to \u00a79a4 and it is what an app should be written in: a typed filter, one row, a relation, a page, a tree of rows. Raw SQL is for the statement that is a report, not for the tenth select * where id = ? . c Lists come back in the order you ask for \u2014 db_all() has no opinion of its own, which made a front page look right on sqlite and wrong somewhere else: c :name placeholders are rewritten to ? (sqlite) or $n (postgres and MySQL); the same code runs on all three. The rewrite lexes: a colon inside a string literal, a quoted identifier or a comment is left alone, so to_char(now(), 'HH24:MI:SS') survives. db_insert(db, T, &t, &id) reports the new key from every driver: sqlite from its rowid, postgres because the statement asks with returning , MySQL from the OK packet \u2014 which is the returning capability in \u00a79a5, not three code paths in the app. db_all(db, Model) returns Vec<Model> * . A transaction belongs to a session, and a session to a request. The first statement a request makes pins one connection for that task; every later statement \u2014 and any transaction \u2014 runs on it, and the connection goes back to the pool when the request ends, even if the handler panicked. Two requests can therefore never sit inside each other's BEGIN / COMMIT : when they can, a rollback undoes another request's committed insert. c tx_scope(...) { \u2026 } takes a block. A nested scope is a SAVEPOINT , so an inner failure can roll back alone ( tx_rollback_here() ), and committing a scope that is not open is a failure with a message in WTx::err , not a silent false : a commit that quietly does not commit is how a write vanishes. tx_begin(db) / db_begin -style handles remain for code that must end a transaction in a different function than it started. A task that ends inside a transaction has it rolled back and its connection returned (a warning names the task): fail() and a panic both unwind past the scope's cleanup. Migrations: migrations_apply(&(WMigrate){ .db = db, .dir = \"migrations\" }) , files applied in filename order, recorded in schema_migrations . A migration is SQL, so it carries the dialect of one database: postgres needs bigserial where sqlite wants integer primary key autoincrement . Put the other spelling next to it as 0001_todos.postgres.sql (or .sqlite.sql ); it is used instead of 0001_todos.sql when present, and the same name is recorded, so one sequence of migrations serves every dialect \u2014 .sqlite.sql , .postgres.sql , .mysql.sql . examples/todo and examples/accounts carry all three spellings. A file may carry several statements: a query with no parameters goes out on the server's simple protocol, which is the one that takes them, so nothing about the file depends on the driver. A migration's column type follows how the field is stored , not how it reads. An enum field ( RPlan , RKind ) is stored as its name \u2014 \"balanced\" , \"stake\" \u2014 so its column is text , never integer ; a bool is boolean (or tinyint(1) on MySQL). SQLite accepts a name in an integer column and will not tell you, which is how an app can have a postgres migration that has never once run: invalid input syntax for type integer: \"balanced\" . Money stays integer cents, and an indexed text column is varchar(190) where MySQL needs a length. The pool opens connections on demand, up to WC_DB_POOL (default: the number of processors, at most 256), and a task waiting for one parks rather than spinning the CPU. Waiting is FIFO and bounded: after db_pool_config(max, acquire_ms) milliseconds ( WC_DB_ACQUIRE_MS , default 5000) the request is refused with a message that names the pool size instead of hanging until the client gives up. db_pool_size(db) reports what is open. The postgres driver does not block a thread. Its socket I/O parks like every other fd in the runtime, so a slow query holds a connection, not a processor: 64 concurrent select pg_sleep(0.3) finish in one wave rather than in batches of nproc . A statement also stops when its request is cancelled \u2014 the session carries req->ctx , and the driver sends a protocol CancelRequest for the backend, so mw_timeout_ms() can answer 504 without waiting for a query it already gave up on. That connection is then closed and its slot recycled; a lost connection no longer poisons the pool. TLS to postgres follows libpq: ?sslmode=disable|prefer|require|verify-ca|verify-full (default prefer , or WEBC_PG_SSLMODE ), with sslrootcert= for the verify modes. A build without TLS refuses require at startup instead of sending a password in the clear; managed postgres, which rejects plaintext clients, is the reason this exists. A null handle is a refusal, not a crash: db_insert_into(nullptr, \u2026) and the model helpers return an error naming DATABASE_URL instead of dereferencing it. 9a. A filter you can read, and one row by one field Asking for one row by a field the long way round is a query built only to carry the value: c Nothing checks the field name, nothing checks the operator, and you have to know who owns the query's arena. Three spellings replace it, one per shape of question. One field, one value. The field is checked against the model \u2014 a typo panics and lists the fields that exist instead of returning no rows \u2014 and the value is always a bound parameter: c The value may be a str , a const char * or an integer; a string literal goes through str_lit() ( _Generic does not decay an array). Each takes a WDB * first when you are not using the default handle: db_by(db, Todo, \"id\", 3) . More than one condition. WWhere names the field in the macro, so the compiler checks it, and the primary key column comes from the model, not from a string: c ww_or_begin() / ww_contains() / ww_or_end() make one question out of several columns instead of filtering the whole table in memory: c db_increment(Story, st, views) adds one to one column in one statement \u2014 the alternative, st->views += 1; db_save(Story, st); , rewrites the whole row (body and all) to change a counter. Set WEBC_TRACE_DB=1 to log every statement with its row count and duration. 9a2. Relations: one declaration, one query, no query in the view A story has a section, and the section is a row. Declare it next to the models: c Fetch the whole list once and link it: c Read it in the view, server or browser half, with no query at all: c The links belong to the request (they live as long as its arena does), and prefetch is one select \u2026 where slug in (?, ?, ?) however long the page is. A relation nobody prefetched reads as null rather than issuing a query per row \u2014 the N+1 that is invisible until the page is slow. WEBC_MODEL_REF is pure data, so it belongs in the shared header with the models; only prefetch needs a database. prefetch_on(db, vec, T, field) is the same on a handle you own (a test). Postgres speaks protocol v3 with SCRAM-SHA-256 authentication; there is no libpq. It is tested against a real server: make pg-test starts a throwaway postgres in docker, runs tests/db_parity_test.c (the same story through every driver it can reach, compared field by field) and both example suites against it, then removes the container. make mysql-test is the same for MySQL 8 (\u00a79a5). Without docker but with a server, set WEBC_PG_URL . An app's own tests run on postgres with WEBC_TEST_DB=postgres://\u2026 webc test . 9a3. The other direction: a section's stories, still one query WEBC_MODEL_REF answers \"what is this row's section\" with one row. The reverse question has its own declaration, because it is a different question with a different result: c The second line names the same relation, read backwards, and generates a typed accessor \u2014 refs(Section, sec, stories) returns a VecStory * . Prefetch it for the page the way you prefetch the single direction: c That is one statement for the sections and one for all their stories \u2014 not one per section. A page of 2 000 sections chunks the owner keys in groups of 512, so the parameter count never runs into a server's ceiling. A collection nobody prefetched reads as empty , and logs relation read without a prefetch once, naming the model and the field: an empty list that was never fetched is the failure that looks like an empty section, and silence about it is how a page loses its rows. Hops chain left to right \u2014 each one is a query on the rows the previous hop found: c Three hops is the limit; deeper is a loop in the app, because a path string is a second query language nobody asked for. 9a4. Questions about a relation, a page, and a whole tree Filters reach through a relation without changing the shape of the result (so the rows still read back into models), sort by a related column, and aggregate: c An aggregate answers over the conditions : an order by and a limit in the same filter are dropped, because a sum of the first page is a different question and the wrong answer looks plausible. A list"},{"t":"10. Auth","p":"reference/auth.html","g":"Reference","h":[],"b":"Reference \u00b7 docs/reference.md Nine functions, one header, no session table. c Two flavours on purpose: a model field and a form value are Text ( const char * ), so auth_hash / auth_verify are what an app calls; auth_hash_password / _iters take a str for code that already holds a pointer and a length. The stored value is self-describing \u2014 pbkdf2-sha256$<iters>$<salt-b64>$<hash-b64> , 16-byte salt, 210 000 iterations by default \u2014 so a raised cost is a new hash and an old one still verifies. The cookie is <uid>.<expiry>.<hmac> , HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax , named by WEBC_SESSION_COOKIE ( \"session\" ), valid WEBC_SESSION_TTL_S (604800) seconds. Signed, not encrypted: the id and the expiry are readable and cannot be changed without breaking the HMAC, which is what makes a session table unnecessary. A deleted account is a lookup that returns nothing, so a handler that uses the id also checks the row. auth_secret() resolves once per process, first match wins: auth_secret_set(value) (what a test pins), WEBC_SECRET , the dev loop's data/.webc-secret file, then a random per-process secret with a LOG_WARN that sessions will not survive a restart . auth_secret_set is also the only way to make cookies from two processes agree. examples/accounts is the worked app (signup, login, logout, a guarded page, and a test suite that mints the cookie and sends it back); the guide is the accounts guide ."},{"t":"11. Frontend: signals","p":"reference/frontend-signals.html","g":"Reference","h":["11a. What re-renders a view (and what does not)","11b. The store: frames in, generations out","Per-instance signals","11c. Per-row state: state_own","11d. Component instances: comp()","11e. Children: WKids"],"b":"Reference \u00b7 docs/reference.md c Writing the same value does not re-run effects (byte comparison). Effects run in topological order, once per batch. signal , computed and effect declare file-scope statics (they define functions), so they do not work inside a function body. That makes them one per translation unit : right for app-wide state, wrong for a view that appears twice on a page. For that there is the _in family below, and for a list there is state_own (\u00a711c). Three declarations, one rule between them \u2014 what is the state one of? declaration one value per you reach it with fits state(T, n, init) page (translation unit) get(n) / set(n, v) the app's own state state_in(T, n, init) WInst you create get_in(&inst, n) , or get(n) inside with_inst a view mounted N times by your code state_own(T, n, init) render position get(n) / set(n, v) a row of a list, a mounted view's own copy 11a. What re-renders a view (and what does not) The one thing every client author asks first, because the answer is not \"the signal\": A render follows every action and every host callback. The bridge re-renders each mounted view \u2014 and diffs it \u2014 when control returns to it: after a click, after a typed field, after a webc_later timer fires, after a fetch or an SSE frame settles, after a push_url -driven nav. Writing a signal does not by itself schedule a render; the flush point is the bridge boundary. So an action never has to \"touch the view\": c There is no tick signal to bump, and a set(tick, get(tick) + 1) is a write nobody reads \u2014 the render would have happened anyway. What a render costs is a different question: the whole view runs again, so keep expensive work out of it \u2014 that is what \u00a712b2's jobs are for. The exception is the one \u00a712j adds: a node the view declared as live ( text_live , attr_live , class_live , show_live ) is written in place when its signal changes, and an action marked action_live skips the render entirely. Everything structural \u2014 if , for_each , part , markup() , a new node \u2014 still needs the render. webc check --wiring also names the trap this rule creates for lists \u2014 a live binding on a page-wide signal inside a for_each (every row then shows the same value, which is what state_own in \u00a711c is for) \u2014 and it knows state_own , so the never-read rule below still applies to it. webc check --wiring notes a signal that a write feeds and nothing else reads, because that is exactly what a leftover tick looks like: set(tick, get(tick) + 1) reads the signal it writes, which is why a plain \"never read\" rule misses the idiom the framework teaches ( set(tick, get(tick) + 1) ). The check counts a read that happens on a line with no write of the same signal, so the self-feeding write is not mistaken for a reader. 11b. The store: frames in, generations out A live document arrives as frames (a seed, then a stream), and each frame's strings must die when the frame two later replaces it. WStore owns that rotation \u2014 the ~100 lines every streaming client would otherwise write first: c store_cur() is the live document between frames, store_arena() the generation it lives in; store_free() returns everything. The server twin is WFrame (\u00a77d). Per-instance signals The same declarations, but the state lives in a WInst you own, so the same view can exist any number of times: c get / set reach the instance bound by with_inst , and panic outside one: a per-instance read that cannot know which copy it means is a bug, not a default. Where there is no ambient instance \u2014 an action, a test, the other card \u2014 name it: get_in(&inst, count) . There is no second spelling: get reads a signal whether it lives in the process or in an instance, and picks the right one.` Instances are values. winst_init(&x, nullptr) is heap-backed and winst_free returns every byte; winst_init(&x, arena) allocates from the arena and needs no free, but must not outlive it. CELL(T) + cell_init / cget / cset is still there for one signal inside a struct you already have. WInst is for a whole view's worth of state. 11c. Per-row state: state_own A list is where local state lives \u2014 a row's open flag, a row's draft text, a row's quantity \u2014 and a row has no name to hang a WInst on. state_own is the declaration whose owner is the position that drew the node : the for_each row, or the mounted view. (A view rendered into an email is a third \u2014 one render, its own copy, discarded after \u2014 so a view with per-row state can be both a page and a mail part.) c Identity is (position, declaration) . Two rows are two values; the same view mounted twice is two values, with no plumbing at all. A row that moves in a keyed list keeps its value, because the position is the key. A live binding may target it. text_live , attr_live , class_live and show_live all take a state_own name \u2014 one write, one op, the row the action belongs to and no other. This is the part state_in cannot do (a WInst signal is refused, \u00a712j) and the part that makes a 1 600-row table interactive. An action resolves it through the node it came from. The bridge knows which element the event carried a hook for, and pushes that element's owner before the action runs; so get(qty) means \"the row of the button that was clicked\", not \"the row rendered last\". get_N() / set_N() are the same thing spelled out, for code that is inside a row body. Lazily created, disposed with the position. A row that never reads its slot allocates nothing; the keyed sweep, wcomp_destroy and the component's own render kill the slots with the row. Cost when it is used: ~184 B per live row ( WInst + one node per declaration), so a 20 000-row table with per-row state is still the wrong shape (\u00a716.8). Reading it outside a position panics , naming the declaration \u2014 the same rule as state_in : a per-position read with no position cannot know which copy it means. state(\u2026) is the declaration for \"one value, anywhere in the page\". The server half is unchanged. SSR draws the declaration's initial values and registers nothing, so hydration is byte-for-byte what it was. state_own and state_in answer different questions and both can be live in one app: a position resolves first ( state_own ), and with no position in scope with_inst answers ( state_in ). toggle(name) flips a flag in place \u2014 set(x, get(x) ? 0 : 1) written out is an inversion the reader decodes; the macro is the name of the thing. And state(T_, N_, \u2026) is generic: state(bool, ahead, false) works, and a flag that is a boolean reads as one in an if instead of carrying a comment that says which integers mean what. 11d. Component instances: comp() state_own is one value per render position , and a position is a mounted view or a for_each row. That leaves the unit a component author actually writes \u2014 the call \u2014 without one: a PART is a plain C function, so calling it pushes no position and two calls of one part in the same view shared one slot. comp() says which call is which: c Identity is (the position that drew it, the call site) . The call site is a __COUNTER__ captured where comp() was written, so the same call on the next render finds the same instance, two calls never share one, a row that moves in a keyed list keeps its components, and a branch that stops drawing one disposes it. Nothing is allocated for a component that asks for nothing. The instance is created the first time a state_own / computed_own is read or an effect_own is started, and killed when the position is swept (the sweep is the keyed list's, keyed by call site). Everything downstream already resolves the instance , because it resolves the node's owner: text_live / attr_live / class_live / show_live , bind_str , input_hook , and every action ( get(qty) in an action means \"the instance of the button that was clicked\", exactly as it means \"the row\" inside a for_each ). It is opt-in. A part called without comp() keeps the older rule \u2014 it draws in the position that called it, so its state_own is shared with every other call in that position. That is what makes the change additive, and webc check --wiring points at the parts where it matters: a part that declares state_own and is called twice in one function gets a note naming both, which comp() at the call sites clears. No wrapper element. comp() is a position, not an element: the part's markup is a direct child of whatever element it was written in, so CSS sees the tree you wrote. SSR is unchanged. The server draws the declaration's initial values and releases the instances when the html is text; the browser builds its instances from the same call sites, so hydration is byte-for-byte what it was. Lifecycle: effect_own , mount_own , on_cleanup , on_mount An instance is worth little without a way to clean up after it, and the framework had no per-position effect and no teardown hook at all: c effect_own(N_) { \u2026 } declares an effect that belongs to the ambient position; the body runs with that position bound, so get / set work as they do in the view. mount_own(N_) starts it, once: a second call on a later render i"},{"t":"12. Frontend: DOM and SSR","p":"reference/frontend-dom-and-ssr.html","g":"Reference","h":["12f. Numbers, money and text you build","12d2. HTML that came from the database: webc/richtext.h","12d3. The facts a page states: webc/ld.h","12d4. Authored HTML as a file: partials/","12e. Parts: a view's composition unit","12d. Prose, and markup you authored","12c. Progressive enhancement","12g. Where the pointer was","12h. Charts: webc/chart.h","12b. Routes, resources and forms on the frontend","12b2. Work that takes too long: res_compute","12i. The standard library: webc/&lt;module&gt;.h","12j. Live bindings: a node that updates itself"],"b":"Reference \u00b7 docs/reference.md c el(\"tag\") { ... } opens an element with a cleanup-linked builder. text() : one argument is a value , two or more are a format. text(story->title) prints the title; text(\"%s/%d\", a, b) formats. The count is the whole distinction and it is decided at compile time, because a rule that treats every first argument as a format turns text(\"100% organic\") into 1000rganic and text(row->title) into a read of a nonexistent variadic argument. Attributes, one spelling per concept , each taking a Text , a str or a literal ( WC_AS_STR , chosen at compile time) and a number where a number belongs: attr(\"width\", 640) , attr_i(\"n\", i) , class_(\"card lead\") , style(\"width:50%\") , id_(\"reader\") , href(url) , src(path) , alt(t) , title_(t) , role(r) , rel(r) , lang(l) , target(t) , value_(v) , name(n) , placeholder(p) , type_(t) , required() , checked(on) , disabled(on) . A style is a declaration list, never a format: % in width:50% is a value. Each of the formatted ones has a _fmt companion that formats for you: class_fmt(\"row row-%d\", i) , style_fmt(\"--cols:%d\", n) , attr_fmt(\"data-id\", \"%d\", id) , post_to_fmt(\"/flights/%d\", id) , href_fmt(...) , src_fmt(...) , id_fmt(...) . if in a view is fine. A branch that appears, grows or disappears \u2014 including in the middle of an element \u2014 is a supported render: siblings it did not touch keep their identity (and their DOM node), and the nodes the branch owned are the only ones removed. show(cond) still has its place for the case where a toggle should not cost a node: it flips display:none on an element that stays in the tree, so an open/closed panel keeps its scroll position and its listeners. show() is also the safe way to hide a container that holds a position: fixed child: display:none on an ancestor takes the whole subtree out of hit-testing, while a class_(\"hidden\") that only sets visibility somewhere else may not. When a click goes to an element you cannot see, WebC.why(x, y) in the console names it (\u00a713b). markup(bytes) for bytes that are already HTML ( raw() is gone; the name now says what the argument is), on(fn, event) for a listener on an element in this view, and bind_str(sig) for a live input bound to a state(Text, \u2026) . A live input is one line , in the view, with no matching line in client_main : el(\"input\") { attr(\"name\", \"reason\"); bind_str(reason); } for a state(Text, reason, \"\") . The two-step form ( input_hook(reason) in the view + bind_input(reason) in client_main ) still works, and it is order-sensitive \u2014 the hook resolves its field when the view runs, so both calls belong where the docs put them. Get it wrong and the field is reported, by name, once rather than doing nothing quietly. Which row was clicked. Inside an action, ev_attr(\"data-id\") (or ev_attr_i64 ) is the nearest value of that attribute at or above the element that fired, and ev_value() is the payload the bridge sent: c A keyed list ( for_each ) still hands its action the item itself; a positional one has no item to hand, and an action that declares one and gets NULL now says so once instead of returning silently. A listener is re-registered every render. An element that a patch reuses keeps its node and its data-wc id; it does not keep its listener list. That is not an implementation detail you have to care about \u2014 named() / on_click() / bind() just work across renders \u2014 but it is why a handler must not close over render-scoped memory: it is called with the context the current render attached. bind_soft(name, event, action) is bind() for a control that is only rendered sometimes (a panel behind a branch): it is excluded from the dead-binding report, which keeps its teeth for a name no view ever renders. Reuse: re-rendering the same component patches only nodes whose text or attributes changed. A counter that ticks costs one text command. wkeyed_list(n, key_at, item, ctx) reconciles by key and disposes the per-item scope when an item leaves; the macro form is for_each(item, vec, KEY) (an index loop is for_i ). portal(\"#modal-root\") { ... } puts a subtree under another element, so a dialog escapes its stacking context. for_each is a render loop, not a way to iterate. A key drawn twice in one run is a panic naming the key: the key is the model's .pk , and 0 is its unset value \u2014 the shape a page of hand-built rows ( vec_new + push , no ids) makes. Before the sentence it was a node that was its own sibling and a render that never finished. Each pass opens a keyed item scope on the node that is currently open, and the loop's last statement sweeps every item of that node the pass did not claim. That is what disposes a removed row's scope \u2014 and it is also why a for_each that renders nothing (a count, a sum, a find ) is not harmless: it runs the sweep on whatever element the view has open. Counting is a plain loop: c for_each_ptr is the same iteration with no keyed scopes at all. page(View, ctx) renders HTML with data-wc / data-on-* anchors; the bridge adopts those nodes on the client without creating any. 12f. Numbers, money and text you build A view that shows a number has three small problems and no good place to solve them: the number needs thousands separators, money needs a currency and a sensible zero, and a percentage needs a decimal count that does not jitter frame to frame. Written by hand they come out different in every view \u2014 10038 in one card and $10,038.00 in the next \u2014 and the third one silently costs a snprintf into a buffer somebody sized by hand. c All five return Text \u2014 a string that lives as long as the current scope, like fmt() \u2014 so they nest in class_fmt , attr_fmt , href_fmt and each other, and none of them allocates heap. fmt_pct takes a fraction : 0.0421 is 4.2% , and the one caller that had it backwards is now a compile-time-looking mistake instead of a page that says 0%. An attribute that is a list of numbers \u2014 points , d , stroke-dasharray \u2014 is built with a string builder instead of six snprintf calls into a scratch buffer: c sb() is scoped to the render exactly like fmt() : the buffer is allocated in the render's arena, so a builder per element costs an unreclaimed pointer and nothing else, and calling sb() twice in one element gives you two independent builders (which is what a chart with a band and a line needs). sb_puts , sb_putstr , sb_putc , sb_i64 , sb_u64 , sb_printf ( __attribute__((format(printf, 2, 3))) , so a wrong format is a compiler warning), sb_num(b, v, decimals) for a double with fixed decimals, sb_cstr for an API that wants a NUL, sb_str for attr . Outside a render \u2014 in a test, or a task \u2014 the builder falls back to the current scope's arena and behaves the same way. 12d2. HTML that came from the database: webc/richtext.h The framework has three cases for authored bytes, and two would be a floor that leaves the third unwritten: text() / prose() for copy, markup() for bytes you wrote. The third is the one every CMS has \u2014 HTML a user wrote and a column stored \u2014 and it is the only one that needs a sanitiser. c It parses once and re-emits through el() / attr() / text() , so escaping is not a second implementation: a tag outside the policy is dropped (its text stays), script , style , iframe , svg , math , form , template and friends are dropped with their contents , every attribute value is entity-decoded before it is judged \u2014 java&#115;cript: is a javascript: URL and is refused \u2014 and href / src / poster accept only http(s), mailto, tel and relative URLs. A data: URI is refused unless .allow_data_images , and then only for a non-SVG image. A <a target=\"_blank\"> and any absolute link get rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" unless the author wrote one. A tag left open is closed at the end of the fragment; a stray closing tag closes nothing; nesting is capped at .max_depth (40). The same policy answers the questions an article page asks, without a second parser: c rich() gives a heading an id derived from its own text ( getting-started , and -2 for the second one), which is what a table of contents links to; wc_rich_stamp_ids() writes the same ids into the stored fragment so an anchor a reader copied still lands after an edit, and it is idempotent. Neither is Markdown (that is prose() ) and neither is a validator: a fragment is the unit, and sanitising on the way out is what makes a stored body safe to render. 12d3. The facts a page states: webc/ld.h Every page already says what it is about \u2014 the <title> , the og: tags, the prose \u2014 and then repeats it for a crawler in a JSON-LD block. The shape every app arrived at on its own was a fmt() carrying the whole document, a hand-written escaper for each value, and the vocabulary's nesting retyped on every page type. Three things are wrong with it and none of them is the app's \u2014 the nesting is knowledge a library should hold, a fmt() cannot omit a key (so a draft claims it was published at \"\" , which is not a date), and the resulting string is not safe in the element it is written into. webc lint names that spelling; this is th"},{"t":"13. The browser bridge","p":"reference/the-browser-bridge.html","g":"Reference","h":["13b. Running in a browser","13c. Calling JavaScript, and being called","13d. The host: network, keys and the document \u2014 js() you never write","13e. The boot block: a project with no HTML file","13f. webc.json: the 404 page and the policy","13g. lint.exempt: when an app's history has to name the word"],"b":"Reference \u00b7 docs/reference.md The C side emits a line protocol, the JS side replays it: text assets/webc.js exposes hydrate(root, commands) , apply(commands) , feed(chunk) , start({wasm}) , dispatch(name, event) (run a binding by name \u2014 a keyboard shortcut, a voice command, another script), diagnostics() and why(x, y) . A batch cannot half-apply. One malformed op \u2014 an attribute with no name, a create into a node that is gone \u2014 is skipped, counted, and named in the console; every other op in the batch still runs. Before that, a single bad line threw out of the replay loop and the rest of the page was simply never built, with no error to say so. WebC.diagnostics() returns { ops, badOps, lastBad, created, hydrated } : js Listeners are delegated. One real DOM listener per event type lives on the document and dispatches by node id, so an element that a patch re-creates keeps its binding, a click on a child reaches the element that asked for it, and portal() content still works. Node ids are stable for anything the framework reuses; names ( data-hook ) never move. Set globalThis.WEBC_DIRECT_LISTENERS = true before WebC.start() to attach one listener per element instead. start({ data }) hands the module bytes. The seed JSON is copied into wasm memory as UTF-8 and the module is told the byte count \u2014 not text.length , which counts UTF-16 units. One accented letter in the seed makes those two numbers differ, and a JSON document that is merely truncated is not a crash: the parse fails, the app renders its empty state, and data: looks like it did nothing at all. Hydration is verified. With hydrate: true the module's first batch adopts the server's markup node by node, by id. If a node cannot be adopted \u2014 a markup() slot, an id that moved between the halves \u2014 the elements the module did not claim are dropped instead of leaving both trees on the page, and WebC.diagnostics().hydrated is false . js anchors is how many data-wc nodes the server sent, createdEls how many elements the first batch had to build (every text node is created on a perfect adoption, which is why the text count is separate), and hydrated is the verdict. The two halves have to render the same tree for adoption to work: seed the module from the server's own JSON with data: before it starts, and keep the first render free of anything that does not exist in the request ( Date.now() , localStorage , a fetch that lands later). \"The click does nothing.\" WebC.why(x, y) names the element under that point and walks up its ancestors with the three facts that decide whether a click can reach it: display , pointer-events , and the node ids that actually have a listener. text 13b. Running in a browser The same library compiles to wasm and runs against the real DOM. An app never tests for the target: it declares which files are server, shared and client in webc.json , and webc build compiles each set for the side it belongs to. c c c c webc build produces both halves from those three sets; webc dev rebuilds both and reloads the tab. Pieces: Piece What it does src/wasm_glue.c exports webc_start , webc_dispatch , webc_take_cmds , webc_cmds_len , webc_alloc , webc_free ; calls the weak wc_app_main() (what client_main becomes) once from webc_start() page(View, ctx) renders the view into the <div id=\"root\"> of public/index.html , or into a bare fragment when there is no shell: the page the browser hydrates named(name) / bind(name, event, action) the view names an element, the client names the behaviour; inside a keyed list the action receives the item mw_web_assets(WC_WASM_PATH) serves /webc.js and /app.wasm with the right types and no-cache ; WC_WASM_PATH is .webc/app.wasm , which is what webc build writes assets/webc.js instantiates, fakes the WASI calls, attaches listeners, replays command batches The shell loads it: html Serving it in production. A browser-only project has no server half, so webc serve is the server: a release build ( -Oz ), no watcher, no reload socket, and the framework's own chain over public/ and .webc/app.wasm with mw_compress() in front of it. console What the browser actually reports for that page ( transferSize , headers included): Resource On the wire Decoded / 947 B 647 B under the 1 KB floor, sent as it is, Vary still set /style.css 2.3 KB 6.0 KB /webc.js 20.9 KB 66.4 KB 32% /app.wasm 64.9 KB 155.0 KB 42% first load 86.9 KB 222.7 KB 39% A reload costs nothing: every one of them carries an ETag (the compressed one ends in -gz ) and answers If-None-Match with a 304, so the second visit transfers headers only. A project with a server half is built as a release binary and handed the process, so its own middleware decides; webc_use(mw_compress()) next to its mw_static is the same one line. hydrate: true adopts the server markup by its data-wc anchors instead of building it again: the measured cost of the hand-over is zero created nodes . A delegated binding resolves its item by key at click time, so a list that grows or shifts never hands an action a stale pointer. A pointer event arrives with its position. The payload the bridge sends is the field's text for a field event and six numbers ( \\x01x,y,w,h,cx,cy ) for a pointer event; ev_point reads the second (\u00a712g). One payload field, two meanings, decided by the first byte. Every callback owns an arena. An event, a timer, a fetch that settled, a field and a popstate all run outside a render, so there was no arena to reclaim what they allocate \u2014 and every synchronous js() copies its result into whatever arena is current. In a loop that is a leak with no reset point: one app's deep run made 21 million js(\"Date.now\") calls, grew the app arena to 2 GiB, wrapped the command buffer's i32 pointer and froze the page with no error. Now each entry point from the bridge installs a callback arena, and two generations rotate: what a callback allocates stays valid through the next callback (so a render can still read a string it made) and is reclaimed two callbacks later. A callback that allocates more than 8 MB reports it once, by name, and starts from released chunks \u2014 js() in a loop is exactly that shape, and the message says so. What a render costs , when a page feels slow and you need to know whether the render is the problem: WEBC_RENDER_COST=1 webc dev prints at most one line a second naming the worst render in it \u2014 text The measurement is two clock reads per render; the line appears only above 4 ms, and only when asked. Whole-view re-render is the model (\u00a711a) and 2\u20134 ms is what it costs for a page-sized view; a render that takes 40 ms is a view doing work inside the render, and res_compute (\u00a712b2) is where that work goes. A view whose changing value is a leaf can skip the render entirely \u2014 \u00a712j. 13c. Calling JavaScript, and being called One door, typed where C can check it ( <webc/js.h> ) \u2014 examples/js is a page that walks through all of it: c Arguments are chosen from their C type by _Generic \u2014 a str or a literal is a string, an integer a number, a bool a boolean, js_obj(\"\u2026\") a JSON literal \u2014 so nothing in application source spells JSON, and a quote in a value stays a quote in a value. (A string literal reaches _Generic as char[N] , undecayed; that is why the default branch is the C-string encoder.) The result is a WJs , a value with a kind and its own error: c r.error belongs to that call, so three calls in a handler have three answers \u2014 js_error() was one slot for the whole module. A failure is also the one thing that is logged once, by the bridge, with the path in it. A path is dotted from globalThis and may name a function (called with the arguments) or a property (read when there are no arguments). An object's fields are only readable when it serialises as data \u2014 JSON.stringify of a Response or a MediaQueryList is {} . There are no object handles in C, deliberately: a handle is a lifetime two sides have to agree about. When a call resolves with data, put the one line that unwraps it where JavaScript is written ( window.api = { get: async (u) => (await fetch(u)).json() } ). Promises are callbacks , not an event loop and not await in C: c The handler runs when the promise settles, as an action: signals are written, the views re-render, the patch batch goes to the page. js_then returns false when there is no JavaScript or when WC_JS_PENDING_MAX calls are already in flight. The other direction \u2014 the page driving the app: c WebC.call(name, \u2026args) marshals the arguments, calls the export and flushes the patch batch; WebC.dispatch(name[, event]) fires the action a view named with named(\"bump\") , with the context the listener was attached with. Both are for the click that does not come from the DOM \u2014 a keyboard shortcut, a voice command, another script. The low-level form is still there and still the ABI: js_call(path, args_json) returns JSON text, and js_get_str / js_get_bool keep their defaults. On a server build every call answers \"undefined\" and js_available() is false , so one file can be both halves. Toolchain: wasi-s"},{"t":"14. The CLI","p":"reference/the-cli.html","g":"Reference","h":["14d. The language server","14e. The VS Code extension","14f. The MCP server","14g. Working with agents"],"b":"Reference \u00b7 docs/reference.md text Three of those are about not lying to yourself: webc build --explain prints the object cache's reasoning ( [stale] src/view.c: \u2026deps sub.h mtime=\u2026 , [cc] src/view.c ) instead of compiling in silence. WEBC_TRACE_WATCH still elsewhere-documented what the dev server watches; this is the build's own account, as a flag, because the moment somebody needs it is the moment they are staring at a build that did the wrong thing. A failed build clears the cache and tries once more. A cache that is stale in a way mtimes cannot see \u2014 a restored checkout, a header touched back in time, a killed build's half-written object \u2014 presents as a compile error in a file that compiles, and the cure is rm -rf .webc . A failing build now says so, clears the object directory and rebuilds; if the second attempt fails too, the errors are real. The wasm half falls back to the one-shot command and clears its own object directory, so the slow path repairs the fast one instead of masking it once. webc size answers \"what does this page cost, and where did it go\" without devtools: text That output is not a demonstration: the command, run on a real app, finds 1.6 MB of data that turns out to be one static Strat g_strats[] = {\u2026} \u2014 21 partially-initialised structs holding 10 001 doubles each, whose zeros a wasm data segment ships byte for byte. Declaring it without the initialiser and filling it in boot() takes the module from 1.77 MB to 165 kB. Nothing else in the framework's tooling can tell you that. webc.json : json dev.connect is the Content-Security-Policy connect-src the dev server sends: a browser half exists to call somebody's API, and webc dev serves that half, so the dev policy already allows any https: origin. Name the origins your app actually calls and the policy becomes exactly those (plus the reload websocket) \u2014 a narrowing knob, never a widening one, and it only ever applies to webc dev . 14d. The language server webc lsp speaks LSP over stdio, from the same binary as the rest of the CLI: no Node, no second download, no build. Point any editor at webc lsp and it has diagnostics, completion, hover, definition, references, an outline and quick fixes for a WebC project. It is two engines with one line between them, because a WebC file is two things at once. C is clang's job \u2014 a whole view type-checks in about a tenth of a second, and a second hand-written C front end would be a second source of truth about C. The DSL is ours : the macros that take a following { \u2026 } , the model tuples, the three file sets, the routes, the wiring. A per-file region map \u2014 which bytes are inside view(\u2026) , el(\u2026) , GET(\u2026) and which are plain C \u2014 decides which engine answers a given caret. That translator is the product. The compiler answers page(Nope, \u2026) with use of undeclared identifier 'view_Nope' behind five frames of WC_CAT ; the server answers \"no view named Nope in this project \u2014 view(Nope) draws it, or declare_view(Nope) in the shared header announces it\", and offers the line to add. GET(\u2026) in a shared source is a caret error naming the half, not eight cascading syntax errors about parameter declarators. The compiler half is the same compiler webc build runs, asked about the buffer the editor is holding rather than the file on disk. Two rules keep it from being a second opinion: a diagnostic inside a DSL macro's own parentheses is dropped (those bytes are the WebC engine's to explain, and the compiler speaks about them in names like view_Nope ), and a block that never closed stops the compiler from running at all. WEBC_CC picks the compiler; when a project's check costs more than 120 ms it moves from every keystroke to every save, because a language server that stutters is a language server people turn off. What it knows, it knows from the sources: the models (including the ones declared in headers, which no webc.json ever lists), the views and where they are drawn, the actions and what is wired to them, the routes and the view each returns, and which standard-library module may appear in which half. Nothing under .webc/ is read, so a fresh clone is a working workspace. It deliberately does not reformat a view. clang-format rewrites el(\"h1\") { text(\"Todos\"); } into four lines, which is not this language's style; document formatting is therefore refused with a reason, and range formatting outside a DSL block is what webc lsp offers instead. There is a second opinion in most C editors, and it is clangd. So the server writes compile_commands.json at the project root \u2014 the flags webc build uses, one entry per source \u2014 which clangd finds without being told anything. Without it clangd invents errors on a WebC file it cannot resolve; with it, the twenty-one it invented in this repository became none. The file is generated, gitignored, and the server never reads it back. The editor client lives at editors/vscode/ : no dependencies, no build step, nothing require d from a registry. Where the binary is comes from resolve.js \u2014 the setting with ~ and ${\u2026} expanded, else build/webc in the workspace or a parent, else the PATH \u2014 because a settings field is typed by a human and spawn(2) expands no tilde. make lsp checks that the client still loads and that the resolver is right about every way a path can be wrong (missing, a directory, not executable, relative, on no PATH), so a client that silently ignores the setting is a red gate rather than a broken window. make lsp is the gate: --selftest asserts what the server says about a real project, and --replay diffs the protocol against a recorded session ( tests/lsp/sessions/*.jsonl ), so a wire change is a diff in review rather than a bug report. --bench prints what one request costs, and --trace FILE writes every message it saw as JSONL. 14e. The VS Code extension editors/vscode/ is that client: extension.js speaks JSON-RPC to webc lsp over stdio itself (no vscode-languageclient ), and resolve.js decides which binary to spawn. It is three settings and two commands: setting default meaning webc.lsp.path \"\" the binary. ~ , ${workspaceFolder} , ${userHome} and ${env:NAME} are expanded; a relative path is taken from the workspace folder; empty means build/webc in the workspace or a parent, else webc on the PATH webc.lsp.args [] extra arguments, e.g. [\"--trace\", \"/tmp/lsp.jsonl\"] webc.lsp.enabled true start the server at all command what it does WebC: Show the language server log the output channel, including every diagnostic the server published WebC: Restart the language server respawn, without reloading the window It spawns <bin> lsp --root <workspace> <args> , clears its diagnostic collection whenever the server stops (a dead server must not leave yesterday's errors on screen), and re-reads its settings on change \u2014 a setting that is only read once at activation is indistinguishable, from the outside, from one that is ignored. A failed start is one message that says which failure it was (not found, not executable, exited) and quotes the last lines of the server's stderr, with the resolved path in it. Installation is a copy, a symlink, or a .vsix ; code --extensionDevelopmentPath= \"$PWD/editors/vscode\" runs it out of the tree. The guide The editor has the commands for VS Code, Cursor, Antigravity, Neovim and Emacs, and the troubleshooting table. 14f. The MCP server webc mcp speaks the Model Context Protocol over stdio, from the same binary as everything else \u2014 no Node, no second download, no version skew between the server that answers and the compiler it answers about. The argument is the one that gave us webc lsp , and it is stronger here: a server that reports diagnostics must be the compiler. It exists for one reason, stated as four: What an agent needs A shell already gives it What MCP adds the gate, after a change webc check {ok, steps:[{name, ok, output}]} \u2014 no prose to parse the project's own index grep models, fields, views, actions, routes and each file's half, with positions the whole public surface grep api.txt a search that returns the declaration, its header and its doc line the page it just built two processes and curl a dev server held across calls, and webc_fetch Seven tools, and one rule: every tool is a door onto something the CLI already does, and nothing parses human output. If a tool needs data, the command that owns it learned --json first. That is what keeps this from becoming a second, disagreeing front end. tool returns writes webc_check the --json gate: build, tests, doctor, boot, schema, routes, lint, consistency \u2014 webc_facts models (+fields, qualifiers), views (+context), actions, routes (+view), files (+half, modules) \u2014 webc_api {query} \u2192 matches:[{header, line, declaration, doc}] \u2014 webc_test {filter?} \u2192 {ok, tests:[{suite, passed, failed, output}]} \u2014 webc_dev {action: start|stop|status} \u2192 {running, url, port} ; one server held by the process \u2714 process webc_fetch {path, method?, body?} \u2192 {status, content_type, body} from that server \u2014 webc_scaffold {template, dir} or {add_mo"},{"t":"14b. Testing","p":"reference/testing.html","g":"Reference","h":["14b1. The test client: a jar, a form encoder, and the row","14c. Assertions about structure"],"b":"Reference \u00b7 docs/reference.md webc test compiles each tests/*_test.c into its own binary (with the app's own sources linked in, its main renamed), runs it, and prints go-test-shaped output. The surface is <webc/test.h> : c TEST(name) { } \u2014 a test. It registers itself with a constructor, so a file is a list of blocks and nothing is enumerated by hand. No main , no list to forget. TEST_MAIN() \u2014 the binary's entry point. TEST_SETUP(fn) / TEST_TEARDOWN(fn) run once around the file (a database, a scheduler). SUBTEST(\"label\") { } and SUBTESTS(rows) { ... } \u2014 a subtest is one block; SUBTESTS runs the block once per row of a static array, binding the row to r and using its name member for the label. A failing row does not stop the next one. Assertions : ASSERT , ASSERT_TRUE/FALSE , ASSERT_EQ/NE/LT/LE/GT/GE , ASSERT_FEQ , ASSERT_STR_EQ (a str / Buf / Response /C string against a literal), ASSERT_STR (two of them), ASSERT_STR_CONTAINS , ASSERT_MEM_EQ , ASSERT_NULL/NOT_NULL , ASSERT_OK/ERR/ERR_KIND (anything with .ok ). An assertion records a failure and continues , like go's t.Error . Responses : pass one straight to ASSERT_STR_CONTAINS(r, \"\u2026\") , and read a header by name: ASSERT_STATUS(r, 303) , ASSERT_HEADER(r, \"Location\", \"/\") , ASSERT_HEADER_CONTAINS(r, \"Set-Cookie\", \"HttpOnly\") , ASSERT_HEADER_COUNT(r, \"Content-Security-Policy\", 1) , ASSERT_BODY_LEN(r, 12) . A duplicate header is legal HTTP and a real bug; counting is the only way a test can see it. Header names are matched case-insensitively (RFC 9110 \u00a75.1): a handler stores Set-Cookie as it spelled it, and a test may ask for set-cookie \u2014 a memcmp comparison misses it, which is why every app that asserts on a session cookie writes its own helper. FAIL(fmt, ...) records a failure with a message; SKIP(why) stops the test and reports it skipped. try(expr) inside a test panics with the error (a TEST body returns void, so it cannot return the Result ); the runner turns that into a failed test with the message. Every test runs inside a panic frame, so a panic in application code \u2014 not just in an assertion \u2014 fails that one test and the rest of the file still runs. The client half of a test. A request with no memory is not a browser, and a test of a mutating route written without one rebuilds a browser: a cookie jar by hand, a form body by hand, and a helper for the header lookup. TestClient is that browser (\u00a714b1 below): c What is actually asserted, spelled out: webc_test_request(method, path) builds a request, runs the router and the middleware chain, and returns the Response \u2014 no sockets. webc_test_request_ex(method, path, headers, body, body_len) is the same with a header block ( \"Cookie: session=\u2026\\r\\n\" ) and a body, which is what a test of an authenticated route needs. Both convert a handler panic into a 500, exactly like the server. An app's tests link the app. webc test compiles the app's sources (found the same way webc build finds them) with main renamed, so the routes are the real ones; the state is built by calling the app's own webc_boot() from TEST_SETUP() ( <webc/app.h> ). examples/todo and examples/accounts are the worked examples: 8 and 7 tests, real database, real sessions, no socket. 14b1. The test client: a jar, a form encoder, and the row c The jar. Every Set-Cookie the response carries is kept (name and value, not the attributes) and sent back on the next request; Max-Age=0 deletes one. A second test_client() in the same test is a second browser \u2014 the two-users case, which was untestable when a test could only send headers it had spelled itself. The form encoder. tc_post_form* serialises the model the way model_from_form reads it: one input per field in declaration order, the pk left out (a create does not post the id back), a bool as on / off , an enum as its value's name, a VecStr as one comma-separated value, and everything percent-encoded as a browser encodes it. The assertions a mutation test needs. ASSERT_REDIRECT(r, \"/where\") , ASSERT_DB_ONE(T, field, str) / ASSERT_DB_ONE_I64(T, field, n) (\"the row it should have written\"), ASSERT_DB_COUNT(T, want) . It does not run JavaScript \u2014 that is webc test --client , which replays the client half's batches through the real bridge \u2014 and it does not follow redirects: a test that follows one hides the 303 it came for. webc test --watch reruns the suite whenever src/ , tests/ , shared/ , content/ or webc.json changes, clearing the screen each pass, so what is on it is the last run and not the history of them. --filter picks suites (and runs them whole); --test narrows by test name across every suite, which is the slower one because every suite builds. 14c. Assertions about structure A substring test passes when the markup moved somewhere the CSS no longer reaches \u2014 which is exactly the regression a redesign causes. Two assertions speak the languages a response is actually written in: c The selector is a subset of CSS: tag, .class , #id , [attr] , [attr=\"value\"] , descendant (space) and child ( > ). wc_json_path(JVal*, \"a.b.0\", &out) and wc_html_count(html, sel) are the functions underneath, for a test that wants the value rather than the assertion. Structured data is neither JSON nor HTML, and it is the thing that breaks silently \u2014 a page whose author gained an empty name still renders, still passes ASSERT_HTML , and has quietly lost its rich result. ASSERT_LD reads the first application/ld+json block out of a page and asks it the same by-path question: c ASSERT_JSON_DOC / ASSERT_JSON_DOC_ABSENT are the same question about a JSON document rather than a response \u2014 what a serializer, a writer, or a ld_* call is tested at, before a route exists. wc_ld_block(html) returns the payload if a test wants it directly. An HTML assertion matches the server-rendered markup with its data-wc anchors in it, so it tests the page a browser receives, not a string this test happened to expect. Every one of them takes any HTML-ish thing , not just a Response : a str from wcomp_html_ctx (the browser half's own render), a Buf , a const char * . That is the whole of what a client-side test needs to read a card: c ASSERT_HTML_NUMBER is the one that replaces a hand-written scanner per app: it takes the text at a selector, keeps the first number in it (any currency symbol, separators, a trailing % or a unit around it are dropped) and compares it to within 1e-6 . A text with no number at all fails, so an empty selector cannot pass by accident. A client test does not hand-write its declarations. webc wires > src/wires.h emits the view prototypes, the action prototypes (both the ones bind() names and the ones a view wires with on_click() ), and a WIRES_<field> constant per named field \u2014 so a test file starts with #include \"wires.h\" instead of one ACTION(x) line per button, and a new button needs no test-file edit at all. It is generated from the sources, so it cannot drift. A pointer can be dispatched with a position, which is what makes hover code testable: c"},{"t":"15. Ops","p":"reference/ops.html","g":"Reference","h":[],"b":"Reference \u00b7 docs/reference.md Logs: log_info(\"request\", { \"path\", req->path }, { \"status\", ... }) . Text by default, JSON with WEBC_LOG=json ; level from WEBC_LOG_LEVEL . Metrics: metrics_counter_inc , metrics_observe ; /metrics in Prometheus format when WebcOptions.metrics is set. The server exports webc_conns , webc_tasks , webc_requests_total and the connection-close counters webc_conn_closed_eof_total (the client closed: normal keep-alive end), webc_conn_close_idle_total , webc_conn_close_partial_total (a request was cut short) and webc_conn_read_error_total (a read failed on our side \u2014 the one counter that should stay at zero). /debug reports heap, arenas, tasks and route count; /debug/cpu?ms=2000 runs the sampling profiler for one window and prints a flat profile, and webc_profile_start() / webc_profile_stop() / webc_profile_report() do it from code. The sampler signals every process thread \u2014 including threads the runtime does not own \u2014 from a dedicated thread, and never itself. Limits, all answerable: `WebcOptions{ .max_body, .max_header_bytes, .header_timeout_ms, .max_conns, .max_multipart_parts, .max_multipart_bytes, .max_json_depth }`. A body over the limit is a 413, headers over the limit a 431, JSON nested deeper than 256 a 400 that says so. Multipart ( <webc/multipart.h> ): multipart_parse() plus model_from_multipart() , so a form with a file lands in the same model as one without. Part and byte limits are arguments. Redis and the query cache add their counters to the same list (\u00a79d): webc_cache_* for what the cache did and webc_redis_* for what the wire did. WEBC_TRACE_CACHE=1 prints one line per statement ( [cache] select \u2026 -> hit ), WEBC_TRACE_DB=1 prints the statements themselves. Budgets are a gate: make budget measures SPEC \u00a710 and exits non-zero on a miss (SSR \u2265 100k req/s, 1 KB JSON \u2264 1 \u00b5s, patch \u2264 0.2 ms, wasm hello \u2264 24 KiB gzip, serve binary \u2264 460 KiB, cold build \u2264 1500 ms). The serve binary's line moved from 400 KiB to 460 KiB when Redis and the cache became part of it \u2014 hello measured 456 544 B on the machine this was written on; make redis-test runs the client and the cache against a real redis-server . Shutdown: SIGINT/SIGTERM stop accepting, drain for drain_ms , then exit."},{"t":"16. Limits and deviations","p":"reference/limits-and-deviations.html","g":"Reference","h":[],"b":"Reference \u00b7 docs/reference.md These are measured facts, not guesses: No nested functions. C23 removed them, so routes() cannot contain GET(...) . Routes are file-scope macros; grouping is done with path prefixes and per-route middleware ( GET_MW ). Bare qualifier flags are invalid C. .required is a syntax error; write .required = true . There are no token-pasting macros named required / max , because a macro that turns a field into a flag cannot be read back by the compiler's own message. state(...) shadows state(2) in any translation unit that includes <webc/web.h> ; select(...) shadows select(2) the same way, and that one predates it. Include the system headers first. Neither is a problem in a translation unit that does not call the POSIX function \u2014 and a file that does can call select_wait (see task.h ) or keep the system header in its own translation unit. No guard page on task stacks. mprotect(PROT_NONE) on one page of an anonymous mapping makes its neighbours SIGBUS on Darwin (verified with isolated repros), so overflow detection is by fault, not by message. A canary at the stack base fires falsely on every task because Darwin's ucontext writes its own frames there. Redis is one server, one database, one process pool. No Sentinel, no Cluster, no MOVED redirection, no SCAN -driven invalidation: the query cache's counter keys and entries assume a single server everybody shares. A Cluster deployment is a different client, not a configuration of this one. TLS is a build feature, off by default. -DWEBC_TLS=openssl (OpenSSL headers and libraries; make tls-test ) enables res_tls / tls_connect . A build without it refuses to start when a listener is configured for TLS instead of serving plaintext on a port someone believes is encrypted. Postgres has no TLS, so sslmode is not implemented. The wasm target needs wasi-sdk (~170 MB, a separate download; webc doctor reports whether it found it). The browser half has no threads, no filesystem and no SQL: include/webc/web.h is the subset it compiles \u2014 core, collections, models, JSON, signals, DOM. Verified end to end in Chrome: render, keyed list add/remove/toggle, two-way bound input, SSR markup hydrated in place. Postgres and MySQL are wired and tested \u2014 make pg-test starts postgres:16-alpine , make mysql-test starts mysql:8 , each runs the same suite across every driver, and each removes its container. Postgres was untested for most of this build; the SQL lexer and the driver twins were found by running it. Only PG16 and MySQL 8 are tested \u2014 MariaDB gets the same protocol but not its own run, there is no PREPARE reuse on the postgres side, and there is no pool-exhaustion test for either. The MySQL driver has no RSA public-key exchange, so sha256_password and caching_sha2_password 's full authentication need TLS. A component instance costs 56 B, and comp() is opt-in. One heap WInst (24 B) and one table slot (32 B) per instance drawn , whether or not it reads state; a part called without comp() pays nothing and shares its position's state (\u00a711d). The 20 000-row table with a component per row is therefore still the wrong shape \u2014 per-row state is the same arithmetic. wcomp_render re-renders the whole view and diffs. That is component-level reactivity, not per-node: the DOM writes are minimal, the C work is not. \u00a712j's live bindings are the per-node half \u2014 a leaf the view declares once \u2014 but they do not make a structural change cheap, and they do not raise the 20 000-op mount budget (\u00a716.8). webc_serve runs one worker per core by default ( cpus = 0 \u2192 the online processor count, capped at 64; set cpus = 1 to force a single worker). A task may be resumed on a different worker than it started on, so request state and the current arena travel with the task, never in a thread-local ( webc_req_current() , wtask_local() , and the scope accessors below). Measured with make load on a 10-core machine: 16 780 req/s of SSR HTML at 16 keep-alive connections and 12 112 req/s of one-request-per-connection churn, zero errors and no leaked connections or tasks over ~260 000 requests. The bugs that had to be fixed first are in findings/scheduler-and-server-bugs.md . Request state is task-local, not thread-local. If you add an API that needs \"the current request\", the markup build in progress or a per-request arena, use the task-local accessors ( webc_req_current() , wbuild_current() , and the scope arena the framework installs) \u2014 never an arena of your own. A _Thread_local there is a bug: many tasks share a worker thread, and a task that parks resumes on another one. wbuild_current() cost a real outage \u2014 examples/todo reads the database inside its view, the task parked there, and every worker began answering 500 \"el() outside a build\"; one render then dereferenced the null and the process died with SIGSEGV at address 72. A request allocates nothing that outlives it. The body, headers, params and rows live in the request arena; the render's component (three arenas and a command buffer) is destroyed before the response is written; a log line allocates nothing at all. Measured: /metrics webc_heap_bytes is byte-for-byte identical before and after 130 000 SSR-with-database requests, and 95 000 text requests; webc_arena_bytes is flat. RSS settles around 11 MB on that workload (allocator retention, not growth). Benchmarks print B/op for exactly this reason: it read 3 200 B/op the hour it was added. arena_destroy gives back what it took. wm_stats().arena_live counts chunks that exist, not reservations. Double-counting and keeping the reservation of an arena that never allocated is how /metrics comes to report 9 GB of live arenas after 300 000 requests. webc_test_request() 's Response lives until the next call on that thread: the helper resets a per-thread arena instead of allocating one per call (which leaked 64 KB per request in a loop). Copy what you keep \u2014 the suites in examples/ show the pattern."},{"t":"Contributing","p":"project/contributing.html","g":"Project","h":["Build it","The gates","House rules that catch everyone","Sign your commits (DCO)","Commits and pull requests","Where the docs live","Reporting a bug","Code of conduct","Licence"],"b":"WebC is a C23 full-stack web framework: one language and one model declaration for the server and the browser, compiled to a static binary. This is the framework's own repository \u2014 not an app built with it \u2014 so the rules below are about changing the framework without breaking what it promises. If you are here to use WebC, start at docs/getting-started.md or the documentation site . If you are here to change it, read on. Build it You need a C23 compiler ( clang 21 or newer \u2014 the suite uses #embed ), make , and the sqlite3 and zlib development headers. wasi-sdk is optional and only needed for the browser half; without it make skips the examples that have one and says so. bash make install puts the built tree in ~/.local (no sudo); make uninstall takes it back out. The gates Nothing lands red. A pull request says which of these you ran and pastes the last lines. Command What it proves make the library, the CLI and every example build make test 104 suites, in-process plus the JS and scaffold suites make dx webc lint --all and the api.txt surface budget make lsp the language server: a scripted session, then a replayed one make mcp the MCP server: a scripted session over examples/todo make check-version VERSION , the CLI, the site, the extension and an exact tag agree make install-test the installer's checksum check refuses a tampered tarball make site the committed site matches its sources ( git status website/ must be empty) make links every relative link and anchor on the site resolves make snippets every C sample in the docs compiles on its own make skill-check the agent skill's generated leaves are current sh scripts/spdx.sh --check every C source carries its licence header make fmt-check no new file is added to the list of unformatted sources make budget SSR throughput, JSON, wasm size, binary size, patch latency, cold build CI runs all of it on macOS and Linux ( .github/workflows/ci.yml ), plus a weekly fuzz run. make budget needs wasi-sdk, so it is a local gate today. make check is make test + make lsp , the two you will run most. House rules that catch everyone These are the conventions that the reviews here keep coming back to. They are also in AGENTS.md , because an agent working in this tree needs them as much as a person does. One answer, not four. The DSL has one scanner ( tools/webc/dsl.c ), one project index ( tools/webc/facts.c ) and one set of diagnostics ( tools/webc/lspx.c ). A tool that needs to know what a file says uses those, not a fourth regex. No new public names without a reason. webc api ratchets api.txt : a name that appears there is a decision. Adding one is fine \u2014 deleting one is a break, and both should be argued in the pull request. If you add one, run ./build/webc api --write and put the reason in the file's header, as a # N: why line \u2014 the tool carries those across every rewrite, so the reason stays next to the number it explains. A budget is a gate. If it matters, make exits non-zero when it is missed. Numbers that matter have a comment beside them saying what was measured, on what, and what the number is for. The retired spellings are retired. webc lint --rules is the table; the gate runs it over apps, examples, scaffolds and the docs. If you are adding a name, check it does not collide with one that was retired. Machine output is a contract. If a command grows --json , a tool may depend on it: keep the shape, and keep it a rendering of what the command actually did. Comments explain why , in the project's voice, and never restate the code. The best comment in this repository says what the alternatives were and what broke when they were tried. The docs are part of the change. Behaviour that changes without a line in docs/reference.md is a change a reader cannot find later. Sign your commits (DCO) Every commit needs a Signed-off-by line. This is a Developer Certificate of Origin \u2014 one line in a commit message that says you wrote the patch or otherwise have the right to submit it under the project's licence (Apache-2.0). There is no CLA to sign and no paperwork to keep: the sign-off is the record. bash That appends: text Use a name and address you are happy to see in the permanent history \u2014 a users.noreply.github.com address is fine. A pull request whose commits have no sign-off will be asked for one; git rebase --signoff fixes a branch that already exists. If you are contributing on behalf of an employer, make sure you are allowed to. Commits and pull requests One change per commit, and an imperative subject line that says what it does: \"the router refuses a duplicate pattern\", not \"fixed routing bug\". The body says why \u2014 what was wrong, what broke, what you measured. The commit messages in this repository are the design record; match their voice. A pull request should be reviewable in one sitting. If it is not, it is two pull requests. Say which gates you ran, and paste the output. \"Tests pass\" is not evidence; `webc 104 suites, 0 failing` is. If you touch the docs, run make site and commit the regenerated website/ \u2014 it is generated and committed, and CI fails when the two disagree. make links and make snippets are the other two that catch doc mistakes. If you touch a public name, the api.txt diff is part of the review. Say why the name is worth adding. If your change is a behaviour change, the pull request should say what a user would notice. Where the docs live docs/README.md is the map: which file is the source of which page, and which command regenerates what. The short version: docs/reference.md \u2192 the Reference section of the site, one page per ## N. section. It is the whole language; the SPEC \u00a7n citations in the source are its sections. website/src/content/*.md \u2192 the Start pages and the Guides, hand-written for the site. examples/<name>/ \u2192 the Examples pages, showing real files. docs/decisions.md \u2192 the decision record: why the licence, why the name, why the Linux port, why the version file. If you are about to argue that something should be different, read it first. skills/webc/ \u2192 the agent skill: SKILL.md by hand, references/ generated from the reference. Editing website/*.html by hand is always wrong: make site overwrites it. Reporting a bug Use the issue template; it asks for webc doctor , your compiler, the target, and the command that failed, which is what a fix needs. A bug with a failing test is a bug that gets fixed \u2014 tests/ is where a suite goes, and ./build/webc test --filter=your_test.c runs just yours. Security issues do not go in the issue tracker. See SECURITY.md . Code of conduct By participating you agree to the Code of Conduct . It is the Contributor Covenant, and it applies to issues, pull requests, discussions and anything else that carries this repository's name. Licence Contributions are accepted under Apache-2.0 , the same licence as the project. The sign-off above is what records that. New files need // SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0 on their first line \u2014 sh scripts/spdx.sh writes it for you."},{"t":"Changelog","p":"project/changelog.html","g":"Project","h":["[Unreleased]","[0.1.0] \u2014 2026-11-18","Added","Notes"],"b":"All notable changes to WebC are recorded here. The format is Keep a Changelog , the version numbers are Semantic Versioning , and the project is pre-1.0: a minor version may change the surface, and api.txt is the diff to read when it does. [Unreleased] [0.1.0] \u2014 2026-11-18 The first release: a C23 framework for full-stack web work, compiled to a static binary, with one model declaration shared by the server and the browser half. Added One language, two halves. view() markup is compiled for both targets from the same file; src/ builds an SSR server, a wasm module for the browser, or both, and the client hydrates the server's HTML rather than re-rendering it. The core runtime. Arenas instead of a collector, str slices, typed errors with must / ? , generics ( Vec(T) , Map(K, V) ), and a task scheduler \u2014 Go's ergonomics with C's ownership story. Models. WEBC_MODEL gives a table, a struct and a wire shape from one declaration, with validation, JSON, form decoding and SQL generated from it; migrations with a twin per dialect. The ORM. Typed filters, relations read with no query (batched or joined), aggregates, pages, trees, and a query cache keyed by generation. Signals, DOM and patching. state , memos and effects; SSR, hydration, keyed lists and two-way binding driven by the same renderer as the server. The HTTP server. A router table, typed handlers, keep-alive and pipelining, chunked bodies, Range on res_file() , gzip, CSP, multipart parsing with limits. Auth. PBKDF2-SHA256 (210 000 iterations) passwords, a signed HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax session cookie with no session table, and a guard that is one call. Four storage backends, no hiredis and no libpq. SQLite, PostgreSQL, MySQL/MariaDB and Redis, each one the wire protocol implemented here, each with its own gate. The CLI. webc new , build , run , serve --watch , test , check , fmt , lint , openapi , client --ts , doctor , version , docs , explain . Tooling that is part of the framework. A language server (diagnostics, completion, hover, quick fixes) and an MCP server, both from the same binary, plus a VS Code extension and an agent skill with generated reference leaves. The documentation. 4 230 lines of reference with SPEC \u00a7n citations from the source, a getting-started guide, seventeen guides, eight runnable examples, and a static site generated from all of it \u2014 no runtime, no CDN, no bundler. Eight examples , each one a different shape of the framework: hello, todo, accounts (auth), web (client-only), js (the seam), stdlib (the opt-in shelf), board (live rooms and a database), minimal (the bundle floor). Linux support : the epoll backend is the default there, and CI runs the suite on macOS and Linux with AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer, plus fuzzers over the parsers. Install : a prebuilt tarball per platform, or make install into ~/.local . The installer refuses a tarball whose digest does not match the published .sha256 . Notes Licence: Apache-2.0 , with a patent grant and a NOTICE that lists what is linked but not vendored. Every source file carries an SPDX header; sh scripts/spdx.sh --check enforces it. No npm package, and no plans for one \u2014 docs/decisions.md D2 says why. The compiler floor is clang 21 : the suite uses #embed . Gates that ship with the repository , all green on main : the suite (104), the developer surface and the api.txt ratchet, the language server, the MCP server, the budgets, the installer test, the site's links and snippets, the not-stale checks for the skill, the formatter ratchet and the licence headers. docs/decisions.md and AGENTS.md say what each one is for. [Unreleased]: https://github.com/bumasoft/WebC/compare/v0.1.0...HEAD [0.1.0]: https://github.com/bumasoft/WebC/releases/tag/v0.1.0"},{"t":"Security","p":"project/security.html","g":"Project","h":["Supported versions","Reporting a vulnerability","What to expect","In scope","Out of scope","How this project already tries","Credit"],"b":"WebC is a web framework: an HTTP server, a router, a template renderer that escapes, a cookie session, password hashing, four database drivers, and a WebAssembly client half. It runs inside other people's applications, so a bug here is a bug in everything built on it. Supported versions WebC is pre-1.0. Only the latest 0.1.x release gets fixes, and until 1.0 the surface can change in a minor version. Fixes go to main and into the next patch release; there are no maintenance branches yet. Reporting a vulnerability Do not open a public issue, a discussion, or a pull request. Use GitHub's private channel: Security tab \u2192 Report a vulnerability \u2192 https://github.com/bumasoft/WebC/security/advisories/new That opens a draft advisory only you and the maintainer can see, which is also where the fix is discussed and where credit is given. If you cannot use it, contact the maintainer through https://github.com/bumasoft \u2014 and say only that you have a report, with no details, so the channel can move somewhere private first. A report is most useful with: what the bug lets an attacker do, and what they must be able to reach to do it; the smallest application that shows it \u2014 a route, a handler, a request, ideally a test; the version ( webc version ), the platform, and the compiler; whether it is WebC's bug or the application's. Both matter, but they are answered differently. What to expect When What within 3 days an acknowledgement, and a first read on whether it is in scope within 10 days an assessment: affected versions, severity, and what a fix looks like at disclosure a patched release, an advisory, and credit \u2014 unless you would rather stay anonymous If a fix will take longer than that, you will hear why rather than nothing. Please give the fix a chance to ship before you publish: coordinated disclosure is the default here, and an advisory that names a version with a patch in it is worth more to users than a warning alone. In scope The framework's own code. The shapes of failure that matter most: HTML escaping and the renderer. text() , attr() , richtext() and the attribute machinery are the only thing standing between a model field and a browser. A way to get unescaped bytes into the document, or out of the CSP that the server sets, is a serious bug. The HTTP layer. Request parsing, chunked bodies, max_body , header limits, keep-alive and pipelining, path decoding, Range on res_file() , and the multipart parser ( max_parts , per-part max_bytes ). Smuggling, a request that is read as something other than what a proxy in front of it read, or a body that exceeds a documented limit. Sessions and auth. The session cookie is signed, not encrypted \u2014 anything put in it is readable by the user, which is documented and intended. What is not intended: forging a cookie, replaying one past its expiry, an auth_hash_password / auth_check_password bug, PBKDF2 iteration downgrade, a timing leak in the comparison, or a session that survives a rotation it should not. The database drivers. WebC implements the PostgreSQL, MySQL/MariaDB and Redis wire protocols by hand, so their parsing and authentication is ours: a server response that makes the driver do something other than what it says, SCRAM/TLS negotiation bugs, and anything that turns a query parameter back into SQL. TLS and gzip. The optional OpenSSL server path ( include/webc/tls.h ), and the compression code's bounds checking in both directions. The browser half. The wasm bridge: the command buffer, the js() seam, hydration and the patch stream. A model value that reaches the DOM without passing through the same escaping the server half uses is a bug even when the server-rendered page was safe. The dev server and local tools. webc serve --watch (its reload port), the language server, and the MCP server all bind locally and trust local input far more than a production server should. They still should not execute what a request asks them to. webc serve binds whatever WEBC_ADDR says \u2014 including :8080 , which is every interface \u2014 so the framework's job is to be safe there, not to expect a firewall. Out of scope Deploying in dev mode, or exposing the reload port / LSP / MCP to a network. They are development tools; running them in production is the misconfiguration, not the bug. An application's own authorisation logic, or a handler that reads a str from a request and trusts it. WebC hands you typed values and escaping that is on by default; it does not know who should see what. Resource exhaustion that the documented limits already bound ( max_body , max_parts , connection limits). A bypass of one of those limits is in scope. Anything that requires an attacker to already have the process, the database, or the filesystem. Reports produced by a scanner with no working exploit and no affected path. Say what an attacker gains; \"the header is missing\" is a bug only when it is one. How this project already tries Not a guarantee \u2014 a list of what to read first when you are looking for somewhere to dig: Version and build: webc version , webc doctor (what the binary was built from, and where it looks for the framework tree). The gate: CI runs the suite on macOS and Linux with AddressSanitizer, plus libFuzzer targets over the parsers. make fuzz is the local entry point. The decision record: docs/decisions.md \u2014 including the two that are security-adjacent (the installer refuses an unvouched tarball; LeakSanitizer is off by default on Linux, so make test-leaks is the debt gate rather than a silent one). Dependencies: SQLite, zlib and optionally OpenSSL, linked but never vendored \u2014 no third-party code is copied into this tree ( NOTICE ). The rest, including the TLS-adjacent protocol work, is in src/ and is fair game. Credit Advisories name the reporter unless they ask otherwise. There is no bounty; there is a fix, a release note, and the record in CHANGELOG.md . Thank you for taking the time \u2014 a report that never needed to be a report is still worth sending."},{"t":"Decisions","p":"project/decisions.html","g":"Project","h":["D1 \u2014 Licence: Apache-2.0","D2 \u2014 The name stays WebC, and there is no npm package","D3 \u2014 Linux is a supported platform, and the epoll backend is the default there","D4 \u2014 The compiler floor is clang 21, because the suite uses #embed","D5 \u2014 LeakSanitizer is off by default on Linux, and the leaks became a debt gate","D6 \u2014 One number, in VERSION, and webc new writes a different one","D7 \u2014 The installer refuses a tarball nobody vouched for","D8 \u2014 Pages publishes website/ from a workflow, because branch publishing cannot"],"b":"One entry per decision that changed the shape of the project, in the order they were taken. The site's decisions page is generated from this file, one card per ## D<n> heading. D1 \u2014 Licence: Apache-2.0 Taken before the repository was published, and before the first outside patch arrived. Apache-2.0 over MIT for the explicit patent grant and the NOTICE obligation: a framework that ships an HTTP server, an auth layer, a template engine and a database driver is the kind of thing a company's legal review reads before adopting, and the patent clause is what makes that review short. The cost is a slightly longer licence; the benefit is that the answer to \"can we ship this?\" is one word. The licence text in LICENSE is the canonical Apache License 2.0 from https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0.txt , with only the copyright line in the appendix filled in. NOTICE carries the attribution and the list of libraries that are linked but never vendored. Every C source file carries // SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0 on line 1, so the licence survives a file being copied out of the tree; sh scripts/spdx.sh --check fails when a file is missing it. D2 \u2014 The name stays WebC, and there is no npm package \"WebC\" is also the name of 11ty's deprecated single-file web-component format and of Motion Canvas's compiler. The name is kept anyway: this is a C framework, not a web-component format, and one sentence of README answers the collision better than a rename would. npm and Node packaging are explicitly out of scope . WebC ships one static binary and a framework tree; there is no package to install into node_modules and no intention to make one. node appears in the requirements only as an optional local dependency of the JS-bridge and check test suites. D3 \u2014 Linux is a supported platform, and the epoll backend is the default there The scheduler had two backends from the beginning \u2014 kqueue and epoll \u2014 but only kqueue was ever built, so the epoll one was dead code that had never met a compiler. Shipping a framework whose README says \"Linux is experimental\" is a promise to fix it later at a worse price, and the price turned out to be small: the split was three #include s, the WC_UCTX_* register fields for aarch64 and x86_64 , and the link flags. WC_EPOLL_NETPOLL is now defined automatically on __linux__ ( WC_NO_EPOLL_NETPOLL opts out), so an app built with the documented house-rule command \u2014 cc -std=c23 -I include app.c build/libwebc.a \u2014 links on Linux without knowing that the flag exists. -lsqlite3 , -lz and -lpthread are link flags on every platform, and TLS is found through pkg-config instead of the Homebrew prefix. The suite runs green on ubuntu:24.04 (aarch64 and x86_64, clang 21) and the CI matrix leg is required, not continue-on-error : a red row that nobody reads is worse than no row. What Linux does not get yet is proof that the artefact works: release.yml has built linux x86_64 and aarch64 rows since before the port, but nothing ever unpacked the tarball and ran it. That smoke test is the next piece of release work; until it exists, a Linux user's supported path is the checkout. D4 \u2014 The compiler floor is clang 21, because the suite uses #embed #embed is C23 and it is the only sane way to put the JS bridge and the wasi module into the CLI binary without a code generator step. Clang has had it since 19, GCC 15 shipped it, but the test suite is the binding constraint: tests/dx3_test.c embeds a file, so a compiler without #embed cannot run make test at all. The floor is therefore stated as clang 21 , which is what the tree is developed and measured on and what CI pins. Saying \"18\" and then failing on the first test would be worse than asking for a newer compiler. D5 \u2014 LeakSanitizer is off by default on Linux, and the leaks became a debt gate The suite builds with -fsanitize=address,undefined . On Linux that includes LeakSanitizer, which macOS's ASan does not have, so two lesson suites that had always passed on Darwin reported leaks the moment they ran on glibc \u2014 25 KB in 9 allocations, all of them in db_exec and the migration path. They are real leaks, they are pre-existing, and they are not a port regression. Turning them into a red gate on day one would have blocked the entire Linux leg on work that has nothing to do with Linux. cmd_test() therefore sets ASAN_OPTIONS=detect_leaks=0 on Linux unless the caller set it, and make test-leaks runs the same suite with WEBC_TEST_LEAKS=1 to reproduce the report on demand. The debt is written down here rather than hidden: the goal is make test-leaks green and make test without the override. D6 \u2014 One number, in VERSION , and webc new writes a different one The tree said 0.1.0 in eleven places: the CLI's --version , the LSP and MCP serverInfo handshakes, the site's header and footer, the editor extension's package.json , the scaffold templates, and a Homebrew formula. Any release would have updated eight of them. The number now lives in VERSION at the repository root and reaches the CLI as -DWEBC_VERSION (with a 0.0.0-dev fallback in <webc/core.h> so a hand-written cc line still compiles), the site through website/build.py , and the extension through tools/check-version.py , which is a gate rather than a convention: it fails when the file, the built binary, the committed pages, editors/vscode/package.json or an exact tag disagree, and it also refuses a literal equal to the current version inside any C source, because that is a number that looks right today and wrong on release day. APP_VERSION_DEFAULT stays a literal and stays 0.1.0 : it is the version webc new writes into a fresh app's webc.json , which is a different question from the framework's own number. Collapsing the two would have every generated project claim to be the toolchain. Formula/webc.rb is gone. It pointed at webc-darwin-arm64.tar.gz (the artefacts are darwin-aarch64 ), at a FILL_ME_IN tag and at sixty-four zeroes, and a half-filled formula in a public repository is worse than no formula: brew users get the same curl | sh line. A tap is worth doing on the day there is a tap, generated from the published digest. D7 \u2014 The installer refuses a tarball nobody vouched for install.sh fetched a tarball and unpacked it into ~/.local without ever looking at the .sha256 the release publishes beside it. The digest names the platform file ( webc-darwin-aarch64.tar.gz ) while the download lands as webc.tar.gz , so shasum -c cannot be used directly \u2014 the digests are compared instead ( sha256sum where it exists, shasum -a 256 on the macOS boxes that only have that), and a missing .sha256 is a refusal rather than a warning: set -eu turns a digest it failed to compute into an abort, not an empty string that matches. That check is now tested, because a comparison nobody has seen fail is a comparison that does not work: scripts/test-install.sh builds a fake release in a temp directory, serves it over file:// (through WEBC_BASE_URL , which also makes mirrors and air-gapped installs possible) and drives three cases \u2014 matching digest installs and the installed CLI reports the version, one changed byte is refused with exit 1 and nothing unpacked, and an unpublished digest is refused too. make install-test , in CI. D8 \u2014 Pages publishes website/ from a workflow, because branch publishing cannot The plan was \"Settings \u2192 Pages \u2192 main / website \". GitHub does not offer that: branch publishing accepts exactly two paths, / and /docs , and the API answers 422 /source/path: \"/website\" is not a possible value for anything else. The two ways out were to rename the generated site to docs/ \u2014 where the Markdown it is built from already lives, so the two would have to be separated first \u2014 or to deploy with GitHub Actions, where the directory is ours to choose, and fail before anyone gets a 404 that looks like a broken repository. .github/workflows/pages.yml uploads the committed website/ and deploys it. It builds nothing: the site is generated and committed, and the docs job in ci.yml fails when website/ and its sources disagree \u2014 so a published page cannot differ from the reviewed one, because the bytes served are the bytes in the commit. The workflow checks that what it is about to publish is a site ( index.html , 404.html , .nojekyll , and the .release marker that makes the footer's version line reproducible) and prints the version it documents before deploying. The cost is one more workflow and the fact that the deployed site is a Pages artifact rather than a branch checkout. The benefit is that website/ keeps its name and CI keeps its hold on it."},{"t":"Roadmap","p":"project/roadmap.html","g":"Project","h":["Where this is going","What a version number means here","Platforms","What is next, in the order it will probably happen","What it will never do","How to argue with this page"],"b":"Where this is going WebC is pre-1.0 and one person's framework. That is not a disclaimer \u2014 it is the reason the roadmap below is short and the reason every item on it is something an app in this repository already needed. There is no list of features \"planned\" in the abstract: a feature exists here after it has been used, not before. What a version number means here 0.x \u2014 the surface may change in a minor version. api.txt is the diff to read: it ratchets every public name, and webc api tells you what a build has that the file does not. 1.0 \u2014 not a date. It is the point where the reference stops needing a \"may change\" sentence in every section, which is a decision about the language , not about a release calendar. Patches ( 0.1.x ) fix bugs and never add names. Nothing here is a promise that a name added today will outlive 1.0. The api budget exists so that adding one is a decision made in a pull request, not a side effect of a patch. Platforms Server: macOS (arm64 and x86_64) and Linux (arm64 and x86_64) are supported and tested on every push. The epoll backend is the default on Linux, kqueue on macOS. Browser: the client half compiles to WebAssembly for the four major engines. wasm32-wasi through wasi-sdk is the only toolchain it is built with today. Windows: not supported, and not a \"soon\". The server is built on kqueue/epoll and the runtime assumes a POSIX process. If that ever changes it will be a port with a CI job on it, not a claim in a README. The compiler floor is clang 21. The suite uses #embed ; that is a hard requirement, not a preference, and it is written down in the decisions . What is next, in the order it will probably happen 1.0 \u2014 the stability pass. The SPEC \u00a7n citations become stable section numbers, the reference drops its hedges, and api.txt freezes for a major version. Postgres and MySQL are already the operative databases \u2014 the open work is not \"add support\", it is refining what the ORM emits for each dialect (index hints, ON CONFLICT variants, generated columns), driven by the parity suite. More of the client half in C. The JS seam ( js() ) exists for the things the browser owns; every one of those that turns out to be expressible in the framework's own vocabulary moves out of the seam. The editor story. The language server is real today; the next step is the same diagnostics inside webc build output so an editor without LSP still sees them. What it will never do No npm package, no node_modules , no bundler. The browser half is a wasm module built by the same compiler that built the server. D2 says why, and no amount of ecosystem pressure changes the reasoning. No garbage collector. Arenas are the memory model, and every request is a scope. No ORM that hides SQL. db_exec and db_all are there, first-class, next to the typed layer. No configuration file format of its own. webc.json is JSON; there is no webc.yaml coming. How to argue with this page Open an issue. A roadmap that cannot be argued with is a marketing document, and this one is meant to be a plan \u2014 the reasoning is in the decision record , and a case made there has moved this list before."},{"t":"About","p":"project/about.html","g":"Project","h":["Who makes this","Why it exists","The licence","How it is built","Contributing, security, and the record","Colophon"],"b":"Who makes this WebC is written and maintained by Victor-Marian Bu\u0219oi ( @bumasoft ), who reviews every change that lands. It is not a company product: it is a framework built to write real applications, released because the same trade-offs are useful to other people \u2014 C's control over memory and startup time, without giving up the ergonomics that make a web app pleasant to write. Why it exists The pitch on the front page is a set of constraints, and every one of them was chosen against an alternative that is easier: C, because the runtime is the point. A binary that starts in milliseconds, owns its memory, and has no interpreter between the route and the response. Every framework in the adjacent space borrowed a VM or a garbage collector to get ergonomics; WebC borrows the compiler instead. Arenas, because a request is a scope. One arena per request means deallocation is a truncation, not a traversal. The bookkeeping that would be a collector's job is a defer in the handler. One language for both halves , because the alternative is two models, two validation rules and a serialisation boundary that drifts. WEBC_MODEL is declared once and every layer is generated from it \u2014 the form, the JSON, the table, the migration, the browser's copy. A gate for every surface , because a claim that is not counted is a claim that rots. That is why there is an api.txt ratchet, a wasm-size budget, a link checker over these pages, a snippet compiler over every sample, and a CI job that builds this site \u2014 including the number in the hero, which is read from the same file the gate writes. The licence Apache-2.0 , with an explicit patent grant, and a NOTICE that lists what WebC links against without vendoring it (SQLite, zlib, optionally OpenSSL) and states that no third-party source is copied into this tree. Every source file carries an SPDX header, and the build fails when one does not. Copyright \u00a9 2026 Victor-Marian Bu\u0219oi. If you are evaluating WebC for an employer: the licence is the same one most corporate policies already have on their allow-list, and the decision to use it over MIT \u2014 and over a custom licence \u2014 is written down with its reasoning . How it is built Plain make , one C23 compiler, and no build system to learn: bash The site you are reading is generated from the same document the repository ships \u2014 the reference is one file, split into pages by its own section headings \u2014 and it is committed, so it opens from a checkout with no server and no network. There is no CDN, no web font, and no JavaScript framework behind it: one CSS file, three small scripts, and a highlighter written for this site. Contributing, security, and the record Contributing \u2014 the gates, the house rules, the sign-off. Security \u2014 what is in scope, and the private channel. Decisions \u2014 the record: licence, name, Linux, compiler floor, version, installer. Changelog \u2014 what is in a release. Roadmap \u2014 where this is going, and what it will never do. Colophon Set in the reader's own system UI font, because a documentation site should load instantly on a train. Dark by default, light on request, and the theme is applied before the first paint so a page never flashes white. No tracking, no analytics, no cookies \u2014 the only thing stored in your browser is which theme you chose. Built with its own tooling : website/build.py generates every page, website/src/mdhtml.py renders the Markdown, and make links , make snippets and make site are the gates that keep the words attached to the code they describe."},{"t":"All examples","p":"examples/index.html","g":"Examples","h":["Run them"],"b":"Eight apps live in the repository. Each builds, each passes its own tests, and each one is small enough to read in a sitting. They are not toys: the board is the reference for live rooms and drag-and-drop, the accounts app is the reference for sessions. examples/hello Hello A finished first page: a greeting form, a JSON endpoint, and one shared model. Read the page \u2192 examples/todo Todo A list that works with no JavaScript at all, then enhances itself. Read the page \u2192 examples/accounts Accounts Password auth, sessions, a middleware that knows who you are. Read the page \u2192 examples/web Web A client-only page: the same views compiled to wasm. Read the page \u2192 examples/js JS interop The seam to JavaScript: js() , promises, and calls back the other way. Read the page \u2192 examples/stdlib Stdlib The opt-in shelf: every module on one page. Read the page \u2192 examples/board Board A kanban with live rooms, drag and drop, and a real database. Read the page \u2192 examples/minimal Minimal The bundle floor: what a page costs when it asks for nothing. Read the page \u2192 Run them Every example is a project of its own. From the repository root: bash"},{"t":"Hello","p":"examples/hello.html","g":"Examples","h":["webc.json","src/app.h","src/client.c","src/server.c","src/view.c","public/style.css"],"b":"A finished first page: a greeting form, a JSON endpoint, and one shared model. webc dev run it from examples/hello open on GitHub The real app, from examples/hello \u2014 screenshot taken from webc dev . webc.json examples/hello/webc.json src/app.h examples/hello/src/app.h src/client.c examples/hello/src/client.c src/server.c examples/hello/src/server.c src/view.c examples/hello/src/view.c public/style.css examples/hello/public/style.css"},{"t":"Todo","p":"examples/todo.html","g":"Examples","h":["webc.json","src/server.c","src/todo.h","src/view.c","migrations/0001_todos.sql","public/index.html"],"b":"A list that works with no JavaScript at all, then enhances itself. webc dev run it from examples/todo open on GitHub The real app, from examples/todo \u2014 screenshot taken from webc dev . webc.json examples/todo/webc.json src/server.c examples/todo/src/server.c src/todo.h examples/todo/src/todo.h src/view.c examples/todo/src/view.c migrations/0001_todos.sql examples/todo/migrations/0001_todos.sql public/index.html examples/todo/public/index.html \u2026and 2 more files in examples/todo ."},{"t":"Accounts","p":"examples/accounts.html","g":"Examples","h":["webc.json","src/accounts.h","src/server.c","src/view.c","migrations/0001_accounts.sql","public/index.html"],"b":"Password auth, sessions, a middleware that knows who you are. webc dev run it from examples/accounts open on GitHub The real app, from examples/accounts \u2014 screenshot taken from webc dev . webc.json examples/accounts/webc.json src/accounts.h examples/accounts/src/accounts.h src/server.c examples/accounts/src/server.c src/view.c examples/accounts/src/view.c migrations/0001_accounts.sql examples/accounts/migrations/0001_accounts.sql public/index.html examples/accounts/public/index.html \u2026and 2 more files in examples/accounts ."},{"t":"Web","p":"examples/web.html","g":"Examples","h":["webc.json","src/app.c","src/notes.h","public/index.html","public/style.css"],"b":"A client-only page: the same views compiled to wasm. webc dev run it from examples/web open on GitHub The real app, from examples/web \u2014 screenshot taken from webc dev . webc.json examples/web/webc.json src/app.c examples/web/src/app.c src/notes.h examples/web/src/notes.h public/index.html Not the app's code: this is the document webc new writes for a browser-only project. You edit the title and the stylesheet link; the mount point and WebC.start() are the scaffold's and stay as they are. examples/web/public/index.html public/style.css examples/web/public/style.css"},{"t":"JS interop","p":"examples/js.html","g":"Examples","h":["webc.json","src/app.c","public/data.json","public/index.html","public/style.css"],"b":"The seam to JavaScript: js() , promises, and calls back the other way. webc dev run it from examples/js open on GitHub The real app, from examples/js \u2014 screenshot taken from webc dev . webc.json examples/js/webc.json src/app.c examples/js/src/app.c public/data.json examples/js/public/data.json public/index.html examples/js/public/index.html public/style.css examples/js/public/style.css"},{"t":"Stdlib","p":"examples/stdlib.html","g":"Examples","h":["webc.json","src/app.c","public/index.html","public/style.css"],"b":"The opt-in shelf: every module on one page. webc dev run it from examples/stdlib open on GitHub The real app, from examples/stdlib \u2014 screenshot taken from webc dev . webc.json examples/stdlib/webc.json src/app.c examples/stdlib/src/app.c public/index.html examples/stdlib/public/index.html public/style.css examples/stdlib/public/style.css"},{"t":"Board","p":"examples/board.html","g":"Examples","h":["webc.json","src/app.h","src/client.c","src/server.c","src/view.c","migrations/0001_init.sql"],"b":"A kanban with live rooms, drag and drop, and a real database. webc dev run it from examples/board open on GitHub The real app, from examples/board \u2014 screenshot taken from webc dev . webc.json examples/board/webc.json src/app.h examples/board/src/app.h src/client.c examples/board/src/client.c src/server.c examples/board/src/server.c src/view.c examples/board/src/view.c migrations/0001_init.sql examples/board/migrations/0001_init.sql \u2026and 3 more files in examples/board ."},{"t":"Minimal","p":"examples/minimal.html","g":"Examples","h":["main.c","webc.json","public/index.html","public/style.css"],"b":"The bundle floor: what a page costs when it asks for nothing. webc dev run it from examples/minimal open on GitHub main.c examples/minimal/main.c webc.json examples/minimal/webc.json public/index.html The whole document: a viewport, a stylesheet, a mount point and WebC.start() . Nothing here is app logic \u2014 the page is wasm, and this is the page it lands in. examples/minimal/public/index.html public/style.css The look, in one file and one class per element. Shown here up to the card, the brand mark and the heading; the rest is the big number, the button, the dark half and the phone case. examples/minimal/public/style.css"}];
